{section 1 bid=scope: Scope}

{C:eictar} puts files into one archive file. Unlike {C:tar}, it compresses each
member separately. When encryption is on, it also encrypts each member
separately. An index at the end of the archive records every member. As a
result, a listing or a selective extraction does not read the member data.

{section 2 bid=decisions: Decisions}

{stable col=3 header sb b== i=3% w=94%:
| # | Question | Decision |
| 1 | Container layout | The body is append-only. The index is at the end, followed by a fixed-size trailer. |
| 2 | Encryption | XChaCha20-Poly1305 AEAD, Argon2id key derivation, and one subkey for each member. |
| 3 | Index encryption | Optional, with {C:--encrypt-index}. {b:Off by default.} |
| 4 | {C:ccrypt} fallback | Not necessary. Go's {C:x/crypto} supplies a modern AEAD and KDF. The requirements no longer include {C:ccrypt}. |
| 5 | Dependencies | Third-party modules in pure Go are permitted. No CGO and no external binaries. |
| 6 | v1 scope | Create, list, extract, append, update, delete, compact, verify, info and repair. Full metadata. A digest for each member. |
| 7 | Paths in an archive | At most one live member for each path. {C:-r} on a path that is already in the archive replaces it ({ref t=#one-live-member-each:§9.2}). |
| 8 | macOS ACLs | Not recorded. They need CGO, and the no-CGO rule stays ({ref t=#other-platforms:§15.1}). Decided for M8, from open question 4. |
| 9 | Xattrs across platforms | Names are recorded exactly. Each name is applied where the destination takes it, and one notice lists the rest ({ref t=#metadata-extraction:§7.7}). Decided for M8, from open question 5. |
| 10 | Module path | {C bc=/._:github.com/philmalin/eictar} ({ref t=#package-layout:§12}). Decided for the first release, from open question 3. |
| 11 | License | GPL-3.0 ({C:LICENSE}). Under section 7(e), the license gives no right to use the name "eictar" for a modified version ({C:TRADEMARKS.md}). |
| 12 | Archive name | The conventional extension is {C:.ect}. Create adds it to a name with no extension ({ref t=#archive-name:§10.12}). |
| 13 | Selection by regular expression | {C:-R} and {C:--exclude-regex}. An expression matches the whole stored path ({ref t=#patterns:§10.11}). |
| 14 | Dictionaries | {C:-Z zstd:train} trains a zstd dictionary from the files, and the archive stores it ({ref t=#dictionaries-m10:§4.2}, M10). |
| 15 | Identical content | Stored one time. A copy refers to the blob of the member that holds it ({ref t=#identical-content-m11:§4.3}, M11). |
}

{section 2 bid=non-goals-v1: Non-goals for v1}

{list t=*:
  {item:
    {b:Delta storage between versions.} When a file changes, the program stores
    it again as a whole new member. It marks the old member as dead (a
    tombstone). There is no chain of patches.

    A chain makes extraction slower as the history grows, and it breaks when a
    member in the middle is deleted. This decision has a cost. A file that you
    append N times occupies N full copies, until {C:--compact} removes the dead
    copies. {ref t=#delta-storage-between-versions:§4.5} records the analysis, and why delta storage is not adopted.
  }
  {item:
    {b:Incremental archives, as in {C:tar --listed-incremental}.} That feature
    keeps a snapshot of a tree so that the next run stores only the changes.
    It is a feature above the format. It can come later with no change to the
    bytes on disk.
  }
  {item:
    {b:Chunk-level deduplication.} A shared store of chunks, as borg and restic
    use. It changes the storage model: members become lists of shared chunks,
    not one continuous blob. That needs a chunk index, reference counts and a
    garbage collector. For these reasons it is not in v1. Whole-file
    deduplication is in v1 ({ref t=#identical-content-m11:§4.3}). {ref t=#chunk-level-deduplication-not:§4.4} records the full analysis and why
    the design leaves chunk deduplication out.
  }
  {item:
    {b:Public-key recipients} (as in {C:age}). The format keeps space for them
    ({ref t=#crypto-header-cbor-plaintext:§6.2}).
  }
  {item:
    {b:Pipes.} The program cannot read or write an archive through a pipe. The
    reader starts at the end of the file, so the file must be seekable.
  }
  {item:
    {b:Signatures on the whole archive.} In v1, integrity comes from the digest
    of each member and from the AEAD tags.
  }
}

