{section 2 bid=terms: Terms}

Each term has one meaning in this document.

{stable col=2 header sb b== i=3% w=94%:
| Term | Meaning |
| member | One entry in the archive: a file, a directory or a link. |
| blob | The bytes of one member's content in the body of the archive. |
| owner | The member whose blob holds the content that another member shares ({ref t=#identical-content-m11:§4.3}). |
| dictionary | Text that zstd uses as the start of each chunk, stored in the archive ({ref t=#dictionaries-m10:§4.2}). |
| chunk | A fixed-size piece of a member's content. Each chunk is compressed, and sealed, separately. |
| index | The catalog of all members, stored near the end of the archive. |
| trailer | The fixed 96-byte record at the end of the file. It points to the index. |
| generation | A counter that increases each time the program writes a new index. |
| tombstone | A member marked dead. Listings ignore it. Its blob stays until {C:--compact}. |
| seal | To encrypt and authenticate with the AEAD. "Unseal" is the reverse. |
| digest | A BLAKE3-256 value of some bytes. In an encrypted archive, the digest of content is keyed ({ref t=#crypto-header-cbor-plaintext:§6.2}). |
| reader, writer | The parts of the program that open and create archives. |
}

{section 1 nc bid=part-i: Part I — The format on disk}

Sections 2 to 6 describe the bytes, and give the reasons for them.
{C:doc/format.md} gives the same bytes as rules only, and it is the contract
for every implementation. The sections after section 6 describe what this
implementation does.

{section 1 bid=overview-format: Overview of the format}

{Image s=fig/Archive_Layout.png w=60% center: The layout of an archive file}

The reader opens an archive in this sequence:

{list t=1:
  {item:
    Read the file header at offset 0 and check it.
  }
  {item:
    Read the trailer from the last 96 bytes and check it against the header
    and the file size.
  }
  {item:
    If the archive is encrypted, read the crypto header and derive the keys
    ({ref t=#crypto-header-cbor-plaintext:§6.2}).
  }
  {item:
    Read the index bytes that the trailer points to.
  }
  {item:
    Check the index digest that the trailer records ({ref t=#authenticating-index:§6.4}).
  }
  {item:
    If the index is sealed, unseal it. Then decompress it and decode it.
  }
  {item:
    Check the byte range of every member and dictionary against the archive.
  }
}

Step 7 exists because {C:Index.Validate} has no file to compare with. A blob
must start at or after the body and end at or before the index. A
member that points into the header, into the index, or past the end of the
file is a corrupt index. Without this step, a bad range can return the wrong
bytes as file content.

Member blobs are {b:continuous} and {b:unordered}. The format does not tie
the order of blobs to the order of the paths on the command line. Thus the
writer can accept the member that a worker finishes first ({ref t=#concurrency-model:§8}).

All integers on disk are little-endian, except where this document says
otherwise. The chunk counters in AEAD nonces are big-endian, as is usual for
AEAD stream constructions.

{section 1 bid=fixed-structures: Fixed structures}

{section 2 bid=file-header-64-bytes: File header (64 bytes, at offset 0, never encrypted)}

{stable col=4 header sb b==:
| Off | Size | Field | Notes |
| 0 | 8 | {C:magic} | {C:45 49 43 54 41 52 1A 0A} = {C:"EICTAR\\x1a\\n"} |
| 8 | 2 | {C:format_major} | 1 |
| 10 | 2 | {C:format_minor} | 0 |
| 12 | 4 | {C:header_flags} | bit 0: the archive is encrypted ({C:format.HeaderFlags}) |
| 16 | 8 | {C:created_unix_nanos} | the creation time of the archive |
| 24 | 16 | {C:archive_uuid} | random. The key schedule and the index digest use it. |
| 40 | 4 | {C:crypto_header_len} | 0 when not encrypted. Maximum 64 KiB ({ref t=#crypto-header-cbor-plaintext:§6.2}). |
| 44 | 16 | {C:reserved} | must be zero |
| 60 | 4 | {C:crc32c} | of bytes 0..59 |
}

{b:Flags and reserved bytes are for later versions.} A reader refuses a bit of
{C:header_flags} that it does not know, and a {C:reserved} byte that is not zero.
The error is a version that it does not know, with the message that the
archive needs a newer eictar. Thus a later version can mark a change that an
older reader must not ignore, such as a new kind of member, with a flag. A
change that an older reader can ignore raises {C:format_minor}, which a reader
accepts above its own. Readers before v1.0.4 do not make the check: they
accept any flag and any reserved byte, and an archive that needs the check
fails in them later, as a corrupt index.

The magic follows the idea of the PNG signature. The {C:\\x1a} stops the display
of the file by the {C:type} command of DOS and Windows. The {C:\\n} shows a
transfer in text mode, which changes line endings, because the magic is then
wrong.

{section 2 bid=trailer-96-bytes-last: Trailer (96 bytes, the last 96 bytes of the file)}

{stable col=4 header sb b==:
| Off | Size | Field | Notes |
| 0 | 8 | {C:trailer_magic} | {C:"EICTRAIL"} |
| 8 | 2 | {C:format_major} | must be equal to the header |
| 10 | 2 | {C:format_minor} | must be equal to the header |
| 12 | 4 | {C:trailer_flags} | bit 0: the index is sealed. Bit 1: the index is compressed. ({C:format.TrailerFlags}) |
| 16 | 8 | {C:generation} | increases each time an index is written |
| 24 | 8 | {C:index_offset} |  |
| 32 | 8 | {C:index_length} | bytes on disk |
| 40 | 8 | {C:prev_index_offset} | 0 if there is none. For recovery ({ref t=#changes-checks:§9}). |
| 48 | 8 | {C:live_member_count} | tombstones not included |
| 56 | 32 | {C:index_digest} | BLAKE3-256 of {C:archive_uuid}, {C:generation} and the index bytes on disk. Keyed when the archive is encrypted ({ref t=#authenticating-index:§6.4}). |
| 88 | 4 | {C:reserved} | zero |
| 92 | 4 | {C:crc32c} | of bytes 0..91 |
}

The reader checks {C:trailer_flags} and {C:reserved} as it checks the header
({ref t=#file-header-64-bytes:§3.1}). A trailer from a newer version is not damage, so {C:--repair} does not
cut the archive back to a generation that this version can read ({ref t=#repair:§9.5}).

The writer writes the trailer with one {C:write} call. That write is the
commit point for the whole archive ({ref t=#changes-checks:§9}).

The two flag words are different Go types, {C:HeaderFlags} and
{C:TrailerFlags}. Both use bit 0 of a {C:uint32}, and both are about encryption.
With plain integers, {C:trailer.Flags & FlagEncrypted} compiles and gives the
wrong answer without an error. With different types, it does not compile.

{section 1 bid=member-payloads-chunks-compression: Member payloads: chunks and compression}

The writer divides the content of each member into plaintext chunks of
{C:chunk_size} bytes. The default is 4 MiB, and {C:--chunk-size} changes it. The
last chunk can be shorter. The writer compresses each chunk separately. If
the archive is encrypted, it then seals each chunk separately ({ref t=#aead-layer:§6.3}):

{code:
plaintext chunk -> compress -> [seal] -> bytes appended to the member's blob
}

The chunks are independent. No compressor state and no cipher state goes
across a chunk boundary. For one large member, this independence gives four
things:

{list t=*:
  {item:
    parallel compression
  }
  {item:
    parallel decompression
  }
  {item:
    extraction of a byte range
  }
  {item:
    authentication that streams, with no need to hold the whole file in memory
  }
}

A member that is smaller than {C:chunk_size} is one chunk, and it loses
nothing.

{b:The body has no framing.} The index records the length on disk of each
chunk ({ref t=#chunk-table:§5.2}). Thus the body is only data: no headers, no lengths and no
separators between members. There is a cost, which {ref t=#changes-checks:§9} records. If the index
is lost, a scan cannot recover the archive, because there is nothing to find.

{section 2 bid=chunks-that-compression-makes: Chunks that compression makes larger}

Some chunks compress to their own size or more. The writer stores such a
chunk as plaintext. No flag records this, and no flag is necessary. A
compressed chunk is always shorter than its plaintext. Thus this test has
only one meaning, and the reader uses it:

{code:
on-disk length == plaintext length   ->   the chunk is stored
}

This rule costs nothing. It also keeps an archive of data that is already
compressed from growing larger than the data.

{b:When the archive is encrypted, the rule applies to the unsealed bytes.} A
sealed chunk is its payload plus a 16-byte Poly1305 tag. The reader unseals
the chunk first. Then it compares the unsealed length with the plaintext
length. This is the same test as {C:on-disk length == plaintext length + 16},
but it has no tag arithmetic to get wrong.

{section 2 bid=dictionaries-m10: Dictionaries (M10)}

A codec that compresses each file alone cannot use what the files have in
common: license headers, imports, and other text that many files repeat.
{C:tar | zstd} compresses one stream, so it stores such text one time. A
dictionary gives most of that result, and the members stay independent. The
writer trains a dictionary from samples of the files. Then each chunk is
compressed as if the dictionary came before it, and text that the
dictionary holds costs a few bytes.

A measurement on 441 Go source files of 15 KiB on average, at the strongest
zstd level, gave these sizes:

{stable col=2 header sb b== i=3% w=94%:
| Method | Size |
| each file alone | 22.6% |
| each file alone, with a dictionary | 19.4%, and 21.2% with the dictionary itself |
| one stream | 17.3% |
}

The dictionary has a fixed cost. Thus the gain grows with the number of
files. For large files and for data that is already compressed, the gain is
almost zero. On the module cache of this project, 232 MB in 2607 files, the
best dictionary saved 0.4%. A dictionary of 1 MiB made that archive 0.4 MB
larger than one of 112 KiB: the dictionary bytes cost more than they saved.

{b:{C:-Z zstd:train} makes a dictionary} ({ref t=#compression-selection:§10.2}). Only zstd has dictionaries. The
library {C:klauspost/compress/dict} trains it. The library calls its trainer
experimental. A poor dictionary costs ratio, not correctness: each member's
digest is still checked, and the zstd frame names its dictionary.

The size bounds the whole dictionary. The trainer's own size is that of the
content only, and the header and the tables add about 100 bytes. Thus when
the result is too large, the writer trains again with less content. Without
this rule, {C:train=1M} gives a dictionary above the 1 MiB limit. The index
check then refuses the archive at the end of the run.

{b:Training is a pass before the compression.} The writer walks the paths,
with the same filters, and makes a list of the regular files and their
sizes. It takes the first 32 KiB of each file as a sample. When the samples
are more than 100 times the dictionary size, it takes every k-th file, with
the smallest k that fits. The pass walks two times: the first finds the
files and their sizes, and the second reads the samples that the first
chose, while the walk holds their directories ({ref t=#archiving-tree-that-other:§14.5}). For {C:train=auto}, the budget is 100 times 1 MiB,
the largest size. Thus the choice of samples depends only on the tree.

{b:The trainer of klauspost/compress v1.20.3 to v1.20.5 is not deterministic.} The
same samples give a different dictionary on each run. The builder in
{C:dict/builder.go} collects its candidate strings from Go maps, and the order
of a map changes from run to run. Its main sort does not use a strict
order, and two other sorts have no rule for ties. Thus the order of the map
decides the result. On 1500 YAML files, 10 runs gave 10 different
dictionaries. The archive size changed by up to 0.1%, and {C:train=auto}
chose 8 KiB on some runs and 12 KiB on others. The archives with 8 KiB were
about 3% larger. A poor choice
costs ratio, not correctness: the archive stores the dictionary that it
uses.

The branch {C:dict-deterministic} of the fork of the library ({ref t=#outstanding-items:§15.2}) corrects
this. It sorts the candidates by hash before the main sort, and breaks ties
by hash. A test in the library builds each kind of dictionary four times
and compares the bytes. On seven trees, the fixed trainer chose the same
size on each run. Each archive size was in the range of the release
builds. A local build with {C:build-fork.sh} has the fix. A release build
does not have it until a release of the library has it.

The archive is still not the same byte for byte from run to run. With more
than one worker, the members go into the archive in the order that they
finish ({ref t=#concurrency-model:§8}), so their offsets change. With {C:-j 1} and the fixed trainer,
two runs gave the same size.

Then the writer trains the dictionary, and the normal walk compresses the
files with it. A file that changes between the two walks costs ratio only.
If the trainer cannot make a dictionary, for example from too little data,
the writer compresses without one, and prints a notice.

{b:The best size depends on the files.} A larger dictionary holds more of
the text that the files share, but the archive stores each byte of it.
Above the best size, a byte of dictionary saves less than one byte of
payload. A sweep of sizes from 4 KiB to 1 MiB on 29 trees found the best
size of each tree. The table gives how much larger each choice made the
archive than the best size, for the dictionary and the payload together:

{stable col=3 header sb b== i=3% w=94%:
| Choice | Mean | Worst |
| no dictionary | +10.7% | +69% (YAML) |
| 112 KiB for each tree | +7.3% | +35% (Go, 17 files) |
| sample bytes / 64, a rule from the file sizes | +0.55% | +6.4% (JSON) |
| {C:train=auto} | +0.25% | +1.2% (Go, 554 files) |
}

Fourteen trees were Go source, from 50 KB to 232 MB, and the documents of
this project. Fifteen were generated: JSON, YAML, HTML, Markdown, CSV, logs,
random data, and mixtures of small and large files. The results:

{list t=*:
  {item:
    {b:A small tree needs a small dictionary, or none.} 112 KiB is more than
    all the text that 100 small files share. On such a tree, the archive was
    larger than with no dictionary.
  }
  {item:
    {b:Structured files share little text.} JSON and YAML gained 30% to 41%
    from a dictionary of 16 KiB to 32 KiB. A larger one cost more than it
    saved, for 3000 files and for 20000.
  }
  {item:
    {b:A rule from the file sizes cannot see how much text the files share.}
    Sample bytes / 64 was good on source code, but it chose 640 KiB for JSON.
    Thus the writer measures.
  }
  {item:
    {b:A mixture of small and large files needs no special case.} Each file
    gives at most 32 KiB of samples. Thus a few large files do not hide many
    small ones, and the small files are where the dictionary helps. Random
    data gives a gain of zero, and the measurement then chooses a small
    dictionary or none.
  }
}

{b:{C:train=auto} measures the size.} {C:train} alone is {C:train=auto}, the
default form ({ref t=#compression-selection:§10.2}). The writer:

{list t=1:
  {item:
    Holds out every fourth sample. The trainer does not see these. A sample
    that the trainer saw makes a large dictionary look better than it is.
  }
  {item:
    Makes the candidates: 4 KiB, 8 KiB, and each power of 2 up to 1 MiB, but
    not above half of the sample bytes of the tree.
  }
  {item:
    For each candidate, trains a dictionary from 20 times its size of the
    other samples. Then it compresses up to 1 MiB of the held-out samples, at
    the level of the archive, with and without the dictionary.
  }
  {item:
    Calculates the net gain of the candidate: the bytes saved, times the
    sample bytes of the tree divided by the held-out bytes, less the size of
    the dictionary.
  }
  {item:
    Walks the candidates in order of size. A larger candidate wins only when
    its net gain is more than 2% above the best. With the trainer of the
    release, the measurement changes by about 1% from run to run, and a
    smaller dictionary costs less time. The
    walk stops at the second candidate in a row that does not win.
  }
  {item:
    Uses no dictionary when no candidate has a positive net gain, and prints
    a notice.
  }
  {item:
    Trains the final dictionary at the chosen size, from 100 times its size
    of samples, as for {C:train=SIZE}.
  }
}

{b:The candidates train in parallel,} at most {C:-j} at once, with the bound
for memory of the encoder ({ref t=#shared-encoder:§8.2}). A candidate starts only when the
candidate two places before it is done, and has not stopped the walk. The
training time doubles with each size. Thus a candidate after the stop point
costs as much as all the smaller ones together, and the rule lets only one
run. More parallel candidates made the measurement slower, not faster.
Results after the stop point are not used, so the choice does not depend on
{C:-j}.

{b:Other settings were worse.} Candidates trained on 10 times their size
chose worse sizes. Trial compression at level 3 chose worse sizes, +4.6% at
worst. The final dictionary needs 100 times its size: a dictionary from 20
times saved 3% to 10% less, which was 1.8% of a JSON archive.

{b:The measurement costs time.} The table compares {C:train=auto} with a fixed
112 KiB, one run after the other, at the default {C:-j}:

{stable col=4 header sb b==:
| Tree | 112 KiB | {C:train=auto} | Size chosen |
| Go, 120 files, 0.9 MB | 0.9 s | 2.1 s | 16 KiB |
| Go, 2607 files, 232 MB | 8.0 s | 14.3 s | 64 KiB |
| JSON, 3000 files | 8.7 s | 7.6 s | 16 KiB |
| JSON, 20000 files | 21.4 s | 21.5 s | 32 KiB |
| YAML, 1500 files | 2.0 s | 8.8 s | 12 KiB |
| random data | 2.9 s | 0.2 s | none |
| all 29 trees | 106 s | 140 s |  |
}

Most trees took 1 to 2 times as long. The worst was the YAML tree, at 4.4
times: its gain is flat from 16 KiB up, and the walk trains up to 256 KiB
before it stops. A tree with a small best size can be faster than 112 KiB,
because the final training is smaller.

{b:The gain over no dictionary depends on the files.} The table gives the
size that {C:train=auto} saved against no dictionary, for the dictionary and
the payload together:

{stable col=2 header sb b== i=3% w=94%:
| Tree | Saved |
| YAML, 1500 files | 40.8% |
| JSON, 150 to 20000 files of 0.3 KB to 3 KB | 25% to 32% |
| Go, a small package that repeats much code (pflag, 88 files) | 18.6% |
| Markdown, and HTML with a shared header and footer | 13% |
| Go, 120 to 950 files | 5% to 8% |
| mixtures where large files hold most of the bytes | 0.4% to 2.3% |
| large files: CSV, logs, a large Go module | 0.3% to 0.6% |
| Go, 2607 files, 232 MB | 0.4% |
| random data, trees of 19 files or fewer, the documents | 0%: no dictionary |
}

Three things decide the gain:

{list t=*:
  {item:
    {b:The size of the files.} zstd compresses each file alone, and a small
    file starts with no history. The dictionary gives it the shared text at
    the start. In a file of tens of KB, zstd finds most repeats in the file
    itself.
  }
  {item:
    {b:The text that the files share.} The YAML files share almost all their
    structure, and differ only in values. The Go files share license headers,
    imports and some idioms, but their bodies are different.
  }
  {item:
    {b:The part of the bytes in small files.} In a mixture of 2000 small JSON
    files and 40 large logs, the JSON files gained about 30%. But they hold
    4% of the 110 MB, so the archive gained 1.8%.
  }
}

A fixed size of 112 KiB made 9 of the 29 trees larger than no
dictionary, by up to 35%. With {C:train=auto}, no tree was larger than with
no dictionary. The measurement is an estimate, so this is not certain, but
a wrong choice costs little: the worst tree was 1.2% above its best size.

The time is the trade-off. Without a dictionary, the 120 Go files took
0.2 s, the 20000 JSON files 0.7 s, and the 232 MB tree 0.8 s, against the
times of {C:train=auto} in the table above. The trainer uses one
thread for each candidate, and it takes most of the time. Thus {C:train} is
good for many small, similar files. For a tree of large files, it costs
seconds and saves almost nothing.

A dictionary does not give the result of one stream (the first table of
this section). The dictionary holds text from the start of the files. One stream
also finds repeats deep inside the files, and between them.

{b:The archive stores each dictionary as a blob,} before the members of the
generation that uses it. The index lists the dictionaries, and each catalog
entry names the dictionary of its codec ({ref t=#schema:§5.1}). A reader needs only the
dictionary of the members that it reads.

{b:In an encrypted archive, the dictionary is content.} It holds pieces of
the files, so the writer seals it with a key of its own ({ref t=#aead-layer:§6.3}). Its digest
in the index is keyed, like the member digests ({ref t=#crypto-header-cbor-plaintext:§6.2}).

{b:{C:-r} and {C:-u} with {C:train} use the archive's newest dictionary,} when
there is one. A few changed files are a poor sample, and the dictionary from
the whole tree fits them better. When the archive has no dictionary, the
operation trains one from its own files. Without {C:train}, a new member uses
no dictionary. {C:--recompress zstd:train} always trains a new dictionary,
from the members of the archive.

{b:A dictionary stays while a member uses it.} Compact keeps a dictionary
that a kept member uses, and drops the others ({ref t=#compact:§9.3}). {C:--verify} checks each
dictionary that a selected member uses. {C:--info} shows each dictionary, its
size, and the number of live members that use it.

{section 2 bid=identical-content-m11: Identical content (M11)}

A tree often holds files with the same content. Examples are copies of a
library in two places, generated files, and files that a later version did
not change. A member whose content is the same as that of a member already
in the archive does not need a blob of its own. It {b:refers} to the blob of
that member, the {b:owner}.

A library tree of 8514 files had 1023 copies, which took 26.8 MB of 245 MB.
With references and a dictionary, its archive is 214 MB.
{C:tar | zstd --long=27} makes 180 MB of the same tree, and {ref t=#delta-storage-between-versions:§4.5} explains
most of the difference.

A reference is not a hardlink. A hardlink is one file with two names, and
extraction makes it again as one file. A reference is two files, each with
its own metadata, and extraction makes two files. The archive shares their
bytes only.

{b:Two members have the same content when their payloads are equal} ({ref t=#chunk-table:§5.2}):
the same size, the same sparse map, and the same digest. The digest is
BLAKE3-256. Thus two different payloads with one digest are not a practical
risk.

{b:The emitter finds each duplicate.} It is the one goroutine that writes
blobs ({ref t=#concurrency-model:§8}). It keeps a map of the owners of the archive, by digest, size and
sparse map. When a member's payload is already in the map, the emitter does
not write its blob, and the member records a reference to the owner. This
rule catches two copies in one run too, but the workers compress the copy
for nothing.

The emitter uses an owner that the run wrote, or an old owner that passed
its check (below). Otherwise it writes the blob, and the new member becomes
the owner for the rest of the run.

{b:A file whose size is the size of an owner is hashed first.} A duplicate
must have the size of its owner. Thus a file with a size that no owner has is
compressed at once, with no extra read. A file with the size of an owner is
read and hashed before it goes to the workers. If its payload is in the map,
it becomes a reference, and nothing is compressed.

BLAKE3 is much faster than any codec, so the extra read costs little. Two
copies that one run adds at the same time fall back to the rule of the
emitter.

{b:The owner is always a member with a blob.} A reference never names
another reference, so there are no chains. The owner can be a tombstone,
as the target of a hardlink can ({ref t=#one-live-member-each:§9.2}). Compact keeps a tombstone that a
live reference names. In an encrypted archive, the chunk AAD binds the id of
the owner ({ref t=#aead-layer:§6.3}). Thus the reader unseals the blob with the owner's key and
the owner's id.

{b:References need no new secret.} Two members with the same content already
have the same keyed digest ({ref t=#crypto-header-cbor-plaintext:§6.2}). Without {C:--encrypt-index}, a person with
the archive can see that equality in the digests too.

{b:The other operations follow the owner.} Extraction and {C:-O} decode the
owner's blob. {C:--verify} decodes each blob one time, and checks that each
reference has the digest of its owner. {C:-u --update-mode=digest} compares
with the owner's content. {C:--recompress} encodes each owner again, and a
reference stays a reference. A change of passphrase copies the blobs, so the
references stay valid ({ref t=#change-passphrase:§9.7}).

{b:One damaged blob now damages each member that refers to it.} This is the
cost, as for hardlinks. The damage is always found: the digest check and the
AEAD tag refuse the blob, and each member that uses it gives exit 3. But no
member that uses the blob can be extracted. {C:--no-dedup} stores each copy in
full, for a user who wants damage to stay inside one file.

{b:A run checks an old owner before it first refers to it.} The match uses
the digest that the index records, not the blob. Thus a blob that became
damaged after it was written can still match a new file. Before a run refers
to an owner of an earlier generation for the first time, it decodes that
owner's blob and checks its digest. If the check fails, the program warns,
stores the new file in full, and does not use that owner again in the run.

A decode is much faster than a compression. An owner that the run wrote
itself needs no check.

{section 2 bid=chunk-level-deduplication-not: Chunk-level deduplication (not adopted)}

This section records a design that was examined and not adopted. It
explains how chunk deduplication works, what it costs, and why eictar does
not use it.

{b:How it works.} A rolling hash (for example FastCDC) moves over the
content of a file. The writer cuts a chunk where the hash matches a pattern.
The cut points depend on the content, not on the offset. Thus an insert near
the start of a file moves one boundary, and the chunks after it do not
change. The fixed chunks of {ref t=#member-payloads-chunks-compression:§4} do not have this property: an insert moves
every chunk after it. A typical chunker has an average chunk of about 1 MiB,
with a minimum and a maximum.

The writer hashes each chunk and keeps a map of the chunks that the archive
holds. A chunk that is in the map is not written again. A member is then a
list of chunk ids, not one blob. borg and restic work in this way.

Some parts of eictar already agree with this model. Chunks are compressed
and sealed separately ({ref t=#member-payloads-chunks-compression:§4}), so parallel work and range reads stay
possible. Each generation writes a full index, so compact can find the
chunks that live members use, and copy only those. No reference counts are
necessary.

{b:What it changes in the format.}

{list t=*:
  {item:
    The index gets a chunk table: offset, length on disk, plaintext length,
    codec and digest of each chunk. A member gets a list of chunk ids in place
    of {C:off}, {C:len} and {C:chunks}.
  }
  {item:
    The AEAD rules of {ref t=#aead-layer:§6.3} bind the member id and the chunk index. A shared
    chunk has no single member. Each chunk needs its own salt or random nonce,
    and an AAD that binds the chunk. The member's ordered list of chunks, in
    the authenticated index, then protects the order and the length.
  }
  {item:
    From v1.0.4, a reader refuses a header flag that it does not know ({ref t=#file-header-64-bytes:§3.1}).
    Thus a chunked archive can set a flag, and such a reader says that the
    archive needs a newer eictar. A reader before v1.0.4 ignores the flag,
    and refuses the index as corrupt ({ref t=#chunk-table:§5.2}), with no message that a newer
    program made it. For a clear message in those readers too, a chunked
    archive needs {C:format_major} 2.
  }
}

An archive with chunk deduplication off can stay format 1.0, byte for byte.
Thus the program can support both: the mode is set at create, append and
update follow the mode of the archive, and {C:--compact --recompress} can
convert. The design is possible. The question is whether it is useful.

{b:Where it helps.} Large files that change in place or grow, and that the
user adds again with {C:-u}: virtual machine disks, database files,
mailboxes, logs, and uncompressed tarballs or images. A small change then
stores a few chunks, not a new copy. This is most of the gain that delta
storage ({ref t=#delta-storage-between-versions:§4.5}) wants, with no chain of patches.

{b:Where it does not help.}

{list t=*:
  {item:
    {b:Source code and text.} Most source files are smaller than one chunk.
    Each file is then one chunk, and that is whole-file deduplication, which
    {ref t=#identical-content-m11:§4.3} already gives. Text files share small pieces, such as license
    headers, imports and boilerplate. These are far below any useful chunk
    size. Compression finds them, and the trained dictionary ({ref t=#dictionaries-m10:§4.2}) is the
    tool for them. Smaller chunks (8 to 16 KiB) make the index about 100 times
    larger, and each chunk compresses less well.
  }
  {item:
    {b:Compressed formats.} PDF streams, JPEG, video, zip, and office files
    such as docx and xlsx are compressed inside. When an application saves
    such a file again, the bytes change through the whole file, so few chunks
    repeat. An incremental save of a PDF is the exception: it appends to the
    end. An identical copy is already a reference ({ref t=#identical-content-m11:§4.3}).
  }
}

{b:What it costs.}

{list t=*:
  {item:
    {b:Index size.} Each chunk needs about 45 bytes in the index: a 32-byte
    digest, an offset, a length and a codec. Digests do not compress. One TB of
    content in chunks of 1 MiB gives about one million chunks and about 45 MB
    of index. Each append writes a full index ({ref t=#write-sequence:§9.1}), so each {C:-u} on a large
    archive adds that much.
  }
  {item:
    {b:Writer memory.} The writer keeps the map of all chunks. At 1 TB, that
    is 50 to 100 MB, and it must fit in the memory budget of {ref t=#memory-budget:§8.1}.
  }
  {item:
    {b:Information to an observer.} {ref t=#what-observer-sees:§14.1} says that a person who can put files
    into the tree, and see the size of the archive, can test a guess of a
    whole file. With chunks, the person can test a guess of part of a file.
    Chunk lengths are also a fingerprint of the content. borg and restic give
    the chunker a secret seed for this reason. An encrypted archive needs the
    same.
  }
  {item:
    {b:Damage.} One damaged chunk damages each member and each version that
    uses it. The damage is found, but more files are lost than with {ref t=#identical-content-m11:§4.3}.
  }
  {item:
    {b:Fragmentation.} The chunks of one member are spread over the body.
    Extraction seeks more, which is slow on a disk with heads.
  }
  {item:
    {b:Code.} The reader, verify, compact, {C:--recompress}, repair, change of
    passphrase and the stress tester all change. The work is of the same order
    as encryption (M4).
  }
}

{b:Decision.} eictar does not add chunk deduplication. The typical use of
eictar is to archive a tree of source, documents and media. For that use,
whole-file references ({ref t=#identical-content-m11:§4.3}) and the dictionary ({ref t=#dictionaries-m10:§4.2}) already give most of
the gain. The costs above apply to every archive. The gain applies only to
large files that change and that are archived again and again, which is the
work of a backup repository.

{b:What can change the decision.} A measurement on real data. A tool can run
a content-defined chunker over two snapshots of a tree and report, without a
change to the format:

{list t=*:
  {item:
    the bytes that whole-file deduplication saves
  }
  {item:
    the extra bytes that chunks of 64 KiB, 256 KiB and 1 MiB save
  }
  {item:
    the index size that each chunk size costs
  }
}

If the extra saving is large, compared with the index cost, the design can
come back as format 2.

{section 2 bid=delta-storage-between-versions: Delta storage between versions (not adopted)}

This section records a design that was examined and not adopted, as {ref t=#chunk-level-deduplication-not:§4.4}
does.

{b:What it is.} A delta member is compressed against the content of another
member, its base. zstd does this with the base as a raw dictionary
({C:zstd --patch-from}). The content that the two versions share costs almost
nothing. A member records the id of its base, and extraction decodes the
base first.

{b:The measurement.} On the library tree of {ref t=#identical-content-m11:§4.3}, 553 names of large files
have more than one version, and the versions take 109.8 MB.
{C:tar | zstd --long=27} compresses each later version against an earlier one
in its stream, and it is about 30 MB smaller than eictar for that reason.
Per-file compression cannot see across files, whatever the chunk size or the
dictionary.

{b:How to find a base.} There are three signals:

{list t=1:
  {item:
    {b:The same path in an earlier generation.} When append or {C:-u} replaces
    a member, the old member is a tombstone ({ref t=#one-live-member-each:§9.2}). Its content is the last
    version of the file. The index names it, so the signal is exact and free.
  }
  {item:
    {b:Paths that are equal except for a version.} The library tree holds
    {C bc=/._:golang.org/x/sys@v0.47.0/unix/zerrors_linux.go} and the same path with
    {C:v0.48.0}. The writer replaces each token that looks like a version with a
    placeholder, and groups the paths that are then equal. This works inside
    one create, with no extra read.
  }
  {item:
    {b:Similar content.} A sketch of each large file (for example MinHash of
    its content-defined chunks) estimates how much two files share. This finds
    a renamed version, but each file must be read and the sketches indexed.
  }
}

A signal only gives a candidate. The writer compresses the first chunk with
and without the candidate, and keeps the delta only when it saves enough.

{b:What it costs.}

{list t=*:
  {item:
    A member is no longer independent: extraction needs its base first.
    Without a limit, it needs a chain of versions. Each chunk must stay
    independent too, so chunk {i:i} uses the region of the base near the same
    offset, and a large insert near the start loses the gain.
  }
  {item:
    Delete and compact must keep each base that a live delta uses. Damage to a
    base reaches each delta that uses it.
  }
  {item:
    {C:base} is a field that an old reader must not ignore. An archive with
    deltas sets a header flag, or uses {C:format_major} 2, as {ref t=#chunk-level-deduplication-not:§4.4} describes.
  }
  {item:
    A person who can put files into the tree, and see the size of the archive,
    can test a guess against a similar file, not only an identical one ({ref t=#what-observer-sees:§14.1}).
  }
}

{b:Why there is no gain for updates.} eictar cannot list or extract a
tombstone. Thus an old version is only dead space until {C:--compact}. A delta
against it (signal 1) makes the archive grow less between two compacts, and
that is all. At the compact, the program must keep the tombstone because the
delta needs it, which keeps the space that compact must remove, or encode the
delta again in full, which loses the gain. For a user who compacts, the
deltas are useless, and they make compact more complex.

The gain lasts only when both versions are live: trees that hold versions
side by side, such as module caches, vendored libraries, and release or
dated directories (signal 2). Compact keeps both, and nothing else can
remove the copy.

{b:Decision.} eictar does not add delta storage. The typical use does not
hold versions side by side, and for updates, {C:--compact} removes the old
versions with no change to the format.

{b:What can change the decision.}

{list t=*:
  {item:
    A use with many versions side by side. A measurement can pair the files of
    a tree by signal 2, and report the bytes with and without
    {C:zstd --patch-from}, before any change to the format.
  }
  {item:
    A history feature: list and extract a file as it was in an earlier
    generation. Then the old versions have a use, and a delta against them
    (signal 1) saves space that compact would otherwise have to keep.
  }
}

{section 1 bid=index: The index}

{section 2 bid=schema: Schema}

The index uses CBOR ({C bc=/._:github.com/fxamacker/cbor/v2}). CBOR stores the binary
values of extended attributes without base64 growth. New map keys can extend
the schema, and decoding is fast.

After the writer encodes the index, it compresses the index with zstd. The
{C:index_compressed} trailer flag records this, so an uncompressed index is
also readable. If {C:--encrypt-index} is given, the writer then seals the index
as one AEAD message ({ref t=#aead-layer:§6.3}).

{code:
Index := \{
  "v":        1,
  "gen":      <uint64>,
  "codecs":   [ CodecSpec, ... ],     # catalog, referenced by array position
  "dicts":    [ Dict, ... ],          # dictionaries (§4.2); absent when none
  "members":  [ Member, ... ],
\}

CodecSpec := \{ "name": "zstd", "params": \{ "level": 9, "long": 27 \},
               "dict": <uint32> \}     # the id of a Dict; absent when none

Dict := \{
  "id":      <uint32>,       # the zstd dictionary id, the same as in the dictionary
  "gen":     <uint64>,       # generation that added it
  "size":    <uint32>,       # plaintext length, at most 1 MiB
  "digest":  <32 bytes>,     # BLAKE3-256 of the plaintext, keyed as member digests are
  "enc":     \{ "salt": <16 bytes> \},          # absent when not encrypted
  "off":     <uint64>,       # blob offset
  "len":     <uint64>,       # blob length on disk: size, or size + 16 when sealed
\}

Member := \{
  "id":      <uint64>,       # stable, monotonic, never reused
  "gen":     <uint64>,       # generation that added this member
  "path":    "src/main.go",  # slash-separated, relative, cleaned
  "type":    "reg"|"dir"|"symlink"|"hardlink"|"fifo"|"sock"|"chardev"|"blockdev",
  "mode":    <uint32>,       # permission bits + setuid/setgid/sticky, at most 07777
  "uid":     <uint32>, "gid": <uint32>,   # absent with --no-owner
  "uname":   "psm", "gname": "psm",
  "mtime":   <int64 ns>, "atime": <int64 ns>, "ctime": <int64 ns>,
  "size":    <uint64>,       # logical plaintext size
  "link":    "../target",    # symlink target
  "hardlink":<uint64>,       # member id of the first link
  "data":    <uint64>,       # member id of the owner of this content (§4.3)
  "rdev":    [major, minor],
  "xattrs":  \{ "user.foo": <bytes>, ... \},   # POSIX ACLs are stored here
  "sparse":  [ \{ "off": <uint64>, "len": <uint64> \}, ... ],  # data segments; absent = dense; the blob holds only these
  "digest":  <32 bytes>,     # BLAKE3-256 of the payload (§5.2), keyed when encrypted
  "codec":   <int>,          # index into "codecs"; -1 = stored
  "chunk":   4194304,        # plaintext bytes per chunk; absent when empty
  "enc":     \{ "salt": <16 bytes> \},          # absent when not encrypted
  "off":     <uint64>,       # blob offset
  "len":     <uint64>,       # blob length on disk
  "chunks":  [ <uint32>, ... ],  # on-disk length of each chunk
  "dead":    true            # present only on tombstones
\}
}

The catalog stores each codec's parameters as the values in effect, not as
the text that the user typed. Many members can share one catalog entry. A
catalog entry with {C:dict} is a different entry from the same codec without
it, and a reader needs {C:dict}, but not {C:params}, to decode.

A dictionary blob has no chunks. It is one message, and it is not
compressed, because a dictionary is itself dense. The writer takes each id
from a random number, and takes another one if the archive has it already.

{C:uid} and {C:gid} are optional. With {C:--no-owner} they are absent, which is
different from uid 0. A stored zero means root to a reader that restores
ownership. The writer records {C:atime} and {C:mtime}. It does not record {C:ctime},
because no program can restore it.

A member with {C:data} is a regular file with no blob of its own ({ref t=#identical-content-m11:§4.3}). It has
no {C:off}, {C:len}, {C:chunks}, {C:chunk}, {C:enc} or {C:codec}, and its {C:size},
{C:sparse} and {C:digest} are those of its owner. The owner is a regular file
with a blob, and without {C:data}.

For a file with holes, {C:size} is the logical length, and {C:sparse} lists the
data segments. The blob stores only the bytes of those segments, back to
back. The digest covers those stored bytes. The index covers the map, so the
index digest protects it ({ref t=#authenticating-index:§6.4}).

{section 2 bid=chunk-table: The chunk table}

{C:chunk} is a field of the member, not of {C:enc}. A plaintext archive also has
chunks, and its reader needs the same number to find where each chunk ends.
A member with no content has neither {C:chunk} nor {C:chunks}.

The index validator checks that the chunk table agrees with the member:

{list t=*:
  {item:
    The chunk lengths must add up to {C:len}.
  }
  {item:
    The payload must fit in {C:len(chunks)} chunks of {C:chunk} bytes, and must not
    fit in one chunk less. The payload is {C:size} for a dense file, and the total
    of the {C:sparse} segments for a file with holes.
  }
  {item:
    {C:chunk} must not be more than {b:MaxChunkSize (256 MiB)}.
  }
}

Without the second rule, a reader has no expected size for each chunk. A
decompression bomb needs exactly that gap.

The third rule is a security limit. A reader sizes its decode buffer from
{C:chunk}, and {C:chunk} comes from the index, which an attacker can write. In a
test, a 200-byte archive that claimed a 2 GiB chunk size made the reader
allocate 2 GiB. The chunk held five bytes, and extraction does this once for
each worker. The limit is far above the 4 MiB default. The program applies
the same limit to {C:--chunk-size}, so the error comes when an archive is
created, not when someone reads it.

{b:A regular file must have a digest.} The wire schema makes the digest
optional, but the validator refuses a {C:reg} member without one. Only the
digest catches a member whose offset or chunk table points at the wrong
bytes. Without it, a damaged index gives wrong content with no error.

The validator also checks that each field belongs to the member's type:

{list t=*:
  {item:
    Only a symlink has {C:link}, and a symlink must have it.
  }
  {item:
    Only a hardlink has {C:hardlink}, and it must name a {C:reg} member. That rule
    excludes chains, cycles and links to directories.
  }
  {item:
    Only a {C:reg} member has {C:data}. It names an owner with a blob, with the
    same size, sparse map and digest, and without {C:data} itself ({ref t=#identical-content-m11:§4.3}). The
    member has no blob, chunks, codec or key of its own.
  }
  {item:
    A dictionary has a unique id that is not 0, a size of 1 byte to 1 MiB and
    a digest. A catalog entry names only a dictionary that the index holds,
    and only for zstd. An index holds at most 1024 dictionaries.
  }
  {item:
    Only a device has {C:rdev}, and a device must have two values.
  }
  {item:
    Only a {C:reg} member has {C:sparse}. The segments must be in order, must not
    overlap or be empty, and must end inside {C:size}.
  }
  {item:
    {C:mode} must not be more than {C:07777}. The file type is the {C:type} field,
    never mode bits.
  }
  {item:
    An xattr name has 1 to 255 bytes, a value has at most 64 KiB, and a member
    has at most 1024 xattrs. These are the kernel's own limits.
  }
}

A field on the wrong type is not harmless. A reader that acts on a field,
not on the type, can do what the archive did not mean.

The validator does {b:not} require canonical member paths. A hostile archive
must still list, so that a user can see what they received. Extraction is
the step that refuses a bad path ({ref t=#path-handling:§7}).

{section 2 bid=cbor-policy: CBOR policy}

The library defaults are wrong for this format in four ways.
{C bc=/._:src/internal/format/cbor.go} sets the correct behavior:

{list t=*:
  {item:
    {b:The encoder sorts map keys bytewise.} Go map order changes from run to
    run. Without sorting, one index encodes to different bytes each time, but
    the trailer records a digest of those bytes. Thus the format requires
    determinism.
  }
  {item:
    {b:The decoder accepts invalid UTF-8.} A POSIX path is a sequence of bytes,
    not text. The library default refuses a text string that is not valid
    UTF-8. With the default, you can archive a Latin-1 file name but not read it
    back.
  }
  {item:
    {b:The array and map limits are explicit.} The default limit is 131072
    array elements, which limits an archive to 131072 members without a
    message. A larger limit alone lets a nine-byte header demand an unbounded
    {C:make([]Member, n)}. Thus the decoder calculates its limit from the input
    length, because an element cannot exist without bytes. It also applies a
    hard limit, {b:MaxIndexMembers = 4,194,304} members for each archive. A
    larger archive needs the paged index of {ref t=#future-work:§15}, not a larger constant.
  }
  {item:
    {b:The decoder refuses} indefinite-length items, CBOR tags and duplicate map
    keys. The encoder never writes them.
  }
}

{section 2 bid=size-practice: Size in practice}

Before compression, the index uses approximately 200 to 400 bytes for each
member. The chunk table adds 4 bytes for each 4 MiB of content, which is
1 KiB for each GiB. An archive of 1,000,000 files has an index of a few tens
of MiB after compression. That size is acceptable. {ref t=#future-work:§15} describes the next
step if an archive becomes too large for this design.

{section 1 bid=cryptography: Cryptography}

Encryption is optional. When it is off, this section does not apply, and the
archive is exactly what {ref t=#overview-format:§2} to {ref t=#index:§5} describe. When it is on, it adds a crypto
header ({ref t=#crypto-header-cbor-plaintext:§6.2}) and a seal on each chunk ({ref t=#aead-layer:§6.3}). The chunks themselves do not
change.

{section 2 bid=primitives: Primitives}

{stable col=3 header sb b== i=3% w=94%:
| Purpose | Choice | Package |
| Passphrase KDF | Argon2id | {C bc=/._:golang.org/x/crypto/argon2} |
| AEAD | XChaCha20-Poly1305 (24-byte nonce, 16-byte tag) | {C bc=/._:golang.org/x/crypto/chacha20poly1305} |
| Subkey derivation | HKDF-SHA-256 | {C:golang.org/x/crypto/hkdf} |
| Content digest | BLAKE3-256, keyed in an encrypted archive ({ref t=#crypto-header-cbor-plaintext:§6.2}) | {C:lukechampine.com/blake3} |
}

XChaCha20 is better than AES-GCM here for three reasons. It is fast without
AES-NI. Its 24-byte nonce removes the risk of nonce collisions. Its pure-Go
implementation runs in constant time on all targets. Appendix A gives the
full comparison.

{section 2 bid=crypto-header-cbor-plaintext: Crypto header (CBOR, plaintext, present only when encrypted)}

The crypto header comes directly after the file header. It is
{C:crypto_header_len} bytes long. It starts with its own {C:uint32_le} length of
the CBOR that follows, so a reader can decode it without trust in the outer
field. {C:crypto_header_len} must not be more than {b:64 KiB}. A real crypto
header is a few hundred bytes, and a reader allocates the size in the field
before it decodes anything.

{code:
\{
  "v":       1,
  "kdf":     "argon2id",
  "salt":    <16 bytes>,
  "time":    3,             # Argon2id passes
  "memory":  262144,        # KiB (256 MiB default)
  "threads": 4,
  "aead":    "xchacha20poly1305",
  "key":     <72 bytes>,    # the data key, wrapped: see below
  "recipients": [...]       # reserved for public-key mode; absent today
\}
}

A reader that finds {C:recipients} refuses the archive. Such an archive is
addressed to public keys that this build does not know.

{b:The archive has a random data key, and the passphrase only wraps it.}
Create makes a random 256-bit data key. Every subkey comes from the data key.
The passphrase gives a key-encryption key (KEK) through Argon2id, and the
crypto header stores the data key sealed under the KEK:

{code:
dataK   = 32 random bytes, made once, at create
KEK     = Argon2id(passphrase, salt, time, memory, threads, 32)
key     = nonce || seal(KEK, nonce, dataK, aad = "eictar/v1/wrap" || archive_uuid)
          nonce = 24 random bytes

indexK   = HKDF(dataK, info = "eictar/v1/index"      || archive_uuid || generation)
authK    = HKDF(dataK, info = "eictar/v1/index-auth" || archive_uuid)
contentK = HKDF(dataK, info = "eictar/v1/content"    || archive_uuid)
memberK  = HKDF(dataK, info = "eictar/v1/member"     || archive_uuid, salt = member.salt)
dictK    = HKDF(dataK, info = "eictar/v1/dict"       || archive_uuid, salt = dict.salt)
}

A wrong passphrase gives another KEK, and the seal of {C:key} fails its tag. The
reader reports that as a wrong passphrase, before it reads anything else.
Thus the tag also checks the passphrase, and the header needs no other
check value. An
attacker can test a guess offline against the tag, as against any file that a
passphrase protects. The Argon2id parameters exist to make each guess
expensive.

The wrapped key is the reason that a passphrase can change ({ref t=#change-passphrase:§9.7}). A change
seals the same data key under a new KEK. Every subkey stays the same, so no
member is sealed again, and every digest stays valid. A passphrase that
gives the master key directly cannot change without a new seal of each
member.

All HKDF instances use SHA-256. The encoding of {C:generation} is {C:uint64_le}
in every key and digest. {C:member.salt} is 16 random bytes, stored in each
member's {C:enc} field. Thus each member has its own key ({ref t=#aead-layer:§6.3}).

{b:The KDF parameters come from the archive, and the reader treats them as
hostile.} It refuses {C:time} more than {b:64} and {C:memory} more than
{b:4 GiB}. It also refuses a zero {C:time} or {C:threads}, and {C:memory} less than
8 KiB for each thread, which Argon2id cannot use. These are damage, with exit

{list t=1:
  {item:
    Then it compares the memory with this machine ({ref t=#3-argon2id-default:§A.3}). All of this
    occurs {i:before} the prompt for the passphrase. The cost of Argon2id is linear
    in {C:time}, approximately 1.8 s for each pass at the memory limit. Without the
    time limit, a crafted header that asks for 2³²−1 passes stops a listing for
    centuries.
  }
}

{b:In an encrypted archive, the member digest is keyed.} A plain BLAKE3
digest of the content is readable in an index that is not sealed, which is
the default. Anyone with a copy of a file can then find out if the archive
holds it, whatever its name. Worse, anyone can find the content of a small
file with few possible values, such as a PIN or a short answer. They hash
each candidate, and compare. The content is sealed, but its digest gives it away.

Thus in an encrypted archive, the digest of a member is BLAKE3 keyed with
{C:contentK}. {C:--verify}, {C:-u --update-mode=digest}, compact and recompress
work as before, because every member of one archive has the same key. A
digest cannot be compared across two archives, or with a hash made outside
eictar. The digest of a plaintext archive is the plain BLAKE3, as before.

{section 2 bid=aead-layer: The AEAD layer}

The writer seals each chunk after it compresses the chunk ({ref t=#member-payloads-chunks-compression:§4}):

{code:
sealed = seal(memberK, nonce, compressed_chunk, aad)
nonce  = 16 zero bytes || uint64_be(chunk_index)
aad    = uint64_le(member_id) || uint64_be(chunk_index) || final_byte
         final_byte = 0x01 for the last chunk of the member, else 0x00
}

Each member has its own key ({ref t=#crypto-header-cbor-plaintext:§6.2}). Thus a nonce must be unique only inside
one member, and a counter gives that result.

The AAD stops chunks from moving between members or inside one member. A
chunk that is unsealed at the wrong index, or in another member, fails its
tag. {C:final_byte} also catches truncation. If trailing chunks are removed, a
different chunk claims to be the last one, and that claim fails its tag.

The order is compress, then seal, because ciphertext does not compress. {ref t=#security-considerations:§14}
describes the small risk that this order causes.

The writer knows which chunk is last only when the next read reaches the end
of the file. Thus the pipeline holds one chunk back until it knows if another
chunk follows ({ref t=#concurrency-model:§8}).

The index is sealed as one message with {C:indexK}:

{code:
nonce  = 24 random bytes
aad    = uint64_le(generation)
sealed = nonce || seal(indexK, nonce, index, aad)
}

{b:The index nonce is random, and the sealed index stores it.} One
{C:(archive_uuid, generation)} pair can get two different indexes. These are
three examples:

{list t=*:
  {item:
    You copy an archive, and then you append to the copy and to the original.
  }
  {item:
    An append writes its index, and then fails before its trailer. The next
    append uses the same generation.
  }
  {item:
    A compact writes its index, and then its rename fails.
  }
}

{C:indexK} comes from the pair. Thus a nonce that also comes from the pair uses
one key and nonce two times. That exposes the XOR of the two indexes, and the
Poly1305 key that authenticates them. Thus the nonce is random. A random
192-bit nonce cannot collide in practice (Appendix A).

Compact must keep the uuid: the member keys come from it, so a new uuid makes
every sealed blob impossible to open.

A dictionary ({ref t=#dictionaries-m10:§4.2}) is sealed as one message with a key of its own:

{code:
dictK  = HKDF(dataK, info = "eictar/v1/dict" || archive_uuid, salt = dict.salt)
nonce  = 24 zero bytes
aad    = uint32_le(dict.id)
sealed = seal(dictK, nonce, dictionary, aad)
}

The salt is random for each dictionary, so each key seals one message only,
and a fixed nonce is safe. The info string differs from that of a member, so
no dictionary key can be a member key.

{section 2 bid=authenticating-index: Authenticating the index}

{b:When a key exists, the index is always authenticated, sealed or not.}
{C:--encrypt-index} controls {i:confidentiality} only.

The reason is not obvious. The chunk AAD includes {C:member_id}, {C:chunk_index}
and the final flag. It does not include the path, the mode or any other
field of the index.

Suppose that the members are sealed, but the index is plain and its digest
has no key. Then an attacker with no passphrase can rename a member from
{C:notes.txt} to {C:.ssh/authorized_keys}. The attacker can also add
an execute bit, point a path at a different blob, or tombstone members. Every
chunk still passes its tag, because no tag covers these fields.

The fix does not change the trailer layout. When the archive is encrypted,
{C:index_digest} is a {b:keyed} BLAKE3:

{code:
index_digest = BLAKE3-keyed(authK, archive_uuid || uint64_le(generation) || index_bytes)
authK        = HKDF(dataK, info = "eictar/v1/index-auth" || archive_uuid)
}

When the archive is not encrypted, the digest has the same input but no key.
The uuid and the generation stop an index and a trailer from moving to a
different archive. With a key, they also stop that move between two archives
that use the same passphrase.

A reader without the passphrase cannot check a keyed digest. Thus eictar asks
for the passphrase for every operation on an encrypted archive, a listing and
{C:--info} too, whether or not the index is sealed. It never shows metadata that
it cannot authenticate. A reader with the passphrase refuses any changed byte
of metadata.

A plain index is still readable with a CBOR tool, without eictar. Thus only
{C:--encrypt-index} keeps the names and the sizes secret. A user who encrypts
an archive can expect every part of it to need the passphrase. This rule
matches that expectation for eictar itself, and {C:--encrypt-index} matches it
for every other tool.

This protection does {b:not} cover a rewrite of the whole file, a rollback
to an older genuine archive, a downgrade to plaintext, or a deletion. {ref t=#integrity-its-limits:§14.4}
describes them.

{section 1 nc bid=part-ii: Part II — Behavior}

This part describes what the program does with the format. It covers where
the program can write, how it uses the machine, and how an archive changes.

{section 1 bid=path-handling: Path handling}

This section says what a member path can contain and where extraction can
write. It also says why the program checks paths two times. {ref t=#general-options:§10.4} lists the options
that name a destination. This section gives their meaning.

{section 2 bid=where-extraction-writes: Where extraction writes}

Two options name a place. You can give only one of them:

{list t=*:
  {item:
    {b:{C:-d DIR} / {C:--destination DIR}} extracts into DIR. It applies to
    extraction only. If DIR does not exist, the program creates it, with its
    parent directories. This is the {C:unzip -d} spelling.
  }
  {item:
    {b:{C:-C DIR} / {C:--directory DIR}} changes to DIR first, as in {C:tar}. DIR must
    exist already. During create, it also changes how the program finds the
    paths that follow.
  }
}

If you give both, the result is a usage error. Both options name the place of
the work, and a wrong guess puts a tree in a place that the user did not
choose. {C:-d} together with {C:-O}/{C:--to-stdout} is also a usage error, because
content on standard output has no destination directory.

In {C:tar}, {C:-d} means {C:--compare}. In {C:zip}, it deletes entries. Neither
meaning causes a problem here. The compare operation of this tool is
{C:--diff}, with no short letter ({ref t=#compare-disk-diff:§9.8}). {C:-d} does not select an operation.
Thus {C:eictar -d out -f a.ect} fails the one-operation rule, and it deletes
nothing.

{section 2 bid=archive-never-contains-itself: The archive never contains itself}

The writer creates the archive file before the walk starts. A walk through
the directory that holds the archive finds the archive as an ordinary file.
A copy of it reads bytes that the writer is still writing. Thus the writer
skips it and prints one notice. The comparison uses the inode, not
the name. A symlink or a different spelling of the same path cannot get past
it.

{section 2 bid=how-paths-are-stored: How paths are stored}

The writer stores member paths as relative, slash-separated and clean paths,
with {b:no leading {C:/} and no leading {C:..} component}. {C:/etc/passwd} becomes
{C:etc/passwd}. {C:../sibling} becomes {C:sibling}. When a run removes part of a
path, it prints one warning, not one warning for each file:

{code:
eictar: removing leading '/', '..' or drive from member names
}

On Windows, the writer also removes the volume name: {C:C:\\Users\\a.txt} becomes
{C:Users/a.txt}, and {C:\\\\server\\share\\a.txt} becomes {C:a.txt} ({ref t=#other-platforms:§15.1}).

{C:tar}, {C:zip} and {C:pax} use the same rule. An archive holds a portable tree,
not a set of locations in a filesystem. An absolute {C:/etc/passwd} extracts
over the file of the system, not under the destination.

{b:{C:eictar} has no {C:-P}/{C:--absolute-names} to turn this rule off.} An archive
that writes to absolute paths is a hazard. Its only real use is to restore a
system over itself. {C:-d /} does that job, and it states the intention at the
time of extraction, not inside the archive.

A {C:..} inside a path is not an escape. The writer resolves it, so {C:a/b/../c}
becomes {C:a/c}. A path that resolves to nothing ({C:/}, {C:..}, {C:.}) names the root
of the archived tree, which has no name of its own. The writer walks the tree
below it, and does not store a member for the root itself ({ref t=#metadata-extraction:§7.7}).

{section 2 bid=writing-member: Writing a member}

The reader extracts each member to a temporary name in the same directory.
When the digest is correct, it renames the file into place. With a direct
write to the destination, a member that fails part way through destroys the
old file before the failure is known. People usually read an archive
when the original is gone. The rename is atomic. Another reader sees the old
file or the complete new file, never half of a file.

The reader sets the mode and the times on the temporary file {i:before} the
rename. Thus a file is never readable, even briefly, by a user that its final
mode excludes. Directories start as {C:0700} and get their real mode in a final
pass, for the same reason. A directory that ends as {C:0700} must not be open
to other users while the reader writes the files in it.

An interrupted extraction can leave a {C:.eictar-part-*} file. This is
intentional. A leftover file with a clear name is better than a truncated
file with the real name.

{section 2 bid=how-paths-are-checked: How paths are checked on extraction}

A check on the way into the archive is not sufficient, because another
program can write an archive. Thus the reader checks every member again on
the way out. The path must be relative, canonical and free of {C:..}. It must
also resolve to a place under the destination. The reader refuses any other
path, and a refusal is an error, not a skip.

{C:--strip-components} ({ref t=#strip-components-strip-components:§10.14}) removes components only from a path that is
canonical. A path that is not canonical stays as it is, and the check
refuses it. Thus a strip cannot change {C:../../etc/x} into {C:etc/x}, which
passes.

{section 2 bid=symbolic-links: Symbolic links}

Three rules apply to links:

{list t=*:
  {item:
    {b:The writer stores link targets exactly as they are.} This includes
    absolute targets and targets that go outside the tree ({C:../../outside}). A
    link target is content, not a path inside the archive. To restore
    {C:/usr/bin/vi -> /etc/alternatives/vi}, the reader must write that exact
    target. The protection is that extraction never {i:follows} a link. A link
    does nothing until something follows it. The one change is the separator
    on Windows: the archive holds {C:/}, and Windows uses {C:\\} ({ref t=#other-platforms:§15.1}).
  }
  {item:
    {b:The reader restores the times of a link with {C:utimensat} and
    {C:AT_SYMLINK_NOFOLLOW}.} {C:Chtimes} and {C:os.Root.Chtimes} follow the link. On
    a link, they change the time of the file that the link points to, without
    an error. The no-follow call works on the descriptor of the parent
    directory, which comes from {C:os.Root}, with a single name component.
  }
  {item:
    {b:Extraction never goes through a link.} A crafted archive can create
    {C:evil -> /etc} and then write {C:evil/passwd}. That write must not reach
    {C:/etc}. The lexical check of each member path, {C:fsutil.IsStoredPath},
    cannot catch this alone. Two barriers stop it:

    {list t=1:
      {item: {C:beneath} ({C bc=/._:src/internal/archive/dest.go}) opens each directory of a member path one name at a time, from the destination. It refuses a symbolic link at any name of the path, and on Windows a junction, with exit 3. This is also true for a link that stays inside the destination, which the archive made or another user put there. After each open, it checks that the directory that it opened is the one that it looked at. Thus a directory that becomes a link during the open is refused too. An operation that follows the last name of its path (an open, a mode, the times) refuses a link there. No directory below the destination can be the destination itself.}
      {item: Every filesystem operation goes through {C:os.Root} (Go 1.24 and later). {C:os.Root} holds a descriptor on the destination, and refuses every path that leaves it.}
    }
  }
}

Until v1.1.4, the first barrier did not exist. {C:os.Root} {i:follows} a
relative link that stays inside the destination, so extraction did too. A
directory {C:x} and a link {C:x -> .} then gave the metadata of {C:x} to the
destination itself. A link that alice put in her home sent a restore of her
files into the home of bob ({C:Security_Audit_Pass_2.eml}, findings 13 and 14).

These properties are to know:

{list t=*:
  {item: A member path through a link in the destination is refused, also when the user made the link on purpose, such as {C:data -> ../disk2/data}. Extract into the real directory, or remove the link first.}
  {item: {C:beneath} keeps up to 64 directories that it opened, so that the next path in the same directory does not open and check each directory again. An open directory is the one that was checked: a link put at its name later does not change it. The directories at and below a name that the extraction removes, or replaces with a link, are dropped.}
  {item: A hardlink names two paths, and {C:os.Root} has no call that links between two directories that it holds. The program checks both directories, and then links by the paths. A directory that becomes a link between the check and the link can move the hardlink, but only inside the destination.}
  {item: {C:Root.Chmod} and {C:Root.Chtimes} have a documented race (TOCTOU) on the last name. If it becomes a link after the check, the operation can apply to what the link names, but only inside the directory that holds the name.}
}

{section 2 bid=metadata-extraction: Metadata on extraction}

The defaults are safe. Extraction restores what a user can restore, and
nothing that needs privilege or gives it. {C:-p} is the one consent to
restore the rest exactly, as in {C:tar}:

{stable col=3 header sb b== i=3% w=94%:
| Metadata | Default | Option |
| permission bits | restored, less the umask for a user who is not root | {C:-p} restores them exactly |
| setuid, setgid, sticky | {b:not} restored | {C:-p} restores them |
| atime, mtime | restored, links included |  |
| owner | not changed | {C:--preserve-owner}, as root |
| {C:user.*} xattrs, and names with no namespace (macOS) | restored | {C:--no-xattrs} omits them |
| POSIX ACLs | restored as root | {C:-p} restores them for any user. {C:--no-acls} omits them. |
| {C:security.*}, {C:trusted.*} and other {C:system.*} xattrs | {b:not} restored | {C:-p} restores them, as root |
| FIFOs | created, except on macOS ({ref t=#other-platforms:§15.1}) |  |
| device nodes | skipped with a notice | {C:--preserve-devices}, as root |
| sockets | skipped with a notice |  |
| holes | kept |  |
}

{b:An archive cannot open a file to other users by itself.} Without {C:-p}, a
user who is not root gets each mode less the umask, as with {C:tar}. An ACL
can give access as a mode can, so it needs root or {C:-p} too. One notice
counts the ACLs and attributes that {C:-p} restores. Without this rule, an
archive's 0666 file and 0777 directory keep those modes
({C:doc/Security_Audit.eml}).

{b:The root of the archived tree is never applied to the destination.} A
member whose path is {C:.} changes the mode, the times and the owner of the
destination itself. The writer never stores one. The reader skips one
with a notice. Without this rule, a crafted archive can make a destination
of 0755 into 0777.

{b:The owner goes on before the mode.} {C:chown} clears the setuid bit, so a
mode set first loses it. On a regular file, the owner, the xattrs and the mode
go on the temporary file before the rename ({ref t=#writing-member:§7.4}). Thus the file never
exists under its real name with the wrong metadata.

{b:The owner is found by name first.} An archive records the owner by number
and by name. The same uid on two machines is rarely the same person, but the
same user name usually is. If the name does not exist on this machine, the
reader uses the recorded number. {C:tar} uses the same rule.

{b:Privileged xattrs need root and {C:-p}.} {C:security.capability} gives a
program Linux capabilities, as setuid gives it a user. Restored by default,
an archive can install a program that makes any user root.

{C:security.*} also holds an SELinux label that belongs to one machine.
{C:trusted.*} needs {C:CAP_SYS_ADMIN}. Other {C:system.*} names belong to one
filesystem type, and on FreeBSD and NetBSD the system namespace needs root.

{b:Xattr names are recorded exactly, and applied where they fit.} Each
platform names xattrs differently. Linux uses namespaces ({C:user.comment}).
macOS has none ({C:com.apple.quarantine}). FreeBSD and NetBSD have two, which
eictar writes as {C:user.} and {C:system.}. An archive keeps each name as the
source platform gave it, and does not translate it.

On extraction, the reader sets each name that the options allow. A name that
the destination does not take is counted, and one notice at the end of the run
lists those names. Examples are a macOS name on Linux, a Linux {C:security.*}
name on FreeBSD, and any name on a filesystem without xattrs. The member does
not fail.

On create, an xattr larger than the format allows (64 KiB) is left out with a
notice, and the file is archived. Linux never makes one that large, but a
macOS resource fork can be.

{b:A hardlink links to its target.} If the target was not extracted in this
run, for example because a pattern excluded it, the first link gets the
content of the target. Thus a request for one name of a file always gives the
file. The other links to the same target then link to that first copy, so
that the names stay one file.

{b:Pipes and devices are created with {C:mkfifoat} and {C:mknodat}.} Each call
uses a descriptor of the parent directory from {C:os.Root}, and a single name
component. A link in the path cannot redirect them.

{b:Holes stay holes.} The reader writes each data segment at its offset, and
then extends the file to its logical size with {C:ftruncate}. That creates the
holes without writes. With {C:-O}, the reader writes the holes as zeroes,
because a stream has no holes.

{b:A socket is skipped with a notice, on create and on extract.} It has no
content, and its meaning is the process that listens on it. {C:tar} skips
sockets too. This is the one type that is skipped, not refused.

{section 1 bid=concurrency-model: Concurrency model}

{code:
                                  +-> worker 1 -+
 walk and read ---- chunks ------>+-> worker 2 -+--> member spools --> emitter --> archive
 (one goroutine)                  +-> worker N -+   (chunk order)    (one goroutine)
       |                                                                 ^
       +------------ directories and links (no payload) -----------------+
}

{list t=*:
  {item:
    {b:Walk and read.} One goroutine walks the input paths in name order. It
    sends directories, links, pipes and devices directly to the emitter,
    because they have no payload.

    It finds hardlinks by {C:(dev, ino)}: the first name of an inode stores the
    content, and later names point to it. It finds holes with {C:SEEK_DATA} and
    {C:SEEK_HOLE}, and reads only the data segments.

    A file with the size of stored content is hashed first. If the archive
    holds its content, it becomes a reference and goes to the emitter with no
    payload ({ref t=#identical-content-m11:§4.3}). With {C:-Z zstd:train}, a walk of the same paths comes first,
    to take the samples of the dictionary ({ref t=#dictionaries-m10:§4.2}).

    It reads each regular file in order, one chunk at a time, and sends the
    chunks to the workers. It holds each chunk back until it knows if
    another chunk follows, so that the last chunk can be sealed as final
    ({ref t=#aead-layer:§6.3}). Reading is sequential, whatever the number of workers.
  }
  {item:
    {b:Workers.} {C:-j} sets the number of workers. The default is three
    quarters of {C:GOMAXPROCS}, rounded down, and at least 1: 1 and 2 CPUs give
    1 worker, 3 give 2, 4 give 3 and 8 give 6. The other quarter stays free
    for the reader, the emitter and the rest of the machine. The limit is 1024. Each worker compresses a chunk, stores it as
    plaintext if it grew ({ref t=#chunks-that-compression-makes:§4.1}), and seals it if the archive is encrypted. Then
    it adds the chunk to the spool of its member, in chunk order.
  }
  {item:
    {b:Spools.} A spool holds the encoded blob of one member until the emitter
    is ready. It keeps the bytes in memory up to {C:--spill-threshold} (default
    32 MiB). After that, or when memory is short, it moves the bytes to an
    unlinked temporary file in the directory of the archive (on Windows, see
    {ref t=#other-platforms:§15.1}). If the move fails, the member fails. The reader does not wait for memory
    that the spool cannot give back.
  }
  {item:
    {b:Emitter.} One goroutine owns the file offset. It takes each complete
    member in the order that members finish. It writes the blob, records the
    real offset in the index, and releases the spool. Member order in the
    archive has no meaning, so the emitter never waits for a specific member.
    One large file does not block the others.

    A member whose content the archive holds already becomes a reference, and
    the emitter writes no blob for it ({ref t=#identical-content-m11:§4.3}).
  }
  {item:
    {b:Failures.} A chunk that does not encode fails its member. The member
    is then complete when its last chunk returns from the workers, and the
    emitter reports its error. It does not wait for the chunks after the
    failed one in chunk order: they never come. When the emitter fails, the
    build stops, and the reader submits no more chunks. A member that the
    reader reads at that time gets the error, and is not emitted.
  }
}

The writer gives member ids in {b:walk order}, not in the order that members
finish. It sorts the index by id before it writes the index. Thus the byte
layout changes with the number of workers, but the index, and every listing,
does not change.

{section 2 bid=memory-budget: Memory budget}

A global memory budget controls how much data is in flight. {C:--memory-limit}
sets it. The default is the smaller of 25% of RAM and {C:j × 4 × chunk_size}.
{ref t=#other-platforms:§15.1} gives where each platform reads the size of the RAM. When the size is
not known, the default is {C:j × 4 × chunk_size}. The reader goroutine
reserves one chunk of budget before each read. The worker releases the
reservation when it has handed the chunk to the spool.

Four rules prevent deadlock:

{list t=*:
  {item:
    {b:Nothing waits for budget while it holds budget.} A worker releases its
    chunk reservation {i:before} it adds the chunk to the spool, because the
    spool can need memory of its own.
  }
  {item:
    {b:A spool never waits.} A spool releases its bytes only when its member is
    complete. A spool that waits for budget can wait for itself. Thus it uses a
    non-blocking {C:TryAcquire}. If that fails, the spool moves to disk, which is
    also the correct response to low memory.
  }
  {item:
    {b:The reader does not wait for its own member.} While the reader reads a
    member, the finished chunks of that member are in its spool, and they hold
    budget. The member is complete only when the reader goes on. Thus, if the
    budget is not free at once, the reader first moves that spool to disk, and
    then it waits. Everything else that holds budget is released without the
    reader.

    Without this rule, a create with 2 workers and 512-byte chunks stops for
    ever. With the defaults, a file of 24 to 32 MiB on a machine with two CPUs
    can do the same.
  }
  {item:
    {b:The budget is at least two chunks.} The reader holds one chunk while it
    waits for the next. The builder raises a smaller budget to two chunks.
  }
}

A request that is larger than the whole budget goes through immediately.
Waiting cannot help it. Also, the requester can be the only holder that can
release budget. A chunk size above the memory limit gives a slow archive, not
a stopped one.

{section 2 bid=shared-encoder: The shared encoder}

{b:All workers share one encoder, and the encoder must know how many workers
there are.} The setting looks like a codec thread count, but it is not.
The zstd encoder of klauspost keeps a pool of states for its callers. The
size of the pool comes from {C:WithEncoderConcurrency}. {C:EncodeAll} holds one
state for the whole call.

An encoder with a pool of 1, shared by many workers, is correct and free of
races, but it is fully serial. The workers
run, but only one of them compresses at a time.

A measurement shows the cost. With a pool of 1 and 207 MB at zstd level 12,
{C:-j 1} took 3.15 s and {C:-j 8} took 3.28 s. User time was equal to wall time.

When the pool size is equal to the worker count, the same work takes 0.57 s
at {C:-j 8}. User time stays at approximately 3.3 s.
Thus {C:codec.NewEncoder} takes the concurrency as an argument. A codec
conformance test fails if a shared encoder is serial.

{b:The number of encoder states is also bounded by memory.} A state of zstd
at its best speed holds approximately 44 MB with 4 MiB chunks, and an xz
compression holds approximately 30 MB. With 32 workers, that is more than
1 GB. Without a bound, the test machines of FreeBSD and OpenBSD ran out of
memory at the default level.

Thus each codec estimates the memory of one compression in progress. The
writer makes no more states than fit in one sixteenth of the RAM. When
{C:--memory-limit} is given, the states can use half of it. There is always at
least one state. The workers above the number wait for a free state.

{b:zstd caps its window at the chunk size}, rounded up to a power of 2. No
match reaches back past the start of its chunk ({ref t=#member-payloads-chunks-compression:§4}), so a larger window
holds nothing, but the library allocates it. At {C:long=27}, each state held
300 MB. The result of the compression does not change.

A {C:--codec-workers} option for real codec threads (for one very large file)
is not in v1. For this reason it is not in {ref t=#general-options:§10.4}.

{section 2 bid=parallel-extraction: Parallel extraction}

Extraction has four phases:

{list t=1:
  {item:
    {b:Directories, links, pipes and devices}, in path order, on one goroutine. They have no
    payload, and the files need them to exist first. If workers create them
    in parallel, the result depends on a race. A member can go into a real
    directory, or a link with the same name can refuse it. The outcome depends
    on which worker wins.
  }
  {item:
    {b:Files}, across N workers. The workers take the files in batches of
    consecutive files in path order: at most 32 files, or 1 MiB of content.
    Thus the files of one directory mostly go to one worker. Each worker reads
    its own byte ranges with {C:pread}, and has its own decoders (below). There
    is no shared file offset and no lock.

    Each worker keeps open the directory that it writes into, as an
    {C:os.Root} of its own, opened through the destination's root. An operation
    through the destination's root resolves the whole path, one component at
    a time, and each file needed five: a {C:MkdirAll} of its directory, an
    {C:lstat}, the open, the times and the rename. For 20000 small files, these
    lookups were most of the time. Through the directory's root, each takes
    one name. That root is inside the destination too, so the protection of
    {ref t=#where-extraction-writes:§7.1} stays. A directory that does not exist yet is made first.
  }
  {item:
    {b:Hardlinks}, after their targets exist.
  }
  {item:
    {b:Directory metadata} (xattrs, owner, mode, times), deepest first, in a
    final pass. Writing a child changes the mtime of its parent. Also, a
    default ACL set early passes to the files that extraction writes into the
    directory. A directory whose metadata fails here is a failed member.
    Under {C:--keep-going} the pass goes on, so that the other directories do
    not keep their private mode of 0700.
  }
}

Each worker decodes into a buffer of its member's chunk size. The chunk size
comes from the index, so the reader limits the number of workers against the
memory budget: {C:workers × 2 × chunk_size} must fit. Without {C:--memory-limit},
the budget is 25% of RAM, or 512 MiB when RAM is not known.

{b:Decoders are used again.} A new zstd decoder costs more than the decode
of a small file. With a dictionary it costs much more, because each new
decoder parses the dictionary. Thus the reader keeps the decoders that no
member is using, by codec, chunk size and dictionary. A caller takes one for
a member and gives it back after. A list never holds more decoders than
there are callers at one time, and closing the reader closes them. The
workers of {C:--verify} do not use this shared pool: its lock made them wait
for each other. Each worker keeps its own set of decoders, one for each
codec, chunk size and dictionary, with no lock. A decoder keeps no state
from one chunk to the next, so no state goes from one member to the next. A
decoder that returns an error is closed, not used again.

The codec, the chunk size and the dictionary come from the index. Thus a
hostile archive can give each member a kind of decoder of its own, for
example a chunk size of its own. Each idle decoder keeps its tables, and its
dictionary. The pool and each set keep at most 16 kinds. A decoder of a
further kind is closed after its member, as each decoder was before the
pool. A real archive has a few kinds: one for each catalog entry and chunk
size that it uses (Security_Audit.eml, finding 10).

{C:BenchmarkDecoderSetup} (package {C:codec}) decodes one small file. A new
zstd decoder takes about 5 µs, and a decoder used again takes 1.4 µs: 0.26
µs with a dictionary. gzip and xz gain nothing, because their {C:Decode} makes
a new reader for each call. s2 has almost no setup. {C:BenchmarkSmallFiles}
(package {C:archive}) uses 20000 source files of 0.5 to 6 KiB, on 32 CPUs:

{stable col=4 header sb b==:
| Operation, {C:-j 24} | New decoder for each member | Shared pool | Pool, batches and a set for each worker |
| {C:--verify}, zstd | 85 ms | 61 ms | 44 ms |
| {C:--verify}, zstd with {C:train} | 177 ms | 59 ms | 46 ms |
| {C:--verify}, zstd, encrypted | 108 ms | 77 ms | 66 ms |
| extract, zstd | 477 ms | 457 ms | 457 ms |
}

The batches of {C:--verify} are in {ref t=#verify-info:§9.4}. Extraction of small files spent its
time in the lookups of paths, so the decoders gained it little. With the
directory roots and the batches above, and a set of decoders for each
worker, it changed as follows (the same benchmark, {C:-j 24}):

{stable col=3 header sb b== i=3% w=94%:
| Extract | Before | After |
| zstd | 472 ms | 169 ms |
| zstd with {C:train} | 507 ms | 166 ms |
| zstd, encrypted | 542 ms | 181 ms |
| s2 | 515 ms | 162 ms |
}

{C:BenchmarkExtract}, a mixed tree of 64 MiB, went from about 60 ms to about
25 ms. The phases of extraction also keep pointers to the members, not
copies, and the selection of members filters in place. With 20000 members,
extraction allocates 146 MB in place of 233 MB.

{section 2 bid=measured-performance: Measured performance}

{C:make compare} runs {C:bench/compare.sh}, which compares eictar with {C:tar}
piped into a compressor. These results are for source code: the unpacked
modules of the build, 77 MB in 1754 files, on 32 CPUs.

{stable col=4 header sb b==:
| Method | Size | Create | Extract |
| {C:tar \| zstd -3 -T0} | 56.1% | 0.20 s | 0.06 s |
| eictar {C:zstd:level=3} | 60.6% | 0.17 s | 0.09 s |
| {C:tar \| zstd -19 -T0} | 51.1% | 8.53 s | 0.06 s |
| eictar {C:zstd:level=19} | 57.1% | 0.64 s | 0.09 s |
| {C:tar \| xz -6 -T0} | 50.8% | 7.81 s | 1.04 s |
| eictar {C:xz:preset=6} | 57.2% | 2.08 s | 0.53 s |
| {C:tar \| gzip -6} | 61.9% | 1.39 s | 0.26 s |
| eictar {C:gzip:level=6} | 63.9% | 0.13 s | 0.11 s |
| eictar {C:s2} | 65.8% | 0.17 s | 0.09 s |
| eictar {C:zstd:level=3}, encrypted | 60.7% | 0.32 s | 0.29 s |
}

{b:An eictar archive is 3% to 13% larger.} It compresses each file alone,
so it cannot use a pattern that repeats across files. A {C:tar} stream can.
This is the cost of the design ({ref t=#decisions:§1.1}). A dictionary ({ref t=#dictionaries-m10:§4.2}) and references to
identical content ({ref t=#identical-content-m11:§4.3}) take back part of it. These measurements are from
before them.

{b:Strong compression is much faster:}
13 times for zstd level 19, and 11 times for gzip. The chunks of all files
compress in parallel, but one compression stream cannot use more than a few
CPUs. The time of an encrypted archive includes the Argon2id derivation at
the default 256 MiB.

{C:make bench} runs Go benchmarks of eictar alone, on a mixed tree of 64 MiB:
2000 small files, large text files, and random data.

{b:One read buffer stays from file to file}, and a short chunk is copied
out at its own size. A new buffer of the chunk size (4 MiB) for each file,
also a file of 8 KiB, clears 8 GB of memory for 2000 small files. That took
20% of the CPU time. The one buffer makes create 2.8 times faster with zstd,
and 7 times faster with no compression.

{C:BenchmarkCodecs} in {C:codec} measures each codec alone: the encode and the
decode of one chunk of 4 MiB, with no archive and no file system. It also
gives the size of the result. {C:EICTAR_CODEC_DATA} names a file of real
data for the chunk. The benchmarks of the archive package spend most of
their time in the file system, so a change of a compression library shows
first in {C:BenchmarkCodecs}. {C:bench/compare.sh} runs eictar with
{C:--no-config}, so that a configuration file does not change a row.

{section 1 bid=changes-checks: Changes and checks}

This section describes the operations on an existing archive. Append,
update, delete, compact, repair and the change of passphrase change it
({ref t=#write-sequence:§9.1} to {ref t=#compact:§9.3}, {ref t=#repair:§9.5} to {ref t=#change-passphrase:§9.7}). {C:--verify}, {C:--info} ({ref t=#verify-info:§9.4}), {C:--diff} ({ref t=#compare-disk-diff:§9.8}),
a dry run ({ref t=#dry-run-n:§9.9}) and {C:--diff-extract} ({ref t=#compare-extraction-diff-extract:§9.10}) change nothing.
{C:--encrypt-archive} ({ref t=#encryption-existing-archive-encrypt:§9.11}) encrypts an archive that was made without {C:-e}.

Each mutation writes a new index generation. The body is append-only. The
writer never writes over bytes that a valid trailer describes.

A mutation of an encrypted archive needs the passphrase. New members are
sealed, and every new index gets a keyed digest ({ref t=#authenticating-index:§6.4}), even when the
mutation adds no content.

{b:Create writes a new file, not over the old one.} The writer makes a
temporary file beside the archive path, and {C:Close} renames it into place
after the trailer is synced. Until the rename, a file that is already at the
path stays as it was. Thus a create that fails, for example on a mistyped
source path, does not destroy a backup.

The new file gets the permissions of the file that it replaces. If the path
is a symbolic link, the file that the link points to is replaced, and the
link stays. The walk skips both the temporary file and the old archive, so
neither goes into the new archive.

{b:A symbolic link in the path must be one that the kernel follows.} Take a
sticky directory that everyone can write, such as {C:/tmp}. There, Linux
follows a link only when it belongs to the caller or to the owner of the
directory ({C:fs.protected_symlinks}). The writer resolves the path itself and
renames over the target, so the kernel's rule never applies. Thus the writer
applies the rule to each link in the path, for create, compact, change of
passphrase and {C:--encrypt-archive}. Without this rule, a link {C:/tmp/backup.ect -> /etc/passwd} that
another user plants makes a create by root replace {C:/etc/passwd}.

{section 2 bid=write-sequence: The write sequence}

Append, update and delete all use this sequence:

{list t=1:
  {item:
    Open the archive for reading and writing. Check it, and load the index.
  }
  {item:
    Write the new blobs {b:after the old trailer}, at the old end of the
    file. A new dictionary comes first, if the run trains one ({ref t=#dictionaries-m10:§4.2}). The old
    index and the old trailer stay in place as dead space.
  }
  {item:
    Write the new index. Then {C:fsync}: one {C:fsync} makes the blobs and the
    index durable.
  }
  {item:
    Write the 96-byte trailer with {C:generation + 1} and
    {C:prev_index_offset = <old index_offset>}. Then {C:fsync}.
  }
}

Step 4 is the commit. Until then, the old trailer still describes the old
generation exactly, at its old place.

The old trailer must stay intact. A new blob written over it destroys the only
trailer in the file, and after a crash nothing is left to recover. Thus each
generation costs 96 bytes of dead space.

If a mutation fails before step 4 with an error (not a crash), the writer
truncates the file back to its old length. The archive is then exactly as it
was. A run that changes nothing, because every path was skipped or up to
date, also leaves the file as it was. It adds no generation.

The append writer opens the file for reading and writing, and then goes to
the old end of the file before it writes. Without that step, the first write
goes on top of the header.

{section 2 bid=one-live-member-each: One live member for each path}

An archive has at most one live member for each path.

{list t=*:
  {item:
    {b:Append} ({C:-r}) adds the named paths. If a path is already live in the
    archive, {C:--on-conflict} decides:

    {list t=*:
      {item:
        {C:replace} (the default): tombstone the old member and add the new one, in
        the same generation. This agrees with the result that a {C:tar} user sees,
        where the last copy wins.
      }
      {item:
        {C:skip}: keep the old member and add nothing for that path.
      }
      {item:
        {C:error}: stop, and leave the archive unchanged (exit 2).
      }
    }
  }
  {item:
    {b:Update} ({C:-u}) replaces a live member only when the test of {ref t=#update-behavior-u:§10.5} finds
    it out of date. It adds paths that are not in the archive.
  }
  {item:
    {b:Delete} ({C:--delete}) tombstones the live members that match the
    patterns. A directory pattern also matches the members under it. If a
    pattern matches nothing, the program stops and changes nothing.
  }
}

A path that one run names two times, for example {C:a} and a directory that
contains {C:a}, gets one member. The program skips the second one with a
notice. This rule applies to create also.

The old member becomes a tombstone only after the new member is recorded.
Under {C:--keep-going}, a path that cannot be read keeps its old member. If the
tombstone came first, the archive lost both copies.

A tombstone keeps its record in the index, with {C:"dead": true}. Its blob
stays until {C:--compact}.

{b:A hardlink can point to a tombstone.} Suppose that {C:a} and {C:b} are two names
of one file, and a new {C:a} replaces the old one. {C:b} still points to the old
member, whose content is in the old blob. The old member cannot give its
content to {C:b}, because the chunk AAD binds the member id ({ref t=#aead-layer:§6.3}). Thus a
tombstone that a live hardlink points to stays readable. Extraction finds it,
and compact keeps it.

The same rule applies to a tombstone that a live reference names ({ref t=#identical-content-m11:§4.3}).

{section 2 bid=compact: Compact}

{C:--compact} writes a new archive to a temporary file beside the original. It
copies the live blobs byte for byte. It also copies the blobs of the
tombstones that a live hardlink points to, or whose content a live member
shares ({ref t=#identical-content-m11:§4.3}). It does not decompress or unseal a blob.

The new archive keeps the uuid, the crypto header and the encryption settings
of the original. It uses the next generation. Then the program renames the new
file over the original with {C:rename(2)}, and syncs the directory (on
Windows, see {ref t=#other-platforms:§15.1}). A crash before the rename leaves the original
unchanged.

The new file gets the mode of the original. It also gets the owner and the
group, when the process has permission to set them. Otherwise the program
prints a warning. If {C:-f} names a symbolic link, compact replaces the file
that the link points to, and the link stays.

Compact copies each dictionary that a kept member uses, byte for byte, and
drops the others. A dictionary that no live member uses is dead space.
Compact also drops each catalog entry that no kept member uses, and gives
the members the new positions. Thus no entry can name a dictionary that the
new archive does not hold.

If there is no dead space and no tombstone to remove, compact changes nothing
and says so. For an encrypted archive, compact needs the passphrase, for the
keyed index digest.

{b:{C:--recompress SPEC} encodes every member again.} It decodes each kept
member, and sends it through the same pipeline that create uses. The
pipeline uses the codec of SPEC, and the {C:--chunk-size}, {C:-j} and memory
options. A member keeps its id, its digest, its metadata and its tombstone
mark. A member that shares content stays a reference.

The catalog gets the one new entry. With {C:train}, a new dictionary replaces
the old ones.

A sealed member gets a new member salt, and thus a new key. The member
cannot keep its old key: its new chunks are new ciphertext at the old chunk
indexes, so each chunk nonce gets a second use, which {ref t=#aead-layer:§6.3} forbids. The digest of the old
content is checked as it is decoded. The pipeline calculates the new digest
from the same bytes, so a member that changes on the way stops the run.

{section 2 bid=verify-info: Verify and info}

{C:--verify} checks an archive without extraction. By default it decodes every
live member, and checks every digest and every AEAD tag. That catches damage
in the blobs, which is the likely fault. {C:--quick} checks only the structure:
the header, the trailer, the index and the member ranges. It reads no member
data. A damaged member gives exit 3, and the output names each one.

{C:--verify} also decodes each tombstone that a selected hardlink points to,
or whose content a selected member shares, because extraction reads it. It
decodes each blob one time. A hardlink or a reference passes or fails with
the blob that it uses. Only the selected members count in the result.

{C:--verify} decodes the blobs in parallel, with {C:-j} workers. It uses the
memory bound of extraction ({ref t=#parallel-extraction:§8.3}). The workers finish in any order, but the
program reports the results in the order of the index. Thus the output, the
counts and the error that names the first damaged member do not change with
{C:-j}. One member is decoded by one worker, so a single large member does not
go faster. On 32 CPUs, an archive of 200 files of 10 MB (2 GB, in the page
cache) took 3.2 s with one worker and 0.29 s with the default of 24.

The workers take the members in batches of consecutive members: at most 64
members, or 1 MiB of content. With one channel operation for each member,
the hand-over of a small member cost more than its decode. A profile of
20000 small files ({ref t=#parallel-extraction:§8.3}) shows where the rest of the time goes. Of 44 ms
with 24 workers, the decode of the index when the archive opens takes about
20 ms, and the list of members to check takes about 4 ms. These parts use
one goroutine. {ref t=#outstanding-items:§15.2} records the index decode.

Patterns and {C:-R} select members, as for {C:-t}. Each pattern and each {C:-R}
must match at least one member. On success, {C:--verify} prints one summary
line, unless {C:-q} is given. {C:-v} also prints each member that passed.

{C:--info} shows the header, the crypto parameters, the generation, the counts
of live and dead members, the codecs in use, and the dead space. The content
row gives the plaintext bytes of the live members and the bytes of their
blobs. The percentage after it is the stored size as a part of the
plaintext size, for example {C:stored in 35871 bytes (32.5%)}. An archive
with no content shows no percentage. The dead
space is the number of bytes that {C:--compact} removes. It also shows each
dictionary, and the members that share content with the bytes that they did
not store.

{section 2 bid=repair: Repair}

After a crash during a mutation, the file ends with new data, not with a
trailer. The reader refuses the file with exit 3, and the message names
{C:--repair}. The reader does not open an older generation automatically. A
backup tool must not give older data without a clear signal.

{C:--repair} searches back from the end of the file for the last valid trailer.
It reads the file in windows of 1 MiB and looks for the trailer magic. For
each candidate, it does every check that a reader does, as if the file ended
after that trailer. These checks are the magic, the CRC, the bounds, the
index digest, the index decode and the member ranges. For an encrypted
archive, the digest check needs the passphrase.

{C:--repair} asks for the passphrase one time, and derives the keys one time.
An archive of archives, stored with no compression, contains a real trailer
inside each member. One Argon2id run for each candidate is too slow.

Then {C:--repair} truncates the file after the first valid trailer from the
end. That restores the last complete generation exactly, and it reports how
many bytes it removed. On an archive that opens correctly, {C:--repair} changes
nothing. A wrong passphrase stops the repair before the scan starts, because
that archive is not damaged.

{b:A crash and damage look different at the end of the file.} The writer
syncs the index before it writes the trailer, and the trailer is its last
write ({ref t=#write-sequence:§9.1}). Thus a crash leaves blob bytes or index bytes at the end, and
no trailer. A file that ends with the trailer magic, 96 bytes from its end,
ends with a whole trailer. Its newest generation was complete, and later its
trailer or its index was damaged. A repair then removes a whole generation
that was committed: the members that it added or changed are lost.

Thus, if the file ends with the trailer magic, {C:--repair} stops with exit 3
and changes nothing. The message gives the generation that a repair restores
and the number of bytes that it removes. The user can restore the archive
from a copy, or give {C:--discard-damaged} to remove the generation. A
configuration file cannot set {C:--discard-damaged}. {C:--repair -n} gives the
same numbers, and changes nothing, also with {C:-q}. It takes the lock and
scans as a repair does.

One crash can also end the file with the magic: a power cut in the write of
a trailer that falls across two sectors, when only the first sector reaches
the disk. Then the refusal is not necessary, but it loses nothing: the
generation was not committed, and {C:--discard-damaged} removes it.
{C:TestRepairOfADamagedTrailer}, {C:TestRepairOfAnInterruptedWrite} and
{C:TestRepairThroughRun} cover these rules.

The body has no framing, so a scan cannot recover an archive whose every
trailer is damaged. This is the cost of the unframed body ({ref t=#member-payloads-chunks-compression:§4}).

{section 2 bid=concurrent-writers: Concurrent writers}

The problem statement requires that two runs that change one archive at the
same time never damage it. Without protection, two appends both write after
the same old trailer, and each writes over the blobs of the other.
Overlapping cron jobs cause this.

Each writer takes an advisory lock on the archive file:

{list t=*:
  {item:
    Append, update, delete, compact and repair take an exclusive lock
    ({C:flock(LOCK_EX | LOCK_NB)}) before they read the trailer. They keep it
    until the new trailer is synced, or until the rename for compact.
  }
  {item:
    Create locks the file that it replaces, if there is one, and keeps the lock
    until its rename.
  }
  {item:
    A create that finds no file at the path has nothing to lock. Its rename
    does not replace a file: it uses {C:link(2)} on Unix, and a rename without
    the flag that replaces on Windows. If another run made an archive there in
    the meantime, the create stops with exit 4 and leaves that archive. A
    rename over it can lose an append that a third run is writing into it.
    The TLA+ model of the writers found this (testing.md §5, step 5). A file system
    with no hard links gets a check and then a rename, with a short window
    between them.
  }
  {item:
    If another process holds the lock, the program stops with exit 4 and the
    message "another eictar is changing this archive". It does not wait. A
    backup job that waits can hang with no message.
  }
  {item:
    List, extract, {C:--verify}, {C:--info}, {C:--diff}, {C:--diff-extract} and every
    dry run ({C:-n}) except that of {C:--repair} take no lock. Create and compact
    rename a complete file over the path, so a reader sees one complete
    generation. An append, an update and a delete write in place, so until
    their trailer lands the file ends in their new bytes. A reader then finds
    no trailer at the end, which is also how damage looks. Thus, when a
    reader finds damage, it tries the lock without waiting. If another
    process holds it, the reader stops with exit 4 and the message "another
    eictar is changing this archive", not with exit 3 and the name of
    {C:--repair}. The try takes the lock only for a moment: the reader closes
    the file at once. {C:TestReadDuringAnAppend} covers it.
  }
}

{b:A lock is on one inode, and compact and create rename a new inode over the
path.} Suppose that a process opens the path immediately before such a
rename. It then locks the old file, which the rename has unlinked, and its
writes are lost. Thus, after it gets the lock, a writer makes sure that the
path still names the file that it locked. If not, it stops with the same
error.

A lock on the new file of compact does not stop the lost write.

The lock is advisory: a program that does not ask for it can still write.
The kernel removes the lock when the process ends, so a crash leaves no stale
lock. Linux, macOS, the BSDs and the other UNIX systems use {C:flock}. Windows
uses {C:LockFileEx} ({ref t=#other-platforms:§15.1}). Plan 9, WASI and AIX have no {C:flock}, so there
the program does not find a second writer. A lock on NFS is not
reliable, and the man page says so.

{section 2 bid=change-passphrase: Change of passphrase}

{C:--change-passphrase} seals the archive's data key under a new passphrase
({ref t=#crypto-header-cbor-plaintext:§6.2}). It needs the old passphrase, and it asks for the new one two times,
as create does. {C:--new-passphrase-file} and {C:--new-passphrase-env} give the
new one without a prompt. The {C:--kdf-*} options set new Argon2id parameters.
Without them, the archive keeps the parameters that it has. Thus a change of
passphrase is also the way to make an old archive's key derivation stronger.

{b:The change writes the file again, as compact does ({ref t=#compact:§9.3}).} It writes a new
file beside the archive, with the new crypto header, and renames it over the
archive. A crash before the rename leaves the old archive, which the old
passphrase opens. A change in place is not possible, because the crypto
header changes size with the new parameters. It is not safe either: a crash
in the middle of the only copy of the wrapped key loses the archive.

The data key does not change, so the blobs are copied byte for byte, with no
second encryption. Like compact, the change drops the dead space. The old
wrapped key is not in the new file.

On most filesystems, the blocks of the old file are free space until
something writes over them. Thus a person can still read what the archive
held then with a copy of the old file. The old passphrase and access to the
disk are enough too. A change of passphrase cannot take back what someone
already has. Only a new archive, with a new
data key, can protect new content from a leaked key.

{section 2 bid=compare-disk-diff: Compare with the disk ({C:--diff})}

{C:--diff} compares the live members of an archive with the tree on disk. It
changes nothing. It is {C:tar -d} ({C:--compare}), with two differences. It also
finds the paths on disk that the archive does not hold. It compares content
by digest. It has no short letter, because {C:-d} is {C:--destination} ({ref t=#where-extraction-writes:§7.1}).

{b:What it compares.} For each member that is on disk, the program compares
these items:

{list t=*:
  {item:
    The type. A hardlink member must be a regular file. When the types are
    different, the program compares nothing more for that path.
  }
  {item:
    The size and the content of a file. When the sizes are equal, the program
    reads the file. It compares the digest with the member's digest, as
    {C:-u --update-mode=digest} does ({ref t=#update-behavior-u:§10.5}).
  }
  {item:
    The target of a symbolic link, and the numbers of a device node.
  }
  {item:
    For a hardlink, that the file is the same file as its target. The program
    checks this when the target is live and on disk.
  }
  {item:
    The mode, except for a symbolic link ({ref t=#symbolic-links:§7.6}).
  }
  {item:
    The owner, by number. An archive made with {C:--no-owner} has no owner to
    compare.
  }
  {item:
    The modification time, to the nanosecond.
  }
  {item:
    The extended attributes and the ACLs of files and directories, as create
    records them.
  }
}

{C:--no-owner}, {C:--no-xattrs} and {C:--no-acls} leave out what they name. The
access time and the change time are not compared, because extraction does
not restore them.

The content check reads no member data. Thus {C:--diff} does not check the
archive itself. {C:--verify} does that ({ref t=#verify-info:§9.4}). {C:--diff-extract} also extracts
each member, and compares the copy with the disk byte by byte ({ref t=#compare-extraction-diff-extract:§9.10}).

{b:Which paths it compares.} The archive does not record the paths that
create was given. Thus {C:--diff} gets the start of each walk from the
members. A walk starts at the highest directory of the archive above a
member. When no directory of the archive is above the member, the walk
starts at the member. Examples:

{list t=*:
  {item:
    {C:eictar -cf a.ect src} stores {C:src}, so {C:--diff} walks all of {C:src}. It
    reports a new file in {C:src}.
  }
  {item:
    {C:eictar -cf a.ect src/main.go} stores no {C:src}. Thus {C:--diff} compares
    {C:src/main.go} alone, and does not report the other files in {C:src}.
  }
  {item:
    {C:eictar -cf a.ect -C dir .} stores no {C:.} ({ref t=#how-paths-are-stored:§7.3}). Thus {C:--diff} does not
    report a new path directly in {C:dir}. It reports a new path in a directory
    of the archive.
  }
}

The program reports each path that the walk finds and the archive does not
hold. For such a directory, it reports the directory only, not its
contents. In the same way, for a member directory that is not on disk, it
reports the directory only. Create skips sockets ({ref t=#metadata-extraction:§7.7}), so a socket on disk
is not reported. The walk skips the archive file, as create does ({ref t=#archive-never-contains-itself:§7.2}).

The walk is the walk of create, with the same options: {C:--exclude},
{C:--exclude-regex}, {C:-R}, {C:-h} and {C:--one-file-system}. Give the options
that create had. Otherwise, the paths that create left out show as
differences. A configuration that sets these options for create also sets
them for {C:--diff} ({ref t=#what-configuration-can-set:§11.1}).

Patterns and {C:-R} select the members, as for {C:-t}. Each must match at least
one member ({ref t=#exit-codes:§10.7}). They also select the paths on disk. Thus
{C:--diff -f a.ect '*.go'} reports a new {C:.go} file anywhere in the archived
tree, and no other new file. A pattern cannot name a path that is only on
disk, because it must match a member.

{b:The output.} The program writes one line for each difference to stdout,
in the order of the paths:

{code:
src/main.go: content differs
src/util.go: mode differs: archive 0644, disk 0600
src/new.go: not in the archive
src/old: not on disk
}

{C:-q} does not hide the differences, because they are the result. When there
is no difference, the program writes one summary line, unless {C:-q} is given.
The exit status is 0 when there is no difference, and 1 when there is one,
as for {C:diff} and {C:tar -d} ({ref t=#exit-codes:§10.7}). A file that cannot be read is an error
of one member. It stops the run with exit 4. Under {C:--keep-going}, it gives
a warning and exit 1. The differences found for that path before the error
are still reported. For example, macOS cannot list the attributes of a
file that it cannot read, and the mode of that file is then a difference.

{b:Results that are correct, but that can surprise:}

{list t=*:
  {item:
    A tree extracted without {C:-p} has each mode less the umask. A user who is
    not root does not get the owners of the archive. Both are differences. An
    operational test checks that a tree extracted with {C:-p} has none. On macOS,
    extraction skips pipes ({ref t=#other-platforms:§15.1}), so a pipe of the archive is not on disk,
    and the test expects that one difference.
  }
  {item:
    A create writes the archive into its directory. If that directory is in
    the tree, its modification time changes, and that is a difference.
  }
  {item:
    An archive made with {C:--no-xattrs} holds no attributes. Thus each
    attribute on disk is a difference, unless {C:--diff} has {C:--no-xattrs} too.
  }
}

{b:Speed.} {C:-j} workers read the files in parallel. One goroutine walks the
tree and compares the metadata. The program sorts the differences, so the
output does not change with {C:-j}. On 32 CPUs, a tree of 2.0 GB in 21270
files, in the page cache, took 1.25 s with one worker and 0.27 s with the
default of 24. {C:--verify} of the same archive took 0.32 s. The walk opens
each file, while it holds the file's directory ({ref t=#archiving-tree-that-other:§14.5}), and a worker reads
it. On 20000 small files in 2000 directories, this made {C:--diff} take
0.175 s, against 0.14 s when the workers opened the files by their paths.

{b:A hostile archive.} The member paths decide where each walk starts on
disk. The program checks each path as extraction does ({ref t=#how-paths-are-checked:§7.5}). Each walk
starts inside an {C:os.Root} of the directory, and goes through each
directory that it opens ({ref t=#archiving-tree-that-other:§14.5}). Thus a symbolic link on disk cannot take
the walk out of the directory, also when it takes the place of a directory
during the walk. Without this rule, an archive can make
{C:--diff} read any file of the system. The output then tells if that file
has a given digest. Below its start, the walk follows no link, except with
{C:-h}. It opens each file as create does ({ref t=#changes-checks:§9}, {C:doc/Security_Audit.eml}
finding 3).

{b:A directory above a member is not an extra path.} {C:-r a/b/c} adds the
member {C:a/b/c}, and no member for {C:a/b}. Extraction makes {C:a/b}. Thus a
directory on disk that holds a compared member is not reported, but a new
path in it is.

{b:With {C:--strip-components},} {C:--diff} compares the tree that extraction
with the same option writes ({ref t=#strip-components-strip-components:§10.14}). The patterns, {C:--exclude} and {C:-R}
match the paths in the archive. A path on disk is shorter than its path in
the archive, so the walk does not apply these options. Instead, a path on
disk that is not a member is in the comparison when one of the removed
prefixes before it gives a path that the options select. A hard link is
compared with the path of its target after the strip. When a collision
left the target out, another member holds that path, and extraction gave
the link a copy of the content. Then the link is not compared with a
target.

{section 2 bid=dry-run-n: Dry run ({C:-n})}

{C:-n} ({C:--dry-run}) shows the paths that an operation would write or delete,
and changes nothing. It applies to {C:-c}, {C:-r}, {C:-u}, {C:-x} and {C:--delete}.
On another operation, it is a usage error ({ref t=#exit-codes:§10.7}). The main use is to see
what a pattern, an {C:--exclude} or an {C:-R} selects before a real run. {C:rsync}
and {C:make} have the same letter. {C:tar} uses {C:-n} for {C:--seek}, which this
program does not have.

{b:The same selection as the real run.} A dry run does not have its own
copy of the rules. It calls the code of the real run:

{list t=*:
  {item:
    Create, append and update walk the paths with the walk of create, with
    {C:--exclude}, {C:--exclude-regex}, {C:-R}, {C:-h} and {C:--one-file-system}. The
    walk skips the archive file ({ref t=#archive-never-contains-itself:§7.2}). A path given twice, a socket and an entry
    that no member can hold are skipped or fail as in create.
  }
  {item:
    Append applies {C:--on-conflict} to each path that the archive holds ({ref t=#one-live-member-each:§9.2}).
    Update applies {C:--update-mode} ({ref t=#update-behavior-u:§10.5}). Thus {C:-u -n} does not show the
    paths that are up to date.
  }
  {item:
    Extract selects the members as extract does, with the patterns,
    {C:--exclude} and {C:-R}. It checks each path ({ref t=#how-paths-are-checked:§7.5}). It applies {C:-k} and
    {C:--newer-only} to the files that are already under the destination. It
    skips a device node without {C:--preserve-devices}, and a socket. With {C:-O},
    it shows the members whose content goes to stdout. With
    {C:--strip-components}, it shows the paths after the strip, and gives the
    warnings for the collisions ({ref t=#strip-components-strip-components:§10.14}).
  }
  {item:
    Delete selects the live members as delete does.
  }
}

{b:The output.} Each path is on its own line on stdout, so that a pipe can
use the list. {C:-q} does not hide the paths, because they are the result, as
for {C:--diff} ({ref t=#compare-disk-diff:§9.8}). With {C:-v}, a word before each path tells what happens
to it: {C:add}, {C:replace}, {C:extract} or {C:delete}. {C:replace} is a path that
replaces a live member ({C:-r}, {C:-u}), or a file on disk ({C:-x}). Unless {C:-q}
is given, a summary line on stderr gives the number of paths and the size of
their content. The size counts the content of a hardlinked file once, as
create stores it once. It does not subtract identical content ({ref t=#identical-content-m11:§4.3}). The
summary also gives the number of paths that are replaced, unchanged,
skipped and failed, and the members that a collision of
{C:--strip-components} left out. The notices of the real run, for example for a socket,
go to stderr as usual.

{code:
$ eictar -unvf home.ect -C ~ Documents
replace  Documents/notes.txt
add      Documents/new.pdf
eictar: dry run, nothing written: 2 paths, 1.2 MiB of file content; 1 replace a member; 340 unchanged
}

The paths come in the order of the walk for create, append and update, in
the order of the paths for extract, and in the order of the index for
delete.

{b:What a dry run does not do:}

{list t=*:
  {item:
    It makes no archive and no temporary file. It locks no archive. It makes
    no destination directory. A destination that does not exist holds no
    files, so no file is replaced.
  }
  {item:
    It reads no file content, except for {C:-u --update-mode=digest}, which
    compares digests. Thus a file that cannot be read, or an extended
    attribute that cannot be read, fails only in the real run. A walk error,
    for example a directory that cannot be read, fails in the dry run too.
  }
  {item:
    It does not train a dictionary, and it does not compress.
  }
  {item:
    For an encrypted archive, append, update, extract and delete ask for the
    passphrase, because they read the index. With {C:--encrypt-index}, the
    paths are in the encrypted index. The digests of an encrypted archive are
    keyed, so {C:-u --update-mode=digest} needs the key ({ref t=#crypto-header-cbor-plaintext:§6.2}). Create asks for
    no passphrase.
  }
}

{C:--progress} with {C:-n} on the command line is a usage error, because a dry
run reads almost nothing. A {C:progress} value from a configuration is
ignored for a dry run ({ref t=#what-configuration-can-set:§11.1}). A configuration cannot set {C:dry-run}.

The exit status is that of the real run: 0, or 1 when a path failed under
{C:--keep-going}. A conflict under {C:--on-conflict=error} exits with 2, a
pattern or an {C:-R} that matches nothing exits with 2, and an unsafe member
path exits with 3 ({ref t=#exit-codes:§10.7}).

{section 2 bid=compare-extraction-diff-extract: Compare with an extraction ({C:--diff-extract})}

{C:--diff-extract} is for the time before you remove the source of an
archive. It extracts each member with the real extraction code, one at a
time, into a scratch directory. It compares each extracted copy with the
file on disk, byte by byte, and then removes the copy. It also does all that
{C:--diff} does ({ref t=#compare-disk-diff:§9.8}). It is slow: each byte is decoded, written, read again
and compared. The code is in {C:diffextract.go}.

{b:Why {C:--diff} and {C:--verify} are not sufficient.} Together, they show that
the disk, the stored digests and the decoded content agree. Both depend on
the stored digests, and on the code that calculates them. Two faults can
pass both commands:

{list t=*:
  {item:
    {b:A fault in the digest code.} Create, {C:--verify} and {C:--diff} use the
    same function. Suppose that it hashes only the first chunk of a file.
    Then the three agree, and the archive can still hold the wrong data.
    A comparison of bytes does not use a digest.
  }
  {item:
    {b:A fault in extraction.} {C:--verify} decodes, but it writes no file. Only
    extraction writes a file, makes the holes of a sparse file, and sets the
    mode, the times and the attributes. A fault there shows only on the day of
    a restore.
  }
}

{C:--diff-extract} removes both gaps. The extracted copy goes through the
same code as a restore, and the comparison uses no digest.

{b:The name.} {C:--diff --extract} is not possible, because {C:--extract} is
the long name of {C:-x}. {C:--diff-extract} is an operation of its own, as
{C:--diff} is, with no short letter. It takes the options of {C:--diff}.

{b:The steps for each regular file.} The walk of {C:--diff} finds the file on
disk, and compares the metadata with the member, as before. Then a worker:

{list t=1:
  {item:
    Extracts the member into the scratch directory, under a name of its own,
    not under its path. The worker calls {C:writeFile} of extraction, with the
    scratch directory as the directory and the scratch name as the base. The
    member path goes only into the messages. Thus the decode checks the
    digest, and the mode, the times and the attributes go on the copy, as in
    a restore.
  }
  {item:
    Opens the copy again by its name, after the rename. Thus the comparison
    reads what extraction wrote, not a buffer in memory.
  }
  {item:
    Compares the sizes. Then it reads the copy and the source in blocks of
    1 MiB, and compares each pair of blocks. The first difference gives its
    byte offset in the message.
  }
  {item:
    Compares the metadata of the copy with that of the source (see below).
  }
  {item:
    Removes the copy, also when a step failed.
  }
}

The worker also compares the digest of the source with the member's, as
{C:--diff} does. For a dense member, the one read of step 3 gives the digest
too. The digest of a sparse member covers only its data, so the worker
reads such a file two times.

{b:The other member types.} Each type goes through its own extraction code,
in the scratch directory:

{list t=*:
  {item:
    A directory: the worker makes an empty directory, and applies the
    metadata, as extraction does at its end ({ref t=#parallel-extraction:§8.3}). It compares the
    metadata, then removes the directory.
  }
  {item:
    A symbolic link: the worker makes the link, and compares its target. It
    also compares the time of the link, when the platform can set it.
  }
  {item:
    A pipe: the worker makes the pipe, and compares its mode and its time.
    macOS has no safe call to make a pipe ({ref t=#other-platforms:§15.1}). There, the pipe is
    compared with the archive only, with one notice for the run.
  }
  {item:
    A device node: only as root, because only root can make one. Without
    root, the comparison is that of {C:--diff}, the device numbers, with one
    notice for the run.
  }
  {item:
    A hardlink: {C:--diff} already makes sure that the path on disk is the same
    file as its target. The content is compared through the target. When the
    target is not compared, the hardlink is extracted with the content of its
    target, as extraction does.
  }
  {item:
    A member that shares the content of another ({ref t=#identical-content-m11:§4.3}) is a file of its own
    on disk. Thus each such path is extracted and compared on its own.
  }
  {item:
    A sparse file is extracted with its holes. The comparison reads the holes
    as zeros, so it compares the content, not the layout of the holes.
  }
}

On Windows, only files and directories are extracted, and the mode is not
compared ({ref t=#other-platforms:§15.1}).

{b:What the metadata comparison includes.} The copy can have only what
extraction restores. Thus the copy is extracted as with {C:-p}: the full
mode, the ACLs and the user attributes. As root, it also gets the owner and
the privileged attributes. The comparison of the copy with the source
includes these items, and the modification time. Items that extraction
cannot restore without root are compared only by {C:--diff}, between the
record in the archive and the disk. {C:--no-owner}, {C:--no-xattrs} and
{C:--no-acls} leave out what they name, from both comparisons.

{b:Each cause is reported one time.} When the source differs from the
archive in one item, {C:--diff} reports it. The copy then differs from the
source in the same item, for the same reason. The program does not report
that item of the copy again. Thus a line {C:after extraction} always shows a
fault that the comparison with the archive did not show.

{b:The precision of times.} Before the first copy, the program sets the
time of a probe file in the scratch directory to a time with nanoseconds.
Then it reads the time back. The difference gives the precision of the
file system: 1 ns, 100 ns, 1 µs, 1 ms, 10 ms, 1 s or 2 s. Two times that
differ by less than this precision are the same. Thus a scratch directory
on FAT, which keeps times to 2 s, gives no false differences of time. A
file system that does not keep a time to within 2 s cannot be used, and
the run stops with a message that names {C:--scratch-dir}. The probe also
finds whether the platform can set the time of a link.

{b:Attributes that the scratch file system refuses.} Extraction counts
them ({ref t=#metadata-extraction:§7.7}), and the copy does not have them. The comparison of the copy
leaves them out. They are compared only by {C:--diff}, and one notice at the
end of the run names them.

{b:The scratch directory.} The program makes one directory with mode 0700,
named {C:eictar-diff-} and a random suffix, and removes it at the end. It
holds at most one copy for each worker. Thus it needs space for the {C:-j}
largest files that are compared at the same time.

{list t=*:
  {item:
    {b:The location.} The default is the directory of {C:$TMPDIR}, or {C:/tmp}.
    {C:--scratch-dir DIR} puts it in DIR. The default keeps the copies on the
    local computer. The archive is often on a backup disk, and the copies of
    an encrypted archive are plaintext.
  }
  {item:
    {b:Not in the compared tree.} A scratch directory inside a walk root is a
    usage error (exit 2). Its creation changes the time of its parent, and
    that is a difference. The walk also finds the copies. The program
    compares the paths after it resolves their links.
  }
  {item:
    {b:A full disk.} A copy that does not fit is an error of the member, as in
    extraction. Without {C:--keep-going}, it stops the run with exit 4. The
    message names {C:--scratch-dir}. On many systems {C:/tmp} is in RAM, so a
    large file can fill it.
  }
  {item:
    {b:An encrypted archive.} Each copy is plaintext, as is the source. The
    program removes each copy, but it does not overwrite it. On a disk, the
    blocks of a copy can stay until the file system uses them again. Thus
    put the scratch directory on a disk that can hold the plaintext of the
    source.
  }
  {item:
    {b:A crash.} After a crash, the directory can stay, with at most {C:-j}
    copies in it. Its name tells what it is.
  }
}

{b:The output.} The lines of {C:--diff} do not change. A difference that the
extracted copy shows says {C:after extraction}, with the values of the
source and the copy:

{code:
src/main.go: content differs after extraction, at byte 1048576
src/run.sh: mode differs after extraction: source 0755, extracted 0644
}

The program sorts all the lines by path, as {C:--diff} does, so the output
does not change with {C:-j}. The exit status is that of {C:--diff}: 0 with no
difference, and 1 with a difference. A member that fails its digest or its
tag is damage in the archive, and gives exit 3, as {C:--verify} does. Under
{C:--keep-going}, the run goes on past damage, but the exit is still 3. An
error of the scratch directory, or of a read, gives exit 4, or 1 under
{C:--keep-going}.

{b:Speed and memory.} One member is extracted by one worker, so {C:-j} makes
many files faster, but not one large file. Each worker holds its decoders,
as in extraction, and two blocks of 1 MiB for the comparison. The memory
bound of extraction applies ({ref t=#parallel-extraction:§8.3}). The second read of the copy usually
comes from the page cache. Thus the check is of the code, not of the scratch
disk.

{b:A hostile archive.} The walk is that of {C:--diff}, with the same rules
({ref t=#compare-disk-diff:§9.8}). The copies go into the scratch directory by their scratch names, so
a member path cannot put a file outside it. The mode of 0700 keeps other
users out of the directory, also when a copy has the setuid bit.

{b:Tests.} The claim of this section is that {C:--diff-extract} finds faults
that {C:--diff} and {C:--verify} do not find. {C:diffextract_test.go} shows each
case:

{list t=*:
  {item:
    {C:TestDiffExtractFindsWhatTheDigestMisses}: a digest that covers only the
