    {C:TestDiffExtractThroughRun} (package {C:cli}): the summary line, and the
    exit codes of {C:--scratch-dir}.
  }
}

Each test of a fault fails when the check that it covers is removed. In
{C:make mutate}, the tests find each change of {C:diffextract.go}, except 28
in {C bc=/._:tools/mutate/accepted.txt}: the code of the owner and of device nodes,
which needs root, the code for Windows, and one change that cannot change
the result. The operational test {C:TestDiffExtractOfTheBinary} runs the built binary on a
created archive, and finds no difference and no scratch directory after the
run.

{section 2 bid=encryption-existing-archive-encrypt: Encryption of an existing archive ({C:--encrypt-archive})}

{C:--encrypt-archive} encrypts an archive that was made without {C:-e}. The
result is the same as a create with {C:-e}: a data key, wrapped for a
passphrase, a crypto header, sealed blobs and keyed digests ({ref t=#cryptography:§6}). The
members, their metadata and their compressed content do not change.

{code:
eictar --encrypt-archive -f home.ect
eictar --encrypt-archive --encrypt-index --passphrase-file pass -f home.ect
}

{b:Create with {C:-e} is the safe way.} An archive that is encrypted later was
on disk as plaintext. The rename replaces the file, but the blocks of the
old contents are free space until something writes over them. Copies,
snapshots and backups of the old contents also stay readable.
{C:--encrypt-archive} cannot remove any of them. Thus the program prints a
reminder on standard error after each successful run:

{code:
eictar: home.ect: encrypted. Copies of its old unencrypted contents can remain in free disk space, snapshots and backups; to never write plaintext to disk, create with -e
}

With {C:-q}, there is no summary, and the reminder starts at "copies":

{code:
eictar: home.ect: copies of its old unencrypted contents can remain in free disk space, snapshots and backups; to never write plaintext to disk, create with -e
}

The reminder starts with "encrypted" so that the reader does not take it
for an error: the file itself is encrypted. The risk is in the copies of
its old contents. The manual page says the same. {ref t=#change-passphrase:§9.7} gives the same limit for an old
passphrase.

{b:The name.} This is one operation, with no short letter. It needs no {C:-e},
because the operation itself says "encrypt". {C:--compact -e} was the other
choice. It was not used, because two options would then make one atomic
operation, and {C:-e} would mean a different thing with {C:-c} and with
{C:--compact}. With {C:--encrypt-archive}, {C:-e} is a usage error that says so.

{b:An archive that is already encrypted.} The reader checks the flag of the
file header ({C:RequirePlaintext}), before it reads the crypto header and
before it asks for a passphrase. The run stops with exit status 2:

{code:
eictar: home.ect: the archive is already encrypted; to change its passphrase, use --change-passphrase
}

{C:--encrypt-archive} does not give an encrypted archive a new data key. A
change of the data key is a different operation, and it is not part of this
one.

{b:The options.} The passphrase options, the {C:--kdf-*} options and
{C:--encrypt-index} have the meaning that they have for create ({ref t=#encryption:§10.3}). Thus
the program asks for the passphrase two times, and {C:--passphrase-file} and
{C:--passphrase-env} give it without a prompt. The keys {C:kdf-*} and
{C:encrypt-index} of a configuration apply too ({ref t=#what-configuration-can-set:§11.1}). {C:-j} and
{C:--memory-limit} set the workers of the decode and their memory, as for
{C:--verify}. {C:--progress} and {C:-v} work as for compact. {C:-q} hides the
summary line. Each other option of create, and any positional argument, is a
usage error.

{b:The steps.} {C:CompactArchive} does the work, with {C:CompactConfig.Encrypt}
set. Thus the lock, the temporary file, the mode and the owner, the rename
and the sync of the directory are those of compact ({ref t=#compact:§9.3}).

{list t=1:
  {item:
    Open the archive with {C:RequirePlaintext}, and take the writer's lock.
  }
  {item:
    Check the KDF parameters, and then this machine ({ref t=#3-argon2id-default:§A.3}). Then ask for the
    passphrase. Make a new random data key, and derive the keys with the
    uuid of the archive, with the code of create ({C:newWrappedKeys}). The uuid
    and the creation time do not change.
  }
  {item:
    Write the file header, with the encrypted flag and the length of the
    new crypto header, and then the crypto header.
  }
  {item:
    Write each dictionary that a kept member uses. {C:loadDict} checks its
    plain digest. {C:storeDict} seals it with a new salt and gives it a keyed
    digest. It keeps its id and its generation.
  }
  {item:
    Pass A and pass B (below) write the members.
  }
  {item:
    Write the index, with the next generation and the keyed index digest.
    With {C:--encrypt-index}, the index is sealed ({ref t=#authenticating-index:§6.4}).
  }
  {item:
    Rename the new file over the archive, as compact does.
  }
}

A crash or an error before the rename leaves the original archive. The
temporary file is removed.

{b:Pass A} decodes each kept member that holds its own content, the empty
ones too, with the parallel decode of {C:--verify}. It runs before the
catalog drops its unused entries, because that gives the codecs new
positions. For each member it
calculates two digests, and keeps them:

{list t=*:
  {item:
    the keyed digest of the decoded content, with {C:contentK} ({ref t=#crypto-header-cbor-plaintext:§6.2}). This
    becomes the new digest of the member.
  }
  {item:
    the plain digest of the blob as it is on disk.
  }
}

The decode also compares the old digest with the content. If they are not
equal, the run stops with exit status 3, and the original stays. Thus
damage in a blob cannot become a sealed blob with a valid tag.

{b:Pass B} reads each blob again, in the order of the file, and seals each
chunk as it is, under a new member salt and thus a new key ({ref t=#aead-layer:§6.3}). The
compressed bytes do not change, so the member keeps its codec and its
catalog entry. Each chunk increases by the 16 bytes of its tag, and the
index gets the new lengths. Pass B also calculates the plain digest of the
bytes that it seals. If it is not equal to the digest from pass A, the run
stops with exit status 3. Thus no byte gets a seal that pass A did not
check.

Two passes keep the memory small. A sealed member is not held until the
members before it are written, so a large file costs only its chunk. The
cost is a second read of each blob, which is usually in the page cache.

{b:The members without a blob.} A member that shares the content of another
member ({ref t=#identical-content-m11:§4.3}) gets the new keyed digest of the member that holds its
content. Each other member with a digest gets one from pass A. If a member
has a digest and no content that gives a keyed one, the run stops: no plain
digest goes into the encrypted archive.

{b:Dead space and tombstones.} The run drops the dead space and the
tombstones, as compact does. A tombstone stays only when a live member needs
its blob, and then its blob is sealed too. Thus no blob of the old file goes
into the new file without a seal.

{b:What is not done.} The run does not compress again. For a new codec,
the user runs {C:--compact --recompress} before or after it. A {C:--verify}
after the run checks the new archive with the new keys.

{b:Tests.}

{list t=*:
  {item:
    {C:TestEncryptArchive}: a tree with each member type, content that does not
    compress, a file with holes, shared content, a hardlink and a
    shared blob that need a tombstone, and a dictionary. After the run, each
    member has the same id and metadata, a new keyed digest, and chunks 16
    bytes longer. The dictionary is sealed. The uuid and the creation time
    stay. {C:--verify} passes. The state of the archive and the extracted tree
    are the same as before. No chunk, dictionary or index of the plaintext
    archive, and no member path, occurs in the encrypted file.
  }
  {item:
    {C:TestEncryptArchiveWithoutEncryptIndex}: the index is not sealed.
  }
  {item:
    {C:TestEncryptArchiveRefusesAnEncryptedArchive}: {C:ErrAlreadyEncrypted}, no
    passphrase asked, and no change.
  }
  {item:
    {C:TestEncryptArchiveRefusals}: an empty passphrase, bad or unaffordable
    parameters, and a failed prompt. {C:TestEncryptArchiveDefaultParameters}.
  }
  {item:
    {C:TestEncryptArchiveOfADamagedArchive}: a changed byte in a member or in a
    dictionary is damage, and the archive does not change.
  }
  {item:
    {C:TestEncryptArchiveSealsOnlyCheckedBytes}: a blob that changes between
    the passes stops the run.
  }
  {item:
    {C:TestEncryptArchiveDoesNotDependOnWorkers} and
    {C:TestEncryptArchiveProgress}.
  }
  {item:
    {C:TestEncryptArchiveOfACatalogThatShrinks}: when no kept member uses the
    first codec, each codec gets a new position. Pass A decodes with the
    positions of the archive that it reads. The stress tester found this
    fault.
  }
  {item:
    The fault and power-loss tests (testing.md §1) run "encryption of an
    archive", with and without a dictionary. After each fault, the archive is
    the plaintext one or the encrypted one, and no temporary file stays.
  }
  {item:
    In {C:cli}: {C:TestEncryptArchiveThroughRun} (the summary, the reminder with
    and without {C:-q}, exit 2 for an encrypted archive),
    {C:TestEncryptArchiveRefusals}, {C:TestEncryptArchiveOptions} and
    {C:TestConfigScopeOfEncryptArchive}.
  }
  {item:
    The operational test {C:TestEncryptArchiveOfTheBinary} and the stress tester
    (testing.md §4) run the built binary.
  }
}

{section 1 nc bid=part-iii: Part III — Interface}

{section 1 bid=command-line-interface: Command line interface}

The command line uses the classic UNIX style. An option selects the
operation, not a subcommand, so a command line has the same shape as a {C:tar}
command line. Every short option has a GNU-style long form, and short options
can be combined.

{code:
eictar -c -f ARCHIVE [options] PATH...        # create
eictar -cf ARCHIVE [options] PATH...          # same, combined
eictar -tvf ARCHIVE [PATTERN...]              # list, verbose
eictar -xf ARCHIVE [-d DIR] [PATTERN...]      # extract
eictar -rf ARCHIVE [options] PATH...          # append
eictar --delete -f ARCHIVE PATTERN...
}

{section 2 bid=operation-selection: Operation selection}

A command line must have exactly one operation option. Two operations are a
usage error. No operation is also a usage error, with one exception. If you
run {C:eictar} with no arguments at all, it prints the full help on stdout and
exits with 0. A person who types the name of an unknown program wants to know
what it does, and that is not a mistake. When there is at least one argument,
a missing or doubled operation gets a short error on stderr and exit 2.

{stable col=5 header sb b==:
| Short | Long | Operation | Positional arguments | Built |
| {C:-c} | {C:--create} | Create a new archive. Replace an existing one when the new one is complete ({ref t=#changes-checks:§9}). | paths to archive | M2 |
| {C:-r} | {C:--append} | Append to an existing archive. A path that is already there is replaced ({ref t=#one-live-member-each:§9.2}). | paths to archive | M6 |
| {C:-t} | {C:--list} | List the members from the index | patterns (default: all) | M2 |
| {C:-x} | {C:--extract} | Extract members | patterns (default: all) | M2 |
| {C:-u} | {C:--update} | Append only the paths whose copy in the archive is out of date ({ref t=#update-behavior-u:§10.5}) | paths | M6 |
|  | {C:--delete} | Tombstone the matching members | patterns | M6 |
|  | {C:--compact} | Write the archive again without the tombstoned blobs | none | M6 |
|  | {C:--verify} | Check integrity without extraction | patterns (default: all) | M6 |
|  | {C:--diff} | Compare the members with the tree on disk, and change nothing ({ref t=#compare-disk-diff:§9.8}) | patterns (default: all) | v1.0.6 |
|  | {C:--diff-extract} | Do what {C:--diff} does, and also extract each member into a scratch directory and compare the copy with the disk byte by byte ({ref t=#compare-extraction-diff-extract:§9.10}) | patterns (default: all) | v1.1.4 |
|  | {C:--repair} | Restore the last complete generation after an interrupted write ({ref t=#repair:§9.5}) | none | M6 |
|  | {C:--info} | Show the archive header, the codecs, the dictionaries, the counts, the shared content and the dead space | none | M6 |
|  | {C:--change-passphrase} | Seal the data key under a new passphrase, and write the archive again without its dead space ({ref t=#change-passphrase:§9.7}) | none | M9 |
|  | {C:--encrypt-archive} | Encrypt an archive that was made without {C:-e}, and write it again without its dead space ({ref t=#encryption-existing-archive-encrypt:§9.11}) | none | v1.1.4 |
|  | {C:--list-codecs} | Show each codec with its parameters, defaults and ranges | none | M2 |
}

All operations are built. An option that is not built yet exits with 70 and
names its milestone ({ref t=#options-that-this-build:§10.6}).

Most operations without a short letter do not exist in {C:tar}. Thus there is
no habit to keep, and no letter to spend. {C:--delete} and {C:--diff} have the
GNU tar spelling on purpose. In GNU tar, {C:--diff} is {C:-d}, which is
{C:--destination} here ({ref t=#where-extraction-writes:§7.1}).

There is no {C:--replace} operation. {C:-r} replaces a path that is already in the
archive ({ref t=#one-live-member-each:§9.2}).

{C:-f} is necessary for every operation except {C:--list-codecs}, and
{C:--show-config} needs none. There is no default archive and no tape device.
The index is at the end of the file, so the file must be seekable, and a pipe
is not possible ({ref t=#non-goals-v1:§1.2}).

{section 2 bid=compression-selection: Compression selection}

{code:
-Z, --compress SPEC    # SPEC := "none" | "NAME[:k[=v][,k[=v]]...]"
-z, --gzip             # the same as --compress gzip
-J, --xz               # the same as --compress xz
    --zstd             # the same as --compress zstd (the default)
}

{C:-z} and {C:-J} have their {C:tar} meanings. {C:-Z} does not. In {C:tar}, {C:-Z} selects
{C:compress(1)}, which is obsolete. This tool does not support it, so the
letter selects the general form here. {C:-j} is {b:not} bzip2 (see {ref t=#general-options:§10.4}).

Examples:

{code:
--compress zstd:level=19,long=27
--compress zstd:level=19,long,train
--compress xz:preset=6
--compress gzip:level=9
--compress none
}

{b:A key without a value is the key's "on" form} (M10). {C:long} is
{C:long=27}, the window of {C:zstd --long}, and {C:train} is {C:train=auto}: the
writer measures the dictionary size on the files ({ref t=#dictionaries-m10:§4.2}). {C:k=on} means the
same as {C:k}, and {C:k=off} turns the key off. Thus a configuration can say
{C:train = on}, and a command line can say {C:train=off} against it. A key with no "on" form, such as {C:level}, needs a
value.

{stable col=4 header sb b==:
| zstd key | Values | Default | Bare |
| {C:level} | 1..22 | 12 | needs a value |
| {C:long} | {C:off}, or 10..30: the window is 2^long bytes | {C:off} | 27 |
| {C:train} | {C:off}, {C:auto}, or a dictionary size, 4 KiB..1 MiB | {C:off} | {C:auto} |
}

{b:The zstd library has four speeds, not 22 levels.} {C:klauspost/compress}
maps each level of the zstd command line to one of its speeds:

{list t=*:
  {item:
    1 and 2: fastest
  }
  {item:
    3 to 5: default
  }
  {item:
    6 to 9: better
  }
  {item:
    10 to 22: best
  }
}

Thus {C:level=12} and {C:level=19} give the same archive. A measurement on 126 MB of
Go sources and module files, with 32 CPUs, gave these results:

{stable col=5 header sb b==:
| Level | Speed of the library | Size | Create | Extract |
| 1 | fastest | 97.5 MB | 0.21 s | 0.10 s |
| 3 | default | 94.7 MB | 0.21 s | 0.11 s |
| 7 | better | 92.4 MB | 0.27 s | 0.10 s |
| 12 or 19 | best | 90.4 MB | 0.72 s | 0.09 s |
}

{b:The default level is 12}, the best speed. It is approximately 5% smaller
than level 3, and a create takes approximately 3.4 times as long. The speed
of extraction does not change. The default is 12, not 19, so that it stays a sensible level if the
library gets finer levels. Appendix B compares the four speeds with the 22 levels of the C
library, and gives the work that finer levels need.

{b:A window larger than the chunk has no effect.} The chunks are
independent ({ref t=#member-payloads-chunks-compression:§4}), so no match reaches back past the start of its chunk.
{C:long=27} needs {C:--chunk-size 128MiB} to have any use. The xz dictionary is
the same: a preset changes only the dictionary size (below), so at the
default chunk size of 4 MiB, presets 3 to 9 compress the same. When a given
{C:long} or {C:preset} asks for more than a chunk, the program prints a warning,
and continues. A codec's default is not a choice of the user, so plain {C:xz}
does not warn.

On 78 MB of Go module sources, {C:zstd:level=12} made 43.85 MB in 0.57 s, and
{C:xz:preset=6} and {C:xz:preset=9} both made 43.98 MB in 1.95 s. The library of
xz is pure Go, without the binary-tree match finder and the slow search
for the best matches that make the C {C:xz} strong at high presets.

A registry checks the name and the keys of each codec. A bad spec is a usage
error (exit 2), and the program reports it before it touches the archive
file. The index catalog stores the values in effect for each codec. {C:eictar
--list-codecs} shows every codec with its keys, defaults and ranges.

{stable col=4 header sb b==:
| Codec | Library | Notes | Built |
| {C:zstd} | {C:klauspost/compress/zstd} | default. {C:level} 1..22 (default 12), {C:long} 10..30, {C:train} ({ref t=#dictionaries-m10:§4.2}, M10) | M2 |
| {C:none} | — | stored | M2 |
| {C:gzip}, {C:flate} | {C:klauspost/compress} | {C:level} 1..9, default 6. {C:gzip} is {C:flate} in a gzip member for each chunk. | M7 |
| {C:xz} | {C:ulikunitz/xz} | {C:preset} 0..9, default 6. Raw LZMA2, slower than the C implementation. | M7 |
| {C:s2} | {C:klauspost/compress/s2} | very fast. {C:mode=fast}, {C:better} (default) or {C:best} | M7 |
}

{b:Every codec must compress and decompress.} Thus there is no bzip2 codec
for decompression only: no writer of eictar makes bzip2 members.

{b:The {C:xz} codec stores raw LZMA2, without the {C:.xz} container.} The
container declares the dictionary size, and the library honors it. Thus a
crafted chunk can make the reader allocate 4 GiB. A raw LZMA2 stream has no
such field. The reader uses the chunk size as the dictionary, and that is
always enough, because chunks are independent.

The container also has a CRC-64, which only repeats what the BLAKE3 digest
checks. {C:doc/format.md} gives the exact form of the stream.

{b:Every {C:xz} preset uses the hash-chain match finder.} The C {C:xz} uses a
binary tree from preset 4. The binary tree of {C:ulikunitz/xz} takes quadratic
time on repetitive input. On 128 KiB of one repeated byte, it takes 10 s. On
a 4 MiB chunk of zeroes, it takes hours. Thus a preset changes only the
dictionary size, which is capped at the chunk size.

{b:{C:s2} and {C:flate} have no check of their own.} A changed byte can decode to
wrong content of the right length. The BLAKE3 digest of the member finds it
({ref t=#index:§5}). The other codecs also find it themselves.

The codec is a field of each member. Thus an archive can contain different
codecs from different append operations. All members of one operation use
the selected codec, as constraint 2 of the problem statement requires.

{section 2 bid=encryption: Encryption}

{code:
-e, --encrypt                # turn encryption on; prompts for a passphrase
    --passphrase-file FILE   # the first line of FILE
    --passphrase-env VAR     # the value of VAR (visible in /proc; not recommended)
    --encrypt-index          # also seal the index (default: off)
    --kdf-time N             # Argon2id passes, 1..64 (default 3)
    --kdf-memory KiB         # Argon2id memory in KiB, up to 4194304 (default 262144)
    --kdf-threads N          # Argon2id threads (default 4)
    --new-passphrase-file FILE   # with --change-passphrase: the new passphrase
    --new-passphrase-env VAR     # with --change-passphrase: the new passphrase
}

You select encryption when you create an archive, and it applies to the whole
archive. An append to an encrypted archive needs the same passphrase. This
agrees with the problem statement: one secret key for all members.

{C:--encrypt} applies to create only. {C:--encrypt-index} applies to create and
to {C:--encrypt-archive} ({ref t=#encryption-existing-archive-encrypt:§9.11}). The {C:--kdf-*} options apply to create,
{C:--change-passphrase} and {C:--encrypt-archive}. On any other operation they
are a usage error, because the header of an existing archive fixes its
encryption. The {C:--kdf-*} limits are the same limits that a reader applies
({ref t=#crypto-header-cbor-plaintext:§6.2}).

The program reads the passphrase from the terminal with echo off. {b:On
create, and for the new passphrase of a change, it asks two times}, because
a mistyped passphrase cannot be recovered. No part of the program can find
out later what you meant. The program asks only when an archive needs a
passphrase. Thus a plaintext
archive never prompts, and a usage error never asks for a secret. The prompt
goes to stderr, so it cannot go into a redirected listing or into extracted
content.

{b:The end of the input stops the prompt with an error.} That is Ctrl-D
at the start of the line, or a terminal that closes, such as an SSH session
that ends. After a hangup, each read of the terminal gives 0 bytes and no
error. {C:term.ReadPassword} of {C:golang.org/x/term} then reads again for ever,
at the full speed of a CPU. Thus on Unix the program reads the line itself
({C:password_unix.go}), and stops at a read of 0 bytes.

{C:--passphrase-env} prints a warning. Any process that can read this
process's environment can see the value.

{b:A passphrase source means "this archive is encrypted".} If you give
{C:--passphrase-file} or {C:--passphrase-env} and the archive is plaintext, the
program refuses the archive with exit 3. This is how it catches an archive
whose encryption was removed ({ref t=#security-considerations:§14}).

{section 2 bid=general-options: General options}

{stable col=4 header sb b==:
| Short | Long | Meaning | Built |
| {C:-f} | {C:--file ARCHIVE} | the archive to use (necessary). A name with no extension gets {C:.ect} ({ref t=#archive-name:§10.12}). | M1 |
| {C:-d} | {C:--destination DIR} | extract into DIR, and create DIR if it does not exist. Extraction only. | M2 |
| {C:-C} | {C:--directory DIR} | change to DIR first. DIR must exist. More than one {C:-C} is refused (open question 2, {ref t=#open-questions:§1.3}). | M2 |
| {C:-v} | {C:--verbose} | list members during the operation. Repeat for more detail. With {C:-t}, {C:-v} gives the long listing and {C:-vv} adds more ({ref t=#listing-format:§10.9}). With {C:-n}, a word before each path tells what happens to it ({ref t=#dry-run-n:§9.9}). | M2 |
| {C:-q} | {C:--quiet} | errors only | M2 |
| {C:-n} | {C:--dry-run} | show the paths that the operation would write or delete, and change nothing ({ref t=#dry-run-n:§9.9}). Create, append, update, extract and delete. | v1.0.6 |
|  | {C:--progress} | a progress meter on stderr when stderr is a terminal ({ref t=#progress-meter:§10.10}) | M7 |
| {C:-j} | {C:--workers N} | number of workers, 1..1024. Default three quarters of {C:GOMAXPROCS}, at least 1 ({ref t=#concurrency-model:§8}). | M3 |
|  | {C:--chunk-size SIZE} | plaintext chunk size, 512 B..256 MiB. Default 4 MiB. | M2 |
|  | {C:--memory-limit SIZE} | the memory budget. Default in {ref t=#memory-budget:§8.1}. | M3 |
|  | {C:--spill-threshold SIZE} | the size at which a member's spool moves to disk. Default 32 MiB. | M3 |
| {C:-T} | {C:--files-from FILE} | read the paths from FILE, or from stdin if FILE is {C:-} | M2 |
|  | {C:--exclude GLOB} | can be repeated. Applies to create, append, update, list, extract and diff. An excluded directory is not entered. | M5 |
| {C:-X} | {C:--exclude-from FILE} | one glob on each line. Blank lines are ignored. There is no comment syntax, because a file name can start with {C:#}. | M5 |
| {C:-R} | {C:--regex RE} | can be repeated. Keep only the paths that match a regular expression ({ref t=#patterns:§10.11}). Create, append, update, list, extract, verify, delete and diff. | after M9 |
|  | {C:--exclude-regex RE} | can be repeated. Leave out the paths that match. An excluded directory is not entered, and on a read it takes its contents ({ref t=#patterns:§10.11}). Create, append, update, list, extract and diff. | after M9 |
| {C:-h} | {C:--dereference} | follow links and store what they point to. With {C:--diff} and {C:--diff-extract}, compare what they point to. Create, append, update, {C:--diff} and {C:--diff-extract}. | M2 |
|  | {C:--no-dedup} | store each copy of the same content in full ({ref t=#identical-content-m11:§4.3}). Create, append and update. | M11 |
