|  | {C:--preserve-owner} | restore the owner ({ref t=#metadata-extraction:§7.7}). Extract only. Without root, exit 2. | M5 |
|  | {C:--preserve-devices} | create device nodes. Extract only. Without root, exit 2. | M5 |
|  | {C:--no-xattrs}, {C:--no-acls}, {C:--no-owner} | on create, append and update, do not record that metadata. On extract, do not apply it. On {C:--diff} and {C:--diff-extract}, do not compare it. | M5 |
| {C:-k} | {C:--keep-existing} | never overwrite an existing file on extract | M2 |
|  | {C:--overwrite} | overwrite (the default) | M2 |
|  | {C:--newer-only} | overwrite only when the member is newer | M2 |
| {C:-O} | {C:--to-stdout} | write the extracted content to stdout | M2 |
|  | {C:--strip-components N} | with {C:-x}, {C:--diff} and {C:--diff-extract}, remove the first N components of each path ({ref t=#strip-components-strip-components:§10.14}). Not with {C:-O}. | v1.0.7 |
|  | {C:--keep-going} | continue after an error on one member, and exit with 1. On create, append and update, an entry that the walk cannot read is such an error: a directory that cannot be read is archived empty, and a file that went away is left out. macOS needs read permission to list the extended attributes of a directory, so there such a directory is left out too, and counts as a second failure. A path given on the command line that cannot be read still stops the run. | M2 |
|  | {C:--long} | the long listing, the same as {C:-tv} ({ref t=#listing-format:§10.9}) | M2 |
