}

{b:Two differences from {C:tar} are intentional:}

{list t=*:
  {item:
    {C:-j} is the {b:number of workers}, not bzip2. Only {C:--compress}, {C:-z} and
    {C:-J} select compression, so the letter is free. Outside {C:tar}, {C:-j N} for
    parallel work is the stronger habit ({C:make}, {C:xz}, {C:zstd}). There is no
    bzip2 codec ({ref t=#compression-selection:§10.2}).
  }
  {item:
    {C:-p} is {C:--preserve-permissions}, as in {C:tar}, but there is no {C:-P} or
    {C:--absolute-names}. Extraction always refuses absolute paths and {C:..}
    ({ref t=#how-paths-are-stored:§7.3}). No option turns that off.
  }
}

{section 2 bid=update-behavior-u: Update behavior ({C:-u})}

{C:-u} decides, for each named path, if the copy in the archive is out of date.
{C:--update-mode} selects the test:

{stable col=3 header sb b== i=3% w=94%:
| Mode | Test | Cost |
| {C:newer} (default) | the source {C:mtime} is {b:greater} than the member's {C:mtime} | {C:stat} only |
| {C:different} | the source size is different, {b:or} the {C:mtime} is different in either direction | {C:stat} only |
| {C:digest} | the source BLAKE3-256 is different from the member's {C:digest} | a full read of each candidate |
}

The program compares the timestamps as int64 nanoseconds from the index.
Thus the comparison is exact, with no problem of time zones or resolution on
our side.

{b:{C:newer} is a heuristic, with known faults.} It is the default because a
{C:tar} user expects it from {C:-u}, not because it is correct:

{list t=*:
  {item:
    A file restored from a backup keeps its original mtime. Its content is
    different from the archived copy, but it is not {i:newer}. Thus {C:newer} skips
    it, and the archive keeps old content without a message. {C:different} finds
    this case. The man page recommends {C:different} for backups with {C:-u}.
  }
  {item:
    {C:touch} with no change of content causes a compression that is not
    necessary.
  }
  {item:
    A filesystem with coarse timestamps, or a clock set backward, causes
    missed changes.
  }
}

{C:digest} compares the content of the file with the content of the member.
For a member with holes, the bytes at the member's data regions must give
its digest, and the rest of the file must be zero. The file's own regions do
not count: a filesystem can report other regions for the same content.
Thus such a file is not archived again (testing.md §4).

{C:digest} never compresses without need and never misses a change. It reads
each candidate in full. A sequential read costs much less than compression,
encryption and a write. Thus on a large tree with few changes, {C:digest} is
often the {i:fastest} mode and also the exact one. The index already stores a
digest of the content of each member ({ref t=#index:§5}), so this mode needs no format
change.

In every mode, the program skips a path that fails the test. It does not
read it again, it does not compress it again, and it does not change the
existing member.

A second name of a hardlink pair has no content of its own. The test uses the
member that holds the content. Otherwise, each {C:-u} stores that content again
in full.

{section 2 bid=options-that-this-build: Options that this build does not support yet}

The program refuses an option that it can parse but cannot apply yet. It
exits with 70 and names the milestone, as it does for an operation that is
not built. This build has every option. The rule stays for the next
option that is not, and a unit test keeps the rule working.

If the program accepts such an option without a message, the result is
wrong and no warning shows it. For example, an {C:--exclude} that excludes
nothing gives a wrong archive. The archive contains the files that the user
wanted to leave out. {ref t=#environment-variables:§11.2} applies the same rule to environment variables.

The program reports a real usage error first. A user who made a typing
mistake fixes the command line before learning that a feature is missing.
The check uses what the user typed, not the value in effect. Thus a default
value never causes this error.

{section 2 bid=exit-codes: Exit codes}

{stable col=2 header sb b== i=3% w=94%:
| Code | Meaning |
| 0 | success |
| 1 | the operation completed, but one or more members failed under {C:--keep-going}. Also, {C:--diff} or {C:--diff-extract} found a difference between the archive and the disk ({ref t=#compare-disk-diff:§9.8}, {ref t=#compare-extraction-diff-extract:§9.10}). Also, {C:--strip-components} gave two members the same path, and one was left out ({ref t=#strip-components-strip-components:§10.14}). |
| 2 | usage error: an unknown option, no operation, two operations, no {C:-f}, a bad compression spec. Also a pattern that matches no member, for {C:-t}, {C:-x}, {C:--verify}, {C:--diff} and {C:--delete}, an {C:-R} that matches no path or member, a regular expression that does not compile, a path that is already in the archive under {C:--on-conflict=error}, and a scratch directory of {C:--diff-extract} in the compared tree. Nothing is changed or extracted. |
| 3 | the archive failed a check. Causes: corrupt data, an unsafe member path, a wrong passphrase or a failed tag. Also a plaintext archive when a passphrase source was given, and an archive path through a link that the program does not follow ({ref t=#changes-checks:§9}). |
| 4 | any other failure, usually an I/O error on the archive or the filesystem |
| 70 | an option that is not built yet ({C:EX_SOFTWARE}). The message names the milestone. No option of this build gives it. |
}

Code 3 is separate from code 4 so that a script can tell "this archive is
damaged" from "the disk is full". Backup tools need this difference.

{section 2 bid=parsing: Parsing}

Combined options ({C:-cvf archive}) and a long form for every short option
exclude the {C:flag} package of the standard library, which supports neither.
The program uses {C:github.com/spf13/pflag}, a pure-Go parser with GNU-style
long options and combined short options. {C:-cf ARCHIVE}, {C:-cvvf ARCHIVE} and
{C:-cf=ARCHIVE} all parse correctly, and a test fixes each form.

pflag has one trap that fails without an error. You must give a short option
when you register the option ({C:BoolVarP}). If you set {C:Flag.Shorthand} later,
pflag registers nothing, and the short option does not exist.

Option parsing has its own unit tests (testing.md §1). They cover every combined
form, the one-operation rule and each path to exit 2. The old {C:tar} form
without the leading dash ({C:eictar cf archive.ect path}) is {b:not}
supported. It makes the first positional argument unclear, and modern {C:tar}
keeps it only for compatibility.

{C:completions/eictar.bash} gives tab completion in bash. It is a static
script, not output of the binary, so a completion runs no part of eictar
and reads no archive. It completes the options, the values of options with
a fixed set of values (codecs, {C:--update-mode}, {C:--on-conflict}),
directories for {C:-C} and {C:-d}, directories and {C:.ect} files for {C:-f}, and
file names. It needs bash 3.2, the version on macOS, and no other package.
Two tests keep it correct (testing.md §1). One compares its lists with the
parser, the codecs and the modes. The other runs it in bash.

{section 2 bid=listing-format: Listing format}

{C:-t} has three levels of detail, as in {C:tar}. Plain {C:-t} prints one path on
each line. {C:-tv} (or {C:--long}) prints the long listing. {C:-tvv} adds more
columns and a line of totals.

{code:
$ eictar -tvf a.ect
drwxrwxr-x  psm/psm       0       -      -  -             2026-09-24 17:59:19  d
-rw-rw-r--  psm/psm  200000  200000   0.0%  zstd:level=3  2026-09-24 17:59:19  d/random.bin
-rw-rw-r--  psm/psm  240001    1432  99.4%  zstd:level=3  2026-09-24 17:59:19  d/text.txt
}

The columns of the long listing are:

{list t=1:
  {item:
    The type and the mode, as {C:ls} shows them.
  }
  {item:
    The owner and the group, or {C:-} if the archive has no owner ({C:--no-owner}).
  }
  {item:
    The size. For a device, it is {C:major,minor}.
  }
  {item:
    The stored size: the bytes of the blob in the archive.
  }
  {item:
    The saved percentage: {C:(size - stored) / size}, as {C:unzip -v} and
    {C:gzip -l} show it. It can be less than zero, for example for a small file
    in an encrypted archive.
  }
  {item:
    The codec, in the form that {C:--compress} takes.
  }
  {item:
    The modification time.
  }
  {item:
    The path, with {C: -> target} for a symbolic link and {C: link to path} for
    a hardlink. A reference to an owner ({ref t=#identical-content-m11:§4.3}) ends with {C: same as path}.
    When the owner is a tombstone, it ends with {C: same as its earlier
    version} if the owner had the same path, and with {C: same as deleted path}
    if not.
  }
}

A member that has no blob shows {C:-} in columns 4 to 6. A hardlink has no blob
of its own, because it shares the blob of its target. A reference shares the
blob of its owner in the same way. For a sparse file, the stored size and the
percentage use the data regions, not the holes. In an encrypted archive, the
stored size includes the 16-byte tag of each chunk.

{C:-tvv} adds three columns before the time. They are the number of chunks,
{C:sealed} for an encrypted member, and the first 16 hex digits of the BLAKE3
digest. A reference shows the digest of its content too, so that equal
content has equal digests in the listing. After the members, {C:-tvv} prints
one line of totals, for the listed members and for the archive:

{code:
7 listed, 4 files, 326688 bytes stored in 27341 (91.6% saved), 2 same as another (54670 bytes not stored); generation 1, 0 tombstoned
}

The part about references is there only when a listed member is one.
{C:--info} gives the same two numbers for the whole archive, in the line
{C:shared content}.

{C:--json} always gives every field, including {C:stored_size}, {C:chunks},
{C:encrypted}, the full {C:digest}, and {C:codec} as an object with a name and the
settings. {C:encrypted} is true when the data of the member is sealed. An
empty file has no data, so it is false. A reference has {C:same_as}, the path
of its owner, and {C:same_as_deleted} when the owner is a tombstone. A program
must not have to ask for detail.

{section 2 bid=progress-meter: Progress meter}

{C:--progress} draws one status line on stderr, and redraws it five times a
second. It draws only when stderr is a terminal. Otherwise it writes nothing,
so that a log does not fill with carriage returns. {C:-q} turns it off.

{code:
1.2 GiB / 3.4 GiB  35%  120.4 MiB/s  0:18 left      # -x, --verify, --compact, --diff
1.2 GiB  1234 members  120.4 MiB/s                  # -c, -r, -u
}

Extraction, {C:--verify}, {C:--compact} and {C:--diff} know their total from the
index, so they show the percentage and the time left. A create walks the tree while it
works, so it has no total, and it shows the members instead.

The meter counts plaintext bytes: what is read from the source files, or
what is decoded. A copying compact counts the stored bytes that it copies.

A {C:-v} line or a warning clears the status line first, and the meter draws
it again after. The meter shows nothing until the first byte. Before that,
the program can ask for a passphrase on the same terminal, and a redraw
writes over the prompt. The clock starts at the first byte too, so that the
time to type the passphrase does not lower the rate.

{section 2 bid=patterns: Patterns}

{C:-t}, {C:-x}, {C:--verify}, {C:--diff}, {C:--delete} and {C:--exclude} take patterns. A
pattern matches a member path in any of these cases:

{list t=*:
  {item:
    It is the path.
  }
  {item:
    It names a directory that the path is under. Thus {C:src} takes
    {C:src/main.go}.
  }
  {item:
    It is a glob that matches the whole path, as {C:path.Match} does. {C:*} does not
    match a {C:/}.
  }
  {item:
    It has no {C:/}, and it matches one name in the path, at any depth. Thus
    {C:*.go} takes {C:src/main.go}, and {C:cache} takes {C:a/cache} and everything
    below it.
  }
}

A directory that a pattern names, or that a pattern with no {C:/} matches by
name, takes its contents, at any depth. Without this rule, {C:--delete cache}
deletes the directory {C:a/cache} but not its members, and {C:--exclude cache}
hides {C:a/cache} but not its contents (testing.md §4).

A glob with a {C:/} matches the whole path only. Thus {C:src/sub*} selects the
directory {C:src/sub}, but not {C:src/sub/main.go}. To select the contents, name
the directory. {C:-n} shows what a pattern selects ({ref t=#dry-run-n:§9.9}).

{b:An excluded directory always takes its contents.} The walk of create,
append, update and diff does not enter an excluded directory. On a read, an
{C:--exclude} pattern also excludes each member under a directory that it
matches, so that a list of an archive agrees with the walk that made it.
Without this rule, {C:-t --exclude 'src/sub*'} lists the contents of
{C:src/sub}, and {C:--diff} with the same option reports them as not on disk.

{b:Regular expressions.} {C:-R RE} ({C:--regex}) and {C:--exclude-regex RE} use
the RE2 syntax of Go's {C:regexp} package. The time of a match is linear in the
length of the path, so no expression can make the program hang. An
expression that does not compile is a usage error (exit 2).

{list t=*:
  {item:
    An expression must match the {b:whole} stored path. The program puts it
    in {C:^(?:} and {C:)$}. Thus {C:dir/a1\\.txt} does not match {C:olddir/a1.txt.bak}.
  }
  {item:
    The expression sees the {b:stored path}: relative, with {C:/} between the
    names, on every platform. It does not see the path on the disk. Thus
    {C:-C ~ -R 'Documents/.*\\.pdf' Documents} gives the same result on every
    machine.
  }
  {item:
    In an expression, {C:.} is any character. Write {C:\\.} for a dot.
  }
  {item:
    A byte of a path that is not valid UTF-8 counts as one character, which
    {C:.} matches. An expression cannot hold such a byte.
  }
}

{C:-R} {b:keeps only} the paths that match one of its expressions. On create,
append and update, it filters what the walk finds below the path arguments.
The walk enters every directory, because a match can be deeper down. A
directory is stored only if it matches, and extraction creates the parent
directories that the archive does not hold. On {C:-t}, {C:-x}, {C:--verify} and
{C:--delete}, it filters the members that the patterns select, or all the
members when there are no patterns. On {C:--diff}, it filters the members and
the walk ({ref t=#compare-disk-diff:§9.8}). {C:--delete} needs a pattern or an {C:-R}. {C:-n} shows what
the patterns and {C:-R} select, and changes nothing ({ref t=#dry-run-n:§9.9}).

Unlike a pattern, a match of a directory does not take its contents.
{C:-R 'build'} keeps the directory {C:build} only, and {C:-R 'build(/.*)?'} keeps
it with its contents.

{C:--exclude-regex} {b:leaves out} the paths that match. It works like
{C:--exclude}. On create, append and update, the walk does not enter an
excluded directory. On a read, an excluded directory also excludes its
contents, so that a list of an archive agrees with the walk that made it.
An exclusion wins over {C:-R} and over the patterns.

Each {C:-R} must match at least one path, or the operation stops with exit 2
and changes nothing ({ref t=#exit-codes:§10.7}), as a pattern does. A mistyped expression is
reported, not answered with an empty result. {C:--exclude-regex} has no such
check, as {C:--exclude} has none.

{section 2 bid=archive-name: Archive name}

The conventional extension of an archive is {C:.ect}. The program finds an
archive by the magic of its header ({ref t=#file-header-64-bytes:§3.1}), not by its name. Thus any name
works, and the extension is only for people.

{b:Create adds {C:.ect} to a name with no extension.} {C:-cf backup} makes
{C:backup.ect}, and the program writes a notice that names the file, except
under {C:-q}. It does so whatever exists with the name as typed. Thus
{C:-cf backup backup} makes {C:backup.ect} from the directory {C:backup}. A name
has an extension when its last element, without leading dots, has a dot. Thus {C:-cf backup.tar} and {C:-cf home.2026-09} keep the name
as typed, and {C:-cf .backup} makes {C:.backup.ect}. A name that is an existing
directory counts as a name with no extension, even when it has a dot. A
directory cannot be the archive. Thus {C:-cf v1.5 v1.5} makes {C:v1.5.ect}
from the directory {C:v1.5}, and {C:-tf v1.5} finds it.

{b:The other operations find the name that create made.} If the name has no
extension, no file has that name, and the same name with {C:.ect} exists, the
program uses that file. Thus {C:-tf backup} lists {C:backup.ect}. A file with
the name as typed always wins. A directory with that name does not count, so
{C:-tf backup} finds {C:backup.ect} next to the directory {C:backup}.

{section 2 bid=names-terminal: Names on the terminal}

A name on Linux can hold any byte except {C:/} and NUL, and the index of a
crafted archive can hold any name. A name that the program writes as it is
can hold an escape sequence. On a terminal, such a sequence can change the
colours, move the cursor, write over the lines before it, or hide other
names. Thus a listing can show what the archive does not hold
({C:doc/Security_Audit.eml}, finding 12).

{b:On a terminal, the program escapes each name that it writes.} This
applies to the paths of {C:-t}, {C:-tv} and {C:-v}, the paths of a dry run, and the
lines of {C:--diff}. It also applies to the link target, the owner and the
codec in the long listing. The escapes are:

{stable col=2 header sb b== i=3% w=94%:
| Character | Shown as |
| newline, tab, carriage return | {C:\\n}, {C:\\t}, {C:\\r} |
| another control character, and DEL | {C:\\xHH} |
| a byte that is not UTF-8 | {C:\\xHH} |
| a C1 control character (U+0080 to U+009F) | {C:\\uHHHH} |
| a character that changes the direction of the text (U+061C, U+200E, U+200F, U+202A to U+202E, U+2066 to U+2069) | {C:\\uHHHH} |
| a backslash | {C:\\\\} |
}

A backslash is doubled, so that each escape has one meaning. Each other
character is shown as it is, accents and other scripts too. The long
listing escapes each column before it measures the widths, so the columns
still line up.

{b:To a pipe or a file, a name is exact,} as with {C:ls}. A script that reads
the names gets the bytes of the archive. A name that holds a newline is
then two lines, so a script that must handle any name reads {C:--json}.
{C:--json} escapes each name in both cases, as JSON does.

{b:A message on stderr is always escaped,} on a terminal or not. A message is
for a person, who can read a log on a terminal later. A message never holds
a control character of its own.

{section 2 bid=strip-components-strip-components: Strip components ({C:--strip-components})}

An archive of a release often has one top directory, for example
{C:project-1.2/}. {C:--strip-components N} removes the first N components of
each path on extraction, as in tar. Thus {C:project-1.2/src/main.go} with
{C:--strip-components 1} becomes {C:src/main.go}. The option applies to {C:-x},
with {C:-n} too, and to {C:--diff}. It is a usage error on another operation,
with {C:-O}, which writes no paths, and with a negative N. A configuration
cannot set it ({ref t=#what-configuration-can-set:§11.1}).

{b:The selection comes first.} The patterns, {C:--exclude},
{C:--exclude-regex} and {C:-R} match the paths in the archive, before the
strip. Thus the same pattern selects the same members with the option and
without it.

{b:A short path is left out.} A member with N components or fewer has no
path after the strip, and is not extracted. tar does the same. The top
directory is such a member.

{b:Each path is checked after the strip} ({ref t=#how-paths-are-checked:§7.5}). A path that is not
canonical is not stripped, so the check refuses it.

{b:A collision is a warning, and exit 1.} Two members can get one path, for
example {C:a/x} and {C:b/x} with N = 1. The program knows all the paths before
it writes, so it finds each collision first. The later member in the order
of the archive paths is extracted. The earlier member is left out, with a
warning that names both:

{code:
eictar: a/x: not extracted: b/x has the same path after --strip-components
}

The run then ends with exit 1 ({ref t=#exit-codes:§10.7}). tar replaces the earlier file with
no warning. The program does not ask the user what to do: it never asks a
question, except for a passphrase, so that a script never waits. A dry run
gives the same warnings, so {C:-n} shows the collisions before a real run.
Two directories with one path are not a collision. The contents of both go
into the one directory, which gets the metadata of the later member.

{b:Hard links follow their targets.} A hard link names its target by its
member id, not by its path. Thus a link goes to the target at the target's
path after the strip. When the target is not extracted, because the strip
or a collision left it out, the first link gets a copy of the content, as
when a pattern leaves the target out ({ref t=#one-live-member-each:§9.2}).

{section 1 bid=configuration-environment-variables-configuration: Configuration: environment variables and the configuration file}

A long command line is tedious to type each time, for example:
{C:--compress zstd:level=19 -j 12 --chunk-size 8MiB}. Thus the same settings can
come from the environment or from a configuration file.

{b:Precedence}, highest first:

{list t=1:
  {item:
    command-line options
  }
  {item:
    environment variables
  }
  {item:
    the configuration file
  }
  {item:
    built-in defaults
  }
}

The program reads exactly one configuration file. It is {C:--config FILE} when
that option is given. If not, it is the first of these files that exists:

{list t=1:
  {item:
    {C:$EICTAR_CONFIG}
  }
  {item:
    {C bc=/._:$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/eictar/config}, or {C:~/.config/eictar/config} when
    {C:XDG_CONFIG_HOME} is not set
  }
  {item:
    {C:~/.eictarrc}
  }
}

A file that {C:--config} or {C:EICTAR_CONFIG} names must exist.

There is {b:no configuration file in the current directory.} Such a file comes
from whatever directory the user is in. That directory can come from the
extraction of someone else's archive. Then the behavior of the tool depends on
where it runs, and an extracted tree becomes a way to attack the user. The
configuration file comes only from the user's home directory, or from a file
that the user names. A test proves that a {C:.eictarrc} in the current
directory has no effect.

{b:{C:--no-config} ignores levels 2 and 3}: no file, and no {C:EICTAR_*}
variable. Then only the command line decides. Scripts and unattended runs
use it. It ignores the variables as well as the file, because a variable
from a login shell also changes what a script does.

{C:--show-config} shows each setting in effect, with its source, and exits. It
needs no operation and no archive. With an operation, it shows the settings
for that operation.

{section 2 bid=what-configuration-can-set: What a configuration can set}

A configuration sets only {i:tuning} options. These come only from the command
line, and a configuration that sets one is an error:

{list t=*:
  {item:
    {b:The operation} ({C:-c}, {C:-x}, {C:-t}, {C:-r}, {C:-u}, {C:--delete} and the others),
    {C:--recompress}, which is part of compact, and {C:-n}, which decides if the
    operation changes anything. A configuration file must never change a
    create into an extract, or make each run a dry run.
  }
  {item:
    {b:What the operation works on:} {C:-f}, {C:-C}, {C:-d}, {C:-T}, {C:-O}, {C:-R},
    {C:--strip-components}, and all positional paths and patterns.
    {C:--strip-components} depends on how the archive was made, so it is not a
    preference. {C:--exclude-regex} can come from a
    configuration, as {C:exclude} can.
  }
  {item:
    {b:The passphrase and its sources} ({ref t=#security-rules:§11.4}).
  }
  {item:
    {C:--config}, {C:--no-config} and {C:--show-config}.
  }
}

Every other long option of {ref t=#compression-selection:§10.2} to {ref t=#general-options:§10.4} can come from a configuration,
except {C:--help} and {C:--version}. The compression shorthands {C:-z}, {C:-J} and
{C:--zstd} have the key {C:compress}.

{b:A configured value applies only to the operations that use it.} On any
other operation, the program ignores it. For example, {C:compress = xz} does
not stop a listing, and {C:preserve-owner = true} applies only to {C:-x}. The
{C:kdf-*} keys apply only to create and to {C:--encrypt-archive}, which make new
keys. Thus {C:--change-passphrase} keeps the
parameters of the archive unless its command line gives new ones ({ref t=#change-passphrase:§9.7}). On the
command line, the rules of {ref t=#general-options:§10.4} still refuse an option where it means
nothing.

{b:Some keys decide one thing together:} {C:compress} with {C:-z}, {C:-J} and
{C:--zstd}, the three overwrite policies, and {C:verbose} with {C:quiet}. If the
command line sets one key of a group, the program ignores the configured
values for the whole group. Thus {C:-z} wins over {C:compress = xz}, and is not a
second codec.

{b:{C:encrypt-index} in a configuration is a preference.} It applies when the
archive is encrypted, by create or by {C:--encrypt-archive}. On the command
line without {C:--encrypt} or {C:--encrypt-archive}, it is an error. {C:progress} is a preference in the same way: a dry run ignores it,
and on the command line with {C:-n} it is an error ({ref t=#dry-run-n:§9.9}).

{section 2 bid=environment-variables: Environment variables}

The rule is mechanical: {C:EICTAR_}, then the long option name in capitals,
with underscores for dashes.

{code:
EICTAR_COMPRESS=zstd:level=19       # --compress
EICTAR_WORKERS=12                   # --workers
EICTAR_CHUNK_SIZE=8MiB              # --chunk-size
EICTAR_MEMORY_LIMIT=4GiB            # --memory-limit
EICTAR_SPILL_THRESHOLD=64MiB        # --spill-threshold
EICTAR_UPDATE_MODE=different        # --update-mode
EICTAR_KDF_MEMORY=262144            # --kdf-memory
EICTAR_NO_XATTRS=1                  # boolean: 1, true, yes, on = on; 0, false, no, off = off
EICTAR_EXCLUDE='*.o'                # one glob; use EICTAR_EXCLUDE_FROM for more
}

Defaults for one codec apply when the user selects that codec without that
parameter. They use {C:EICTAR_CODEC_<NAME>_<PARAM>}, for example
{C:EICTAR_CODEC_ZSTD_LEVEL=19} and {C:EICTAR_CODEC_XZ_PRESET=6}.

An {C:EICTAR_*} variable that the program cannot parse, or does not know, is a
usage error (exit 2). The program does not ignore it. If it ignores a
misspelled {C:EICTAR_COMPESS}, the user gets an archive at a compression level
that they did not ask for, with no message.

Two variables are not settings. {C:EICTAR_CONFIG} names the configuration file.
The variable that {C:--passphrase-env} names holds a secret, and its name can
start with {C:EICTAR_}.

{section 2 bid=file-format: File format}

Each line holds {C:key = value}. The parser ignores blank lines. A {C:#} starts a
comment at the start of a line, or after a space or a tab. Thus
{C:exclude = build#1} keeps its {C:#}.

Keys are long option names without the leading dashes. A key can occur only
one time, except {C:exclude} and {C:exclude-regex}, which add one pattern on
each line. Optional
{C:[codec.NAME]} sections give defaults for one codec. The parser has no
dependency, and it checks each codec value when it reads it, so that an error
names the line.

{code s=ini:
# ~/.eictarrc
compress    = zstd
workers     = 12
chunk-size  = 8MiB
update-mode = different
progress    = true
exclude     = *.o
exclude     = .cache

[codec.zstd]
level = 19
long  = 27
train = on

[codec.xz]
preset = 6
}

A key that is not a known long option is a usage error, for the reason in
{ref t=#environment-variables:§11.2}. So is a section that is not {C:[codec.NAME]}, and a codec that does not
exist.

{section 2 bid=security-rules: Security rules}

Without a configuration file in the current directory, most of the risk goes
away. Two rules remain:

{list t=*:
  {item:
    {b:No passphrase can come from the environment or from a configuration
    file.} The program does not read {C:EICTAR_PASSPHRASE}. {C:passphrase},
    {C:passphrase-file} and {C:passphrase-env} in a configuration file are errors,
    not values. A secret still needs the explicit {C:--passphrase-file}, or
    {C:--passphrase-env VAR} with a variable that the user chose. Thus the secret
    stays out of a file that someone can add to a repository, or to a backup,
    by accident.
  }
  {item:
    {b:The program checks ownership and mode}, for a {C:--config FILE} and for
    the default locations. It refuses a configuration file that another user
    owns, or that the group or other users can write. The owner can also be
    root, for a file that an administrator installs. The file can set {C:exclude}
    and {C:exclude-regex}. Thus a person who can write it can make a backup
    smaller without a message, and a refusal is better than a warning. On
    Windows, the program reads the owner and the ACL of the file ({ref t=#other-platforms:§15.1}).
  }
}

{C:-v} reports which configuration file the program read. Thus a user can find
an unexpected setting without {C:--show-config}.

{section 2 bid=tests: Tests}

The operational tests run the binary with a new {C:HOME}, no
{C:XDG_CONFIG_HOME} file and no {C:EICTAR_*} variable. The unit tests of package
{C:cli} do the same. A test must not depend on the configuration of the person
who runs it.

{section 1 nc bid=part-iv: Part IV — Implementation}

{section 1 bid=package-layout: Package layout}

{C:go.mod} is at the project root. The packages are under {C:src/}. The module
path is {C bc=/._:github.com/philmalin/eictar}, so the import paths are like
{C bc=/._:github.com/philmalin/eictar/src/internal/format}. Thus
{C:go install github.com/philmalin/eictar/src/cmd/eictar@latest} builds the
program.

Such a binary gets its version from the module version that Go
records in it. A {C:make} build gets it from {C:VERSION}, which is the output of
{C:git describe} by default. The release workflow sets it from the tag.

The module cache is in {C:.gocache/}, the build cache in {C:.gobuildcache/}, and
temporary files in {C:.tmp/}. All three are inside the project. The {C:Makefile}
sets {C:GOMODCACHE}, {C:GOCACHE} and {C:TMPDIR}, so a build or a test run writes
nothing outside the project directory. Use the {C:make} targets, not a
bare {C:go} command:

{stable col=2 header sb b== i=3% w=94%:
| Target | Action |
| {C:make build} | build {C:.build/eictar}, stripped and with no local paths ({C:-trimpath -ldflags="-s -w"}). The version that {C:--version} prints comes from {C:git describe}, such as {C:1.0.1-2-g5ccd0ce}, or is {C:dev} outside a git checkout. {C:make build VERSION=1.0.0} sets it. |
| {C:make release} | build the release binaries in {C:.build/release/}, one directory for each platform ({ref t=#releases:§12.3}) |
| {C:make test} | run the unit tests. {C:TESTTIMEOUT} (default {C:30m}) is the limit for each package: the 10 minutes of Go were too few for {C:archive} under {C:-race} on the macOS runner. |
| {C:make test-race} | run the unit tests with the race detector, with the same limit |
| {C:make operational} | run the operational tests (testing.md §2) |
| {C:make large} | run the operational test of an archive larger than 4 GiB (testing.md §2). {C:LARGE=8} sets the size of its large file in GiB (default 4). It needs approximately four times that much free space in {C:.tmp/}. |
| {C:make fuzz} | run each fuzz target of {C:FUZZ_TARGETS} for {C:FUZZTIME} (default 30s). Go keeps the corpus that a run finds in {C:.gobuildcache/fuzz}, and the next run starts from it. A failing input goes to {C:testdata/fuzz} of its package, and becomes a test that {C:make check} runs. |
| {C:make bench} | run the Go benchmarks of create and extract ({ref t=#measured-performance:§8.4}) |
| {C:make compare} | compare eictar with {C:tar} and a compressor, on {C:DIR} (default: the module cache). Needs {C:tar}, {C:zstd}, {C:xz} and {C:gzip}. |
| {C:make crossvet} | run {C:vet} for each platform of the CI workflow: Linux, macOS, FreeBSD, NetBSD, OpenBSD and Windows ({ref t=#other-platforms:§15.1}). A cross-compile finds a name that one platform does not have, before a push. |
| {C:make check} | {C:fmt}, {C:vet}, {C:crossvet}, {C:test-race} and {C:operational}. {C:CROSSVET=} leaves out {C:crossvet}, as CI does. |
| {C:make check-norace} | {C:make check} with {C:test} for {C:test-race}, for NetBSD and OpenBSD |
| {C:make skips} | list each test that this platform skips, with its reason, each test that fails, with its output, and the totals |
| {C:make cover} | the coverage of the unit tests and the operational tests together, in {C:.tmp/cover/} (testing.md §5) |
| {C:make mutate} | mutation testing of the packages in {C:MUTATE} (default {C:format}, {C:crypt} and {C:pipeline}). {C:MUTATE_FLAGS} gives options to {C:tools/mutate}, and {C:MUTATE_ACCEPTED} the accepted list (testing.md §5). |
| {C:make stress} | random end-to-end tests against a model (testing.md §4). {C:STRESS="-duration 30m"} runs longer, and {C:STRESS="-seed N -sequences 1"} replays a failure. |
| {C:make stress-build} | build the stress tester alone, as {C:.build/stress}, and eictar with the disk faults, as {C:.build/eictar-faults} |
| {C:make ectread} | build the second reader, written from {C:doc/format.md} alone, as {C:.build/ectread} (testing.md §5, step 2) |
| {C:make tla} | check the TLA+ models of {C:spec/} with TLC (testing.md §5, step 5). Needs Java ({C:JAVA}, default {C:/opt/java/bin/java}) and {C:tla2tools.jar} in the root ({C:TLA_JAR}). |
| {C:make vuln} | scan for the known vulnerabilities of the modules and of the standard library that the code calls, with {C:govulncheck} ({C:GOVULNCHECK} gives its version). It uses the toolchain of {C:GO}, and needs the network. |
| {C:make gate} | the release gate (testing.md §5, step 6): {C:check}, {C:tla}, {C:vuln}, {C:mutate} on each package, {C:fuzz} and {C:stress}, on a worktree of the commit {C:GATE_REF} (default {C:HEAD}). It writes a record in {C:.build/gate/}. With the defaults it takes about a day. {C:GATE_STEPS}, {C:GATE_FUZZTIME} (default {C:1h}), {C:GATE_STRESSTIME} (default {C:8h}) and {C:GATE_MUTATE} change it. |
}

{C:GO=go} gives another toolchain, as CI does: {C:make check GO=go}.

{code:
