The configuration layer is in package {C:cli}, not in a package of its own.
It changes nothing but options, and it needs the option parser.

{section 2 bid=core-interfaces: Core interfaces}

The codec interfaces:

{code s=go:
// codec: one chunk in, one chunk out. Encoders hold no state between chunks,
// so all workers can share one encoder.
type Encoder interface \{
    Encode(dst, src []byte) ([]byte, error)
    Resolved() map[string]any // the parameters in effect, for the index catalog
    Close() error
\}

// Decode receives the plaintext size that the index gives. Without it, a
// crafted chunk is a decompression bomb with nothing to compare against.
type Decoder interface \{
    Decode(dst, src []byte, plainSize int) ([]byte, error)
    Close() error
\}

type Factory interface \{
    Name() string
    Describe() Spec                                      // for --list-codecs
    NewEncoder(p Params, concurrency int) (Encoder, error) // concurrency: §8.2
    NewDecoder(maxPlain int) (Decoder, error)
\}
}

A decoder takes no compression parameters, because the stream contains what
decoding needs. Thus a later build can read an archive even if its view of
the recorded parameters changed.

The index encoder takes hooks, so that encryption did not change the shape of
the pipeline:

{code s=go:
// format
type Sealer func(plaintext []byte) (ciphertext []byte, err error)
type Opener func(ciphertext []byte) (plaintext []byte, err error)

type EncodeOptions struct \{
    Compress bool
    Seal     Sealer                           // nil: the index is not sealed
    Digest   func([]byte) [DigestSize]byte    // nil: the plain BLAKE3 (§6.4)
\}
}

The chunk sealer in {C:crypt} is a different thing. It seals one chunk with a
counter and a final-chunk flag. {C:format.Sealer} seals the whole index in one
call:

{code s=go:
// crypt
type MemberSealer struct\{ ... \}
func (s *MemberSealer) Seal(dst, plaintext []byte, chunkIndex uint64, final bool) ([]byte, error)
func (s *MemberSealer) Open(dst, ciphertext []byte, chunkIndex uint64, final bool) ([]byte, error)
}

There is one sealer and one key for each member. {C:final} is a parameter, not
a state of the sealer. The writer learns which chunk is last only when the
next read reaches the end of the file ({ref t=#concurrency-model:§8}).

Each codec registers itself in {C:init()}. Thus a new codec changes only one
file.

A codec can also have interfaces that the package does not export: for a
window, for the memory of an encoder ({ref t=#shared-encoder:§8.2}), for encoder options, and for
dictionaries. Only zstd has dictionaries. The package functions {C:TrainSize},
{C:TrainDict}, {C:DictID}, {C:NewEncoderWith} and {C:NewDecoderWithDict} refuse a
dictionary for any other codec. The functions that take
parameters also turn a key that stands alone into its value ({ref t=#compression-selection:§10.2}), so each
codec sees plain values.

{section 2 bid=diagrams: Diagrams}

The first diagram shows the packages of v1.1.4. The other three follow the
data through the three main paths of v1.1.0: create, extract, and a change
to an archive.

{b:Packages.} An arrow points from a package to a package that it imports.
The imports go in one direction only: {C:format}, {C:crypt} and {C:codec} import
no other package of eictar. Each box names, in grey, the external modules
that the package imports. {C:x/sys/unix} and {C:x/sys/windows} are imported
only on their own systems.

{Image s=fig/Package_Imports.png w=75% center: The packages of eictar, and what each one imports}

{b:Create.} Three kinds of goroutine share the work ({ref t=#concurrency-model:§8}). The walker reads
each file in order and cuts it into chunks. The workers compress and seal the
chunks in parallel, and put each member's chunks in order in its spool. One
emitter writes each finished member to the file, so that only one goroutine
moves the file offset.

Append and update use the same path. They start with {C:OpenAppend}, not
{C:Create}. Before the walk, {C:useDictionary} trains a dictionary, or takes the
archive's newest one, when the codec asks for one ({ref t=#dictionaries-m10:§4.2}).

{Image s=fig/Create_Flow.png w=90% center: Create: the walker, the workers and the emitter}

{b:Open and extract.} The reader checks the archive in a fixed order before
it trusts any number from it ({ref t=#overview-format:§2}). Then extraction runs in four phases
({ref t=#parallel-extraction:§8.3}). All writes go through {C:os.Root}, so no path can leave the
destination.

{Image s=fig/Open_And_Extract.png w=60% center: Open and extract}

{b:A change to an archive.} Append, update and delete write a new generation
after the old trailer ({ref t=#write-sequence:§9.1}). The new trailer is the commit. A crash before
it leaves a file that the reader refuses and {C:--repair} restores. An error
before it truncates the file to its old length.

{Image s=fig/Archive_Change_Sequence.png w=90% center: A change to an archive}

{section 2 bid=releases: Releases}

A release is a tag of the form {C:v1.2.3}. The version follows semantic
versioning. A change of the format that an old reader cannot read needs a
new major version ({ref t=#fixed-structures:§3}).

These steps make a release:

{list t=1:
  {item:
    Run {C:make gate} on the commit (testing.md §5, step 6). The last line of its
    record, {C bc=/._:.build/gate/SHA/record.txt}, must be {C:PASS}.
  }
  {item:
    Make sure that the ci workflow passed on the same commit, on all six
    platforms.
  }
  {item:
    Tag the commit with {C:git tag -a v1.2.3 -m "eictar 1.2.3"}, and push the
    tag.
  }
  {item:
    When the release workflow and the ci workflow on the tag pass, publish
    the draft release.
  }
}

The gate runs on the machine of the developer, not on GitHub, because it
takes about a day.

A push of the tag starts the release workflow
({C bc=/._:.github/workflows/release.yml}). The workflow uses the newest patch
release of the Go version in {C:go.mod}. It runs {C:make check} on Linux, and
{C:make vuln} with the toolchain that builds the binaries.
Then {C:make release} builds one binary for each platform of {ref t=#other-platforms:§15.1}, on amd64
and on arm64, with the man page, {C:README.md}, {C:LICENSE}, {C:TRADEMARKS.md} and
{C:THIRD_PARTY.md}. The licenses of the modules in the binary require their
texts in a binary distribution, and {C:THIRD_PARTY.md} holds them. It must be
updated when {C:go.mod} changes.
On Windows, the binary is {C:eictar.exe}, and the man page is not included.
The workflow packs each one in a {C:.tar.gz} file, or in a {C:.zip} file for
Windows. It writes {C:SHA256SUMS}, and makes a draft release with these files.

The ci workflow runs on the same tag, on all six platforms. On a branch, it
does not run for a push or a pull request that changes only Markdown, HTML or
the license. The man page is not in that list, because a test reads it. A
tag always runs it. The draft is
published by hand, after that run passes too.

{b:Dependencies do not change by themselves.} {C:go.mod} gives the exact
version of each module, and {C:go.sum} its checksum, so each build of one
commit uses the same code. Dependabot ({C:.github/dependabot.yml}) opens a
pull request each month: one for the Go modules, and one for the GitHub
Actions of the workflows. An update of a module follows these steps:

{list t=1:
  {item:
    Read what changed in the packages that eictar uses, and the security
    advisories of the module. The decoders read untrusted archives, so a
    change there can be a security fix. {C:govulncheck} does not see an
    advisory that is not in the Go vulnerability database yet (testing.md
    §5, step 6).
  }
  {item:
    Run {C:make check}, and {C:make fuzz} for some minutes. The golden archives
    show that the format did not change.
  }
  {item:
    Measure the codecs with the old and the new version, with
    {C:BenchmarkCodecs} on real data ({ref t=#measured-performance:§8.4}). Use the same machine and the
    same cores for both.
  }
  {item:
    Update {C:THIRD_PARTY.md}: the versions, and the license texts if they
    changed. Until then, a test fails.
  }
}

{section 1 bid=testing: Testing}

The testing plan is in {C:testing.md}: the unit tests, the operational tests,
the supporting tools, the stress tester, and the assurance plan with the
release gate. Until 2026-10-15 it was this section, and section 13.N is now
section N of {C:testing.md}.

{section 1 bid=security-considerations: Security considerations}

{section 2 bid=what-observer-sees: What an observer sees}

{list t=*:
  {item:
    {b:The plain index (the default) shows} every file name, size, mode,
    timestamp, link target and xattr, with its value. A value can say more than
    a name: macOS records in {C bc=/._:com.apple.metadata:kMDItemWhereFroms} the address
    that a file came from. This default is useful, but the man page must state it
    clearly. {C:--encrypt-index} hides it. Then an observer sees only that the
    archive exists, and its total size.
  }
  {item:
    {b:Content digests.} The plain index also shows the digest of each member.
    In an encrypted archive, the digest is keyed ({ref t=#crypto-header-cbor-plaintext:§6.2}), so it tells an
    observer nothing about the content. A digest that is not keyed shows if
    the archive holds a file that the observer has. Equal keyed
    digests still show that two members have the same content, and so does a
    reference ({ref t=#identical-content-m11:§4.3}).
  }
  {item:
    {b:Dictionaries.} A dictionary holds pieces of the files, so in an
    encrypted archive it is sealed like content ({ref t=#aead-layer:§6.3}). Its size and its place
    are in the index.
  }
  {item:
    {b:Sizes.} With a plain index, the compressed length of every member and
    every chunk is visible. With {C:--encrypt-index}, they are {b:not} visible.
    The body has no framing ({ref t=#member-payloads-chunks-compression:§4}), so the blob boundaries exist only in the
    index. Then only the total size of the archive is visible. v1 has no
    padding, so the total size is still information.
  }
  {item:
    {b:Compression before encryption} permits an inference like CRIME or
    BREACH. It needs an attacker who can put chosen content into the same
    member as a secret. An archive is not such an adaptive setting, so the design
    accepts this risk. {C:--compress none} removes it.
  }
  {item:
    {b:Dictionaries and shared content work across files} ({ref t=#dictionaries-m10:§4.2}, {ref t=#identical-content-m11:§4.3}). Thus
    the inference no longer needs the same member. Suppose that an attacker can
    put files into a tree that is archived again and again, and can see the
    size of the archive. A copy of a file that the archive holds adds almost
    nothing, so the attacker can test a guess of a whole file. With {C:train}, a
    file that looks like the secret files compresses better.

    {C:--encrypt-index} hides the size of each member, but not the size of the
    archive. For such a tree, use {C:--no-dedup} and no {C:train}.
  }
}

{section 2 bid=keys-passphrases: Keys and passphrases}

{list t=*:
  {item:
    {b:One key for all members, and no forward secrecy}, as the problem
    statement requires. If the passphrase is known, the whole archive is open,
    earlier generations too.
  }
  {item:
    {b:The passphrase wraps a random data key} ({ref t=#crypto-header-cbor-plaintext:§6.2}). Thus
    {C:--change-passphrase} needs no second encryption of the members ({ref t=#change-passphrase:§9.7}). A
    change does not protect what someone already has: a copy of the old file,
    or the data key.
  }
  {item:
    {b:{C:--encrypt-archive} encrypts what was plaintext} ({ref t=#encryption-existing-archive-encrypt:§9.11}). The old file,
    its free blocks, and its copies, snapshots and backups stay readable. Create
    with {C:-e} keeps plaintext off the disk.
  }
  {item:
    {b:Passphrase handling.} The program reads the passphrase from the terminal
    with echo off, and sets it to zero after the key schedule. The reader sets
    the data key to zero when it closes. The program does not set the derived
    subkeys to zero, and it does not lock memory. Go can copy memory without
    notice, so zeroing is only a partial protection. {C:--passphrase-env} prints
    a warning.
  }
  {item:
    {b:A passphrase file that other users can read gives a warning}, as ssh
    gives for a key.
  }
  {item:
    {b:The configuration never supplies a secret, and no other user can change
    it} ({ref t=#configuration-environment-variables-configuration:§11}, {ref t=#security-rules:§11.4}).
  }
}

{section 2 bid=extraction-reads-disk: Extraction and reads of the disk}

{list t=*:
  {item:
    {b:Paths} ({ref t=#path-handling:§7}): the writer cleans paths, and the reader checks them again.
    Both refuse absolute paths and {C:..}, because another program can write an
    archive. Extraction never follows a link and never goes through a linked
    directory, also one inside the destination that the archive made or another
    user put there ({ref t=#symbolic-links:§7.6}). Thus root can restore a tree
    in which other users own directories.
  }
  {item:
    {b:Metadata.} By default, extraction restores nothing that needs privilege
    or gives it. Setuid, setgid, privileged xattrs and exact modes need {C:-p},
    and a user who is not root gets each mode less the umask. Owners need
    {C:--preserve-owner}, and device nodes need {C:--preserve-devices}, both as
    root. A member {C:.} never changes the destination. {ref t=#metadata-extraction:§7.7} gives the full
    table.
  }
  {item:
    {b:New node types.} Pipes and device nodes are created with {C:*at} calls, on
    a directory descriptor from {C:os.Root} and a single name component. A
    planted link cannot redirect them. A test proves this for a pipe.
  }
  {item:
    {b:Decompression bombs.} The index gives the plaintext size of each chunk.
    A chunk that decodes to another size is an error.
  }
  {item:
    {b:Allocation from a crafted archive.} Before the reader uses a number to
    size a buffer, it applies a limit to that number:

    {list t=*:
      {item:
        the index length: {C:MaxIndexSize}, 1 GiB, and at most 200 times its
        compressed size, or 64 MiB, whichever is more
      }
      {item:
        array elements: the input length, and {C:MaxIndexMembers}
      }
      {item:
        the chunk size: {C:MaxChunkSize}, 256 MiB ({ref t=#chunk-table:§5.2})
      }
      {item:
        the window of a zstd frame: the chunk size, or 1 MiB, whichever is
        more ({C:doc/format.md} {ref t=#cryptography:§6})
      }
      {item:
        the crypto header length: 64 KiB
      }
    }

    Extraction also reduces its number of workers, so that
    {C:workers × 2 × chunk_size} fits in the memory budget. Without that, a
    256 MiB chunk size with 64 workers needs 32 GiB.
  }
  {item:
    {b:Cost from a crafted archive.} The KDF limits of {ref t=#crypto-header-cbor-plaintext:§6.2} apply before the
    passphrase prompt.
  }
  {item:
    {b:Names.} On a terminal, a name from the archive is escaped, so that an
    escape sequence in it cannot change what the terminal shows. A message on
    stderr is always escaped ({ref t=#names-terminal:§10.13}).
  }
  {item:
    {b:Compare.} {C:--diff} reads the disk at paths from the archive. It finds
    the start of each walk through {C:os.Root}, so a link on disk cannot take it
    out of the directory ({ref t=#compare-disk-diff:§9.8}).
  }
}

{section 2 bid=integrity-its-limits: Integrity and its limits}

{b:For a plaintext archive, integrity is error detection, not tamper
evidence.} The digests find what storage does: bit rot, a truncated
transfer, a partial overwrite. They do not find what a person with write
access does, because an attacker can calculate every digest and CRC in the
file again. Thus {C:--verify} answers "is this archive consistent and
undamaged?". Its documentation must not suggest the answer to "is this the
archive that I made?". A user who needs that answer must record the hash of
the archive in a place that the attacker cannot control, or sign the
archive.

{b:An encrypted archive is tamper-evident in content and in metadata.} The
AEAD covers the chunks, and the keyed index digest ({ref t=#authenticating-index:§6.4}) covers the index.
Four attacks stay outside v1:

{list t=*:
  {item:
    {b:A full rewrite.} An attacker can always replace the file with a
    different archive. To detect that, a signature is necessary ({ref t=#future-work:§15}).
  }
  {item:
    {b:A downgrade.} An attacker with no passphrase can remove the encryption
    from the framing of an archive. The steps are: clear the header flag, set
    the crypto header length to zero, change the plain index, and calculate
    the {i:unkeyed} digest again.

    The result opens with no prompt and lists the names that the attacker
    chose. A plaintext reader never looks for a key, so
    this attack goes around {ref t=#authenticating-index:§6.4}. The member content still cannot be forged,
    because the sealed blobs do not agree with their plaintext digests. But
    people read the listing to check a backup.

    The defense is the
    expectation of the user: with a passphrase source, a plaintext archive is
    refused ({ref t=#encryption:§10.3}). A user who gives no passphrase option gets no such
    warning. This gap cannot be closed, because a reader cannot know that an
    archive was meant to be encrypted unless the user says so.
  }
  {item:
    {b:A rollback.} An attacker can put back last month's genuine archive, and
    every check passes. Proof of freshness must come from outside the file.
    One method is a record of {C:archive_uuid} and {C:generation} in a place that
    the attacker cannot reach. Another method is signatures with timestamps.
  }
  {item:
    {b:Deletion.} No format can prevent it. More than one copy is the answer.
  }
}

Two smaller limits are known and accepted. The trailer flags are not in the
keyed digest, so a changed flag stops the archive from opening. That is a
denial of service, not a forgery. The derived subkeys are not set to zero
({ref t=#keys-passphrases:§14.2}).

{section 2 bid=archiving-tree-that-other: Archiving a tree that other users can write}

A backup often runs as root, over directories that other users can write.
Such a user can change the tree while the program walks it, and can plant
links where the program writes. Three rules apply:

{list t=*:
  {item:
    {b:The writer opens a walked file only as it was walked.} Between the
    walk's {C:lstat} and the open, a user can put a symbolic link to
    {C:/etc/shadow} in place of a file. The open does not follow a link, except
    with {C:-h}. The file that it opens must have the device and the inode that
    the walk saw. Otherwise the member fails, as for a read error. This rule
    also applies to the samples of a dictionary and to {C:-u
    --update-mode=digest}.
  }
  {item:
    {b:The walk goes through each directory that it opens.} A path is
    resolved again at each call, and a directory above an entry can become a
    link between two calls. Thus the walk holds each directory that it enters
    open, as an {C:os.Root}. Below it, each {C:lstat}, {C:readlink} and open uses one
    name in that directory. A subdirectory must have the device and the inode
    of its {C:lstat}, or it fails as a member that changed, and the walk does
    not enter it. The extended attributes come from the open file or
    directory, not from the path. Without this rule, a link put in the place
    of a directory takes the walk into the directory that the link points to
    ({C:doc/Security_Audit.eml}, finding 11).

    A path that the user names is opened by its path, from its parent. A
    directory that the process cannot open is archived empty, and its
    attributes come from the path. Root can open every directory. {C:-h}
    follows a link by its path, on purpose.
  }
  {item:
    {b:The archive path follows only the links that the kernel follows} ({ref t=#changes-checks:§9}).
  }
}

{section 2 bid=audit: Audit}

{C:doc/Security_Audit.eml} records the review of v1.0.1: eight findings, the
fixes of v1.0.2, and a test for each fix. It also lists what the review
examined and found sound. Its {ref t=#cryptography:§6} records a follow-up review of the changes
after v1.0.2, with one more finding and its fix in v1.0.3. Its {ref t=#path-handling:§7} reviews
{C:--diff} and {C:-n}, with two findings, each fixed: a directory that becomes a
link during the walk ({ref t=#archiving-tree-that-other:§14.5}), and control characters in names on the
terminal ({ref t=#names-terminal:§10.13}).

{section 1 bid=future-work: Future work}

Some of these items change the format. An older reader must then refuse the
archive, with a header flag or a new {C:format_major} ({ref t=#file-header-64-bytes:§3.1}, {ref t=#chunk-level-deduplication-not:§4.4}).

{list t=*:
  {item:
    {b:Public-key recipients} in the reserved {C:recipients} field.
  }
  {item:
    {b:Content-defined chunking} with a table of chunk hashes, for chunk-level
    deduplication and rsync-style synchronization. {ref t=#chunk-level-deduplication-not:§4.4} records why it is not
    adopted, and what can change that decision.
  }
  {item:
    {b:A B-tree or sorted-page index} for archives with tens of millions of
    members, marked by a new {C:index_kind} in the trailer flags.
  }
  {item:
    {b:A streaming variant} that repeats member metadata inline, for use through
    a pipe.
  }
  {item:
    {b:A detached signature} ({C:.ect.sig}) of the trailer.
  }
}

{section 2 bid=other-platforms: Other platforms}

eictar supports the five UNIX-like platforms and Windows (see "Windows"
below). The CI workflow passes on all six platforms (see "Testing" below).

The metadata code ({C:src/internal/meta}) has a part that the five UNIX-like
platforms share, and a small file for each platform. Windows has
{C:meta_windows.go}. On any other platform (illumos, Plan 9 and the rest), the
program archives content, directories and links, and records no other
metadata.

{stable col=7 header sb b==:
| Feature | Linux | macOS | FreeBSD | NetBSD | OpenBSD | Windows |
| owner, special bits | yes | yes | yes | yes | yes | not recorded |
| times, hardlinks, link times | yes | yes | yes | yes | yes | yes |
| holes ({C:SEEK_DATA}, {C:SEEK_HOLE}) | yes | yes | yes | stored dense | stored dense | stored dense |
| xattrs | yes | yes, no namespaces | {C:user} and {C:system} | {C:user} and {C:system} | none on the platform | not recorded |
| POSIX ACLs | yes, as xattrs | not recorded ({ref t=#decisions:§1.1}, row 8) | not recorded | not recorded | none on the platform | none on the platform |
| pipes and device nodes on extraction | yes | skipped with a notice | yes | yes | yes | skipped with a notice |
| memory size for the budget | {C:/proc/meminfo} | {C:sysctl hw.memsize} | {C:sysctl hw.physmem} | {C:sysctl hw.physmem64} | {C:sysctl hw.physmem} | {C:GlobalMemoryStatusEx} |
}

"Stored dense" is not a failure. Without hole detection, the reader reads the
whole file, which is correct.

{b:A hole is only as fine as the filesystem reports it.} ext4 reports data in
4 KiB blocks. ZFS, the usual filesystem on FreeBSD, reports it in whole
records of 128 KiB by default. Thus on ZFS, each small data region costs a
record. On the FreeBSD CI runner, three regions of 8, 8 and 4 KiB in a 64 MiB
file stored 384 KiB. The holes are still kept.

{C:meta.Supports} gives this table in the code, one row for each platform. The
unit tests read it, so that each test runs where its feature exists and says
why it does not elsewhere. The operational tests keep their own copy of the
table, from this section. A test must not take its expectations from the code
that it tests.

{b:The files:}

{list t=*:
  {item:
    {C:meta_unix.go} has what the five UNIX-like platforms share: {C:Stat}, link times, the
    owner, and the checks for a single name component.
  }
  {item:
    {C:sys_<os>.go} has what differs: the field names of {C:syscall.Stat_t}, the
    pipe and device calls, and the platform's row of the table. FreeBSD has
    {C:mkfifoat} as system call 497, which {C:x/sys} does not wrap, so eictar calls
    it by number. On NetBSD, eictar makes {C:mknodat} itself too, because the
    wrapper of {C:x/sys} passes the device number in the wrong place.
  }
  {item:
    {C:xattr_names.go} reads NUL-separated names on Linux and macOS.
    {C:xattr_extattr.go} calls the {C:extattr} system calls on FreeBSD and NetBSD.
    {C:xattr_none.go} is for OpenBSD.
  }
  {item:
    {C:holes_seek.go} is for Linux, macOS and FreeBSD, and {C:holes_dense.go} for
    NetBSD and OpenBSD.
  }
}

{b:Faults in {C:x/sys} v0.48.} Two made eictar call {C:extattr} directly on
FreeBSD and NetBSD:

{list t=*:
  {item:
    The Linux-style {C:Llistxattr} returns the raw BSD list: a length byte, then
    the name, with no namespace. Linux returns NUL-separated full names. Code
    that splits on NUL, as the Linux code does, gets meaningless names.
  }
  {item:
    {C:ListxattrNS}, {C:LlistxattrNS} and {C:FlistxattrNS} return {C:err} where they
    mean {C:e}. Thus a failed call looks like a file with no xattrs.
  }
}

A third fault is on OpenBSD. {C:x/sys} finds the MIB of an OpenBSD sysctl name
in its own table, and the table has no {C:hw.physmem64}. It gives MIB {C:\{6, 19\}},
which is {C:HW_PHYSMEM64}, the name {C:hw.physmem}. Thus {C:memory_bsd.go} asks for
{C:hw.physmem} on OpenBSD. A request for {C:hw.physmem64} gets an error, and
then OpenBSD has no memory size. {C:TestTotalMemory} makes sure that each
platform gives a size.

A fourth fault is on NetBSD. NetBSD declares {C:mknodat} as {C:mknodat(int fd,
const char *path, mode_t mode, int PAD, dev_t dev)}, and {C:unix.Mknodat}
puts the device number where the padding is. The kernel then read 0 for
the number. Thus each device node that {C:unix.Mknodat} makes on NetBSD is
0:0. {C:sys_netbsd.go} makes the system call itself, with the padding.
{C:TestMknodAsRoot} makes sure of the device number, in the CI job of NetBSD,
which runs as root.

All four are worth a report to the {C:x/sys} project. The test helpers read xattrs
with their own code, not with the code in {C:meta}, so that a fault in {C:meta}
cannot hide itself.

{b:macOS cannot make pipes and device nodes safely.} macOS 13 has
{C:mkfifoat} and {C:mknodat}, but {C:x/sys} does not wrap them. A path-based call
can go through a planted link ({ref t=#symbolic-links:§7.6}). Thus the reader skips pipes and device
nodes on macOS, with a notice. Pipes are rare in a macOS backup.

{b:macOS file names must be UTF-8.} APFS refuses other names with {C:EILSEQ}.
An archive from Linux with such a name gives an error for that member on
macOS. The tests of those names skip there.

{b:illumos is different again.} Its xattrs are a hidden directory for each
file, which is a separate model.

{b:Testing is the real condition.} A cross-compile proves that the code
builds, not that it works. The differences between platforms are the subtle
ones: xattr meanings, hole reports, and what a no-follow call does on each
filesystem.

{C:.github/workflows/ci.yml} runs {C:make check} on Linux and macOS,
and in virtual machines for FreeBSD, NetBSD and OpenBSD. NetBSD and OpenBSD
have no race detector, so they run {C:make check-norace}. A platform is
supported while the workflow passes on it.

Each job runs on its platform, so CI leaves out {C:crossvet} ({C:CROSSVET=}). On
Linux and macOS, {C:setup-go} saves the build cache and the module cache
between runs. The Makefile keeps them in {C:.gobuildcache/} and {C:.gocache/},
where {C:setup-go} does not look. So these jobs give the directories of
{C:setup-go} on the command line of {C:make}. Without them, each run builds
every package again, with the race detector and for each fuzz target.

Windows has no {C:make} in the runner image. Its job runs {C:go vet}, the unit
tests and the operational tests directly, without the race detector. The
Linux job runs {C:gofmt} and the fuzz targets for all platforms.

{b:Windows.} The program archives content, directories,
links and hardlinks on Windows, with the times of each. It records no owner,
no mode and no extended attributes ({C:meta_windows.go}). Windows differs from
Unix in these ways, and the program does as follows:

{list t=*:
  {item:
    {b:Paths.} An absolute path starts with a volume name: a drive ({C:C:}) or a
    share ({C:\\\\server\\share}). The writer removes the volume name, as it removes
    a leading {C:/} ({ref t=#how-paths-are-stored:§7.3}). A path that starts with three or more separators,
    such as {C:///etc}, has no volume name. Windows reads it as a share, but the
    writer reads it as {C:/etc}, as on Unix.
  }
  {item:
    {b:Hardlinks.} The {C:FileInfo} of Windows has no file identity. The walk
    opens each entry for its attributes, and reads the volume serial number,
    the file index and the link count. These take the place of the device, the
    inode and the link count of Unix. {C:--one-file-system} uses the volume serial
    number.
  }
  {item:
    {b:File identity of a named path.} The {C:FileInfo} of {C:os.Lstat} has no
    identity on Windows, and {C:os.SameFile} reads it from the path later. The
    walk reads it at once, so that the check against a swap after the walk
    works ({C:doc/Security_Audit.eml}, finding 3).
  }
  {item:
    {b:Times of links.} The reader opens the link relative to the handle of its
    directory ({C:NtCreateFile} with {C:FILE_OPEN_REPARSE_POINT}), and sets the
    times on that handle. Thus the times of the target do not change, and the
    operation stays inside the extraction root, as with the {C:*at} calls of Unix.
  }
  {item:
    {b:Modes and owners.} Windows has the read-only attribute in place of
    mode bits, and ACLs in place of owners. {C:os.Chmod} sets the read-only
    attribute from the write bit of the owner. A file reads back as 0666 or

    {list t=1:
      {item:
        Thus compact and create do not keep a mode such as 0640. A new
        directory gets the ACL of its parent, not 0700.
      }
    }
  }
  {item:
    {b:Access to a configuration file and a passphrase file.} In place of the
    owner and the mode, the program reads the owner and the DACL of the file
    ({C:access_windows.go}). It trusts you, SYSTEM and the Administrators group,
    as it trusts you and root on Unix. It refuses a configuration file that
    another account owns, or that the DACL lets another account write. It
    warns about a passphrase file that another account owns, or that the DACL
    lets another account read. A right to change the DACL or the owner counts
    as a right to read and to write. A file with no DACL, or a NULL DACL, gives
    each right to Everyone. This is also what a file on FAT gives, so the
    program refuses a configuration file there. The check reads the ACEs in
    order, as Windows does. A deny to an account, or to Everyone, takes away
    the right from later allows. A deny to a group does not take away the right
    from its members, because the program does not know the members. Thus the
    check can report an account that Windows stops, but it does not miss one
    that Windows lets through. An inherit-only ACE does not apply to the file.
    The program counts an allow with a condition, and does not evaluate it.
  }
  {item:
    {b:Link targets.} Windows gives a target with {C:\\}. The writer stores it
    with {C:/}. The reader changes {C:/} back to {C:\\} when it creates the link on
    Windows. On Unix, the writer and the reader keep a {C:\\} in a target, because
    there {C:\\} is a character of a name.
  }
  {item:
    {b:Replacement of an open file.} Windows does not let a rename replace a
    file that is open, and Go opens each file without {C:FILE_SHARE_DELETE}.
    Create, compact and the change of passphrase rename a new archive over an
    old one that is open and locked. So on Windows, the program opens each
    archive with {C:FILE_SHARE_DELETE}, and renames with POSIX semantics
    ({C:FileRenameInfoEx}). Then the rename removes the old file from the
    directory, as on Unix, and the open handles can still read it. A file
    system without POSIX semantics (FAT) gets {C:MoveFileEx}. There, the rename
    fails while another handle has the archive open.
  }
  {item:
    {b:The lock.} A {C:LockFileEx} lock is mandatory: no other handle can read the
    bytes that it covers. The program locks the byte at offset 2^63 - 1, far
    past the end of any archive. Thus a reader is not stopped, as on Unix
    with {C:flock} ({ref t=#concurrent-writers:§9.6}).
  }
  {item:
    {b:Spill files.} Windows cannot delete an open file. A spill file has
    {C:FILE_FLAG_DELETE_ON_CLOSE} in place of the unlink ({ref t=#concurrency-model:§8}).
  }
  {item:
    {b:Directory sync.} Windows cannot sync a directory. The program does not
    sync it after a rename ({ref t=#compact:§9.3}). NTFS records the rename in its journal.
    Thus a success is durable only when NTFS has written its journal, as the
    model {C:Replace.tla} shows (testing.md §5, step 5).
  }
  {item:
    {b:Configuration file.} The home directory is {C:%USERPROFILE%}, not {C:$HOME}.
  }
  {item:
    {b:Memory size.} {C:GlobalMemoryStatusEx} gives the size of the RAM for the
    budget ({ref t=#memory-budget:§8.1}) and for the check of the key derivation. {C:x/sys/windows}
    does not wrap it, so {C:memory_windows.go} calls it from {C:kernel32.dll}.
  }
  {item:
    {b:Names.} Windows file names are UTF-16. A name that is not valid UTF-8
    cannot be created, and the tests of such names skip. A name cannot hold a
    control character. Windows refuses such a name from an archive made on
    Unix, so that member does not extract there.
  }
}

{section 2 bid=outstanding-items: Outstanding items}

These items are not faults. Each one needs a decision or a measurement
before any work starts.

{list t=*:
  {item:
    {b:The decode of the index.} Each operation decodes the whole index when
    it opens the archive. {C:BenchmarkDecodeIndex} (package {C:format}) decodes
    an index of 20000 members in about 19 ms: 16 ms of CBOR decode, 1.5 ms of
    zstd and 1 ms of {C:Validate}. The CBOR library decodes by reflection, with
    9 allocations for each member, at about 240 MB/s. The check for duplicate
    keys costs nothing that can be measured. A test decoded the members in
    parallel, after a first pass that splits them as raw CBOR items. It saved
    only 6 ms with 16 goroutines, because the first pass costs 7 ms, and it
    added a second path through the parser. It is not adopted. A decoder
    written for the Member map can be much faster. It is a parser of hostile
    input, so it needs a differential fuzz test against the library.
  }
  {item:
    {b:Dictionaries at the best speed.} {C:zstd:train} at level 10 to 22 makes
    create of many small files very slow. Level 12, the default, is in this
    range. 20000 small JSON files take 23.8 s and 265 s of CPU, against 0.7 s
    without a dictionary. At this speed, the library (klauspost/compress
    v1.20.1 to v1.20.5, {C:bestFastEncoder.Reset}) copies the tables that it made from the
    dictionary, about 34 MiB, at the start of each chunk. A small file is one
    chunk, so the copy costs much more than the compression. A large chunk
    hides the copy. The speeds below 10 copy only the parts of their tables
    that the last chunk changed. A small {C:--chunk-size} has the same cost on
    large files: a tree of 125 MiB in chunks of 512 bytes took 124 s at
    level 13, and 0.6 s at level 9 (testing.md §4).

    A lower speed for small chunks is not a good fix. With a 32 KiB
    dictionary on the same files, level 7 for small chunks made the archive
    4.6% larger, and level 3 made it 7.9% larger.

    The fix belongs in the library. On 2026-10-04, a test copy of
    {C:bestFastEncoder} put back only the table entries that each chunk wrote.
    It made create 5 to 14 times faster. Its record does not give all the
    settings of the measurement, so its numbers are not used here.

    The fix is now on the branch {C:zstd-best-dict-reset} of a fork of the
    library (github.com/philmalin/compress), for a pull request to the library.
    The tables are divided into shards of 16 entries. Encode sets a bit in a
    bitmap for each shard that it writes. Reset copies back only these shards,
    or the full tables when most shards have their bit set. Encode sets the
    bits only after a Reset with a dictionary, so an encode without a
    dictionary does the same work as before.

    The table gives the results on 2026-10-21, with {C:zstd:train} at level 12
    and the default {C:-j}, on 32 CPUs. Each value is the median of 3 runs:

    {stable col=4 header sb b==:
    | Tree | v1.20.3 | Fork, {C:bool} for each 128 entries | Fork, bit for each 16 entries |
    | 20000 JSON files | 23.8 s, 265 s of CPU | 4.5 s, 24.7 s of CPU | 3.3 s, 11.5 s of CPU |
    | 3000 JSON files | 8.4 s, 47 s of CPU | 1.9 s, 7.6 s of CPU | 1.5 s, 5.4 s of CPU |
    | 1500 YAML files | 6.0 s, 24 s of CPU | 1.0 s, 4.2 s of CPU | 0.8 s, 3.5 s of CPU |
    }

    The first version of the fork kept one {C:bool} for each shard of 128
    entries. Its Reset read all the flags two times for each chunk, and this
    read cost more than the copy. Smaller shards made it slower. With one bit
    for each shard, the read became small, and smaller shards became faster.
    Shards of 8 and 16 entries were the fastest of the sizes from 4 to 128.

    Without a dictionary, the three builds take the same time: 0.7 s and
    5.5 s of CPU on the 20000 files. The release binary reads the archives of
    the fork without error with {C:--verify} and {C:--diff}. In the benchmarks of
    the library, the best speed without a dictionary did not change (A/B,
    10 rounds on one core, compared with {C:benchstat}).

    eictar does not carry a changed copy of the library: {C:go install} refuses a
    module with a {C:replace} directive. A release uses the library in go.mod.
    {C:build-fork.sh} makes a local build with the fork. The item waits for the
    fix in a release of the library.

    The branch {C:dict-deterministic} of the fork has a second fix, for a
    separate pull request: the dictionary trainer gives the same dictionary
    from the same samples ({ref t=#dictionaries-m10:§4.2}). The branch {C:zstd-best-dict-reset} also has
    this fix, so a build with {C:build-fork.sh} has both.

    {C:BenchmarkDictSmallChunks} in {C:codec} measures the cost on one core. Small
    chunks with a dictionary encode at 150 MB/s at level 9. At level 12, they
    encode at 2.4 MB/s with v1.20.3, and at 87 MB/s with the fork.
  }
  {item:
    {b:Finer zstd levels.} Levels 10 to 22 use one encoder of the library
    ({ref t=#command-line-interface:§10}). Appendix B compares it with the C library. At level 12, the default, the C library
    is at most 5% smaller. At levels 16 to 19, it is 7% to 15% smaller,
    because of optimal parsing. The work belongs in the library. Parameters
    for each level are the small step, and optimal parsing is the large one.
  }
}

{section 1 bid=implementation-milestones: Implementation milestones}

The milestones M1 to M11 and the notes of each release are in {C:history.md}.
Until 2026-10-15 they were this section.

{section 1 nc bid=appendix-a: Appendix A. Why these primitives, compared with AES}

This appendix supports {ref t=#primitives:§6.1}. The question "how does this compare with AES?"
mixes two layers. Argon2id is a key derivation function for passphrases.
XChaCha20-Poly1305 is an AEAD cipher. AES alone is a block cipher, and it
needs a mode.

Thus the real comparisons are XChaCha20-Poly1305 against AES-256-GCM, and
Argon2id against the KDF of the AES-based tool. The KDF is
usually the weaker part.

{section 2 nc bid=1-cipher-xchacha20-poly1305: A.1 Cipher: XChaCha20-Poly1305 and AES-256-GCM}

{b:Security margin.} Both ciphers use 256-bit keys, and there is no practical
attack on either. ChaCha20 has 20 rounds, and the best published attack
reaches approximately 7. That is a large margin. The best attacks on AES-256
are biclique attacks, which remove only a few bits from a brute-force search.
That has no practical effect. Neither cipher is the weak part of a real
system.

{b:Speed.} AES is faster only on some hardware:

{stable col=3 header sb b== i=3% w=94%:
|  | with AES-NI or ARMv8 crypto | without |
| AES-256-GCM | ~1–5 GB/s for each core | ~50–150 MB/s, {b:and table-based} |
| XChaCha20-Poly1305 | ~1–2 GB/s for each core | ~0.5–1 GB/s |
}

The second column is the important one. Go's {C:crypto/aes} uses hardware
instructions on amd64 and arm64. On other targets, it uses lookup tables,
which do not run in constant time and are the usual target of cache-timing
attacks. ChaCha20 runs in constant time on every target, because it uses only
additions, rotations and XOR on 32-bit words. Thus AES is faster where the
hardware helps, and slower and less safe where it does not.

{b:Nonces.} GCM has a 96-bit nonce. Random nonces start to collide after
approximately 2^32 messages with one key. One reuse is a disaster: it exposes
the GHASH key, and then any forgery is possible. XChaCha20 has a 192-bit
nonce, so random nonces are always safe.

In this design, the large nonce is necessary once. The per-member keys and
the chunk counters ({ref t=#crypto-header-cbor-plaintext:§6.2}, {ref t=#aead-layer:§6.3}) never reuse a nonce. The index is different: one
generation can get two different indexes ({ref t=#aead-layer:§6.3}). Thus the index nonce is
random, and only a 192-bit nonce makes a random nonce safe.

{b:A limit that matters for an archiver.} GCM has a hard limit of
approximately 64 GiB for each key and nonce. After that limit, its security
proof fails. A tar-like tool that encrypts a 200 GB file as one stream goes
past the limit. The 4 MiB chunks of {ref t=#member-payloads-chunks-compression:§4} avoid the limit with either cipher.
This is a good example of why sealed chunks are the correct design.

{b:For eictar, cipher speed is not important.} zstd at level 19 runs at
approximately 5–20 MB/s for each core. Even level 3 runs at a few hundred
MB/s. Encryption at more than 1 GB/s is never the slowest step. The cipher
speed shows only with {C:--compress none}, where AES-NI is truly faster. The
crypto header names the AEAD ({C:"aead": "xchacha20poly1305"}). Thus an
{C:aes256gcm} option can come later with no change to the format version.

{section 2 nc bid=2-kdf-argon2id-usual: A.2 KDF: Argon2id and the usual choices}

The KDF is the larger difference. Most "AES encryption" tools are weak here:

{stable col=3 header sb b== i=3% w=94%:
| KDF | Memory for each guess | The attacker's advantage |
| Iterated SHA (GnuPG S2K, old zip) | very small | GPUs make billions of guesses each second |
| PBKDF2-SHA256 ({C:openssl enc}, AES-zip) | very small | GPUs make millions of guesses each second |
| bcrypt | 4 KB | resists GPUs, but ASICs are possible |
| scrypt | adjustable | memory-hard, good |
| Argon2id | adjustable (256 MiB default, {ref t=#crypto-header-cbor-plaintext:§6.2}) | memory-hard and resistant to side channels. Winner of the Password Hashing Competition. |
}

Memory-hardness is the important property. A GPU has thousands of cores but
only tens of GB of memory. At 256 MiB for each guess, a large card can make
only some hundreds of guesses at the same time, not millions. The attacker's
speed decreases by several orders of magnitude. That is a larger practical
difference than any choice of cipher.

The {C:id} variant is a hybrid. Its first pass uses addresses that do not
depend on the data, which resists cache-timing attacks. Its later passes use
addresses that depend on the data, which resists time-memory trade-offs. It
is the recommended variant.

{section 2 nc bid=3-argon2id-default: A.3 The Argon2id default}

The default is {b:256 MiB}, with {C:time=3} and {C:threads=4}. One GiB is
stronger, but every open on every machine pays that cost. A quarter of it
still costs a GPU orders of magnitude, and a small machine can still open the
archive.

The parameters are in the crypto header. Thus an archive made on a large
machine can ask a small machine for more memory than it has. Argon2id
allocates that memory at once, so the result is an out-of-memory kill with no
explanation.

To prevent this, {C:eictar} compares the memory with the machine before it
derives the key. It does this on create and on open. On open, it
does it before the passphrase prompt. If the memory is more than half of the
RAM, the program refuses with a message like this example:

{code:
eictar: deriving the key needs 4096 MiB, more than half this machine's 6000 MiB;
lower --kdf-memory, or run this where the archive was made
}

The format also limits what a header can ask for ({C:MaxKDFMemoryKiB}, 4 GiB,
and {C:MaxKDFTime}, 64 passes), because these numbers come from the file.

{section 2 nc bid=4-summary: A.4 Summary}

Compared with the usual pair of AES-256-GCM and PBKDF2, this design has equal
cipher strength. It is slower on x86 with hardware AES, but compression hides
that difference. It is safer on every platform without AES instructions. It
resists passphrase attacks much better.

{section 1 nc bid=appendix-b: Appendix B. The zstd levels of the library, compared with the zstd command}

This appendix supports {ref t=#command-line-interface:§10} ("The zstd library has four speeds, not 22
levels"). It answers two questions. How much smaller can a zstd archive be
than the best speed of {C:klauspost/compress} gives? And what work in the
library gets that gain?

{section 2 nc bid=b-1-how-two: B.1 How the two encoders choose a level}

The C library (the {C:zstd} command) has 22 levels. Each level is a row of
seven parameters: the window, the size of the hash table, the size of the
chain table, the depth of the search, the shortest match, the target length
of a match, and the strategy. Thus each level is a small step from the one
before it.

The strategy is the method that finds the matches. There are nine:

{list t=*:
  {item:
    {C:fast} and {C:dfast} look up one or two hash tables.
  }
  {item:
    {C:greedy}, {C:lazy} and {C:lazy2} follow a hash chain to a set depth. They also
    try the next one or two positions before they accept a match.
  }
  {item:
    {C:btlazy2} keeps a binary tree of earlier positions, and finds longer
    matches.
  }
  {item:
    {C:btopt}, {C:btultra} and {C:btultra2} do optimal parsing. For each block, they
    find the series of matches and literals with the lowest cost in bits. A
    price model that learns from the data gives the cost. {C:btultra2} does two
    passes. Levels 16 to 22 use these strategies.
  }
}

{C:klauspost/compress} has four encoders: fastest, default, better and best
({ref t=#command-line-interface:§10}). Each one has fixed parameters in the code. The best encoder has a
hash table of 2^22 entries for 8-byte matches, and one of 2^18 entries for
4-byte matches. Each entry keeps one earlier position. The encoder tries a
fixed set of candidates at each position, and gives each one a cost from the
predefined tables of the format. It is near {C:lazy2} of the C library, with a
short chain. It does no optimal parsing.

{section 2 nc bid=b-2-method: B.2 Method}

The measurement was on 2026-10-25, on an AMD Ryzen AI Max+ 395 (16 cores,
32 threads). It compared the {C:zstd} command v1.6.0 with {C:klauspost/compress}
v1.20.3, the version in go.mod on that date.

Each encoder compressed each file of a corpus as one frame, as eictar
compresses each chunk ({ref t=#member-payloads-chunks-compression:§4}). A file larger than 4 MiB, the default chunk
size, was first cut into pieces of 4 MiB. Thus the two encoders got the
same inputs, and the inputs were the chunks that eictar makes. There were
four corpora:

{stable col=4 header sb b==:
| Corpus | Files | Bytes | Content |
| source | 6900 | 104.9 MB | {C:golang.org/x} from the module cache: Go source, and some test data |
| binary | 158 | 107.3 MB | Objects from the Go build cache, and the eictar binary |
| json | 20000 | 39.0 MB | Small generated JSON files, 1.9 KB on average |
| logs | 60 | 60.6 MB | Generated log files, 1 MB each |
}

The C side ran {C:zstd --single-thread -L -c --filelist LIST}, for levels 1
to 19, 20 and 22, with {C:--ultra} above 19. {C:--single-thread} keeps the read,
the compression and the write on one thread. The output passed {C:zstd -t}.
The Go side was a small program that read each file and called {C:EncodeAll}.
It used one encoder state, a window of 4 MiB and the checksum, as eictar does.
It decoded each frame of its first run and compared it with the input.

Each corpus ran on its own physical core ({C:taskset}), and the four corpora
ran at the same time. Each time is the median of three runs, and includes
the read of the files from the page cache. The sizes include the frame
headers and the checksums. The decode speed was not measured.

{section 2 nc bid=b-3-results: B.3 Results}

The first table gives the size at the best speed of the library, and the
change in size at six levels of the C library. A negative number is a
smaller output:

{stable col=8 header sb b==:
| Corpus | Go best | C 3 | C 9 | C 12 | C 15 | C 16 | C 19 |
| source | 21.56 MB | +10.0% | +0.2% | −1.0% | −3.5% | −6.5% | −7.4% |
| binary | 26.15 MB | +8.1% | −0.3% | −1.2% | −3.3% | −7.8% | −14.8% |
| json | 13.59 MB | +3.1% | +0.6% | +0.4% | +0.1% | +0.1% | +0.1% |
| logs | 14.77 MB | +9.3% | −2.3% | −4.8% | −7.1% | −10.7% | −11.9% |
}

The second table gives the speeds of the same encoders on one core, in MB of
input each second:

{stable col=8 header sb b==:
| Corpus | Go best | C 3 | C 9 | C 12 | C 15 | C 16 | C 19 |
| source | 50 | 406 | 101 | 50 | 23 | 12 | 5.5 |
| binary | 34 | 458 | 121 | 60 | 25 | 13 | 5.7 |
| json | 36 | 160 | 76 | 32 | 16 | 11 | 9.2 |
| logs | 44 | 360 | 86 | 54 | 19 | 8.7 | 6.1 |
}

The third table gives, for each encoder of the library, the lowest C level
that is as small or smaller. It gives the speeds of the two in MB/s:

{stable col=5 header sb b==:
| Corpus | fastest | default | better | best |
| source | C 1: 394, 495 | C 1: 310, 495 | C 5: 235, 205 | C 10: 50, 88 |
| binary | C 1: 434, 603 | C 2: 323, 524 | C 5: 244, 233 | C 8: 34, 123 |
| json | C 1: 169, 174 | C 5: 149, 115 | C 6: 99, 104 | none: 36 |
| logs | C 2: 361, 420 | C 3: 264, 360 | C 5: 213, 195 | C 7: 44, 122 |
}

Levels 20 and 22 gave the same size as level 19, to 0.02%. Their larger
windows have no effect, because no input is larger than 4 MiB.

{section 2 nc bid=b-4-what-results: B.4 What the results show}

{list t=1:
  {item:
    {b:The best speed is near C level 8 to 10 in size, but is 1.8 to 3.7 times
    slower than that level.} At the same speed, C level 12 is 1.0% to 4.8%
    smaller, except on the small JSON files.
  }
  {item:
    {b:Most of the gain is in optimal parsing.} From level 15 to level 16,
    the output decreases by 3% to 5% on source, binary and logs. Level 19 is
    7.4% to 14.8% smaller than the best speed. It is 6 to 9 times slower.
  }
  {item:
    {b:Small files get no gain.} On the JSON files, each level from 6 up is
    within 0.6% of the best speed. The best speed is the smallest. A frame of
    2 KB is too short for a better parser to find more matches. A dictionary
    ({C:zstd:train}, {ref t=#dictionaries-m10:§4.2}) is the tool for these files.
  }
  {item:
    {b:The lower speeds are near the C levels.} better is as small as C
    level 5 or 6, at about the same speed. On source, binary and logs,
    fastest and default are 14% to 38% slower than the C level of the same
    size.
  }
}

{section 2 nc bid=b-5-what-work: B.5 What the work in the library involves}

A change to the encoders does not change the format. The output stays
standard zstd frames, so each decoder, and each earlier eictar, reads it.
The {C:level} key of eictar already takes 1 to 22 ({ref t=#command-line-interface:§10}), so the interface does
not change. Only the output of a level changes. There are three steps, in
order of size:

{list t=1:
  {item:
    {b:Parameters for each level, in the encoders that exist.} The best
    encoder can take the depth of its chain, the size of its tables and the
    number of candidates from the level. Then levels 10 to 15 become steps
    between C levels 9 and 15. The results give a gain of 1% to 3.5% on
    source and binary, and up to 7% on logs. The tables are arrays of a
    fixed size now, and the search loop is tuned for them. Tables of a size
    chosen at run time can make the loop slower.
  }
  {item:
    {b:The speed of the best encoder.} The best speed is as small as C level
    8 to 10, but is as slow as C level 12, or slower. Thus a faster search at the
    same size is possible. This is a separate gain from step 1.
  }
  {item:
    {b:Optimal parsing.} A binary-tree match finder and a parser with a
    price model, as {C:btopt} and {C:btultra} of the C library, give the gain of
    levels 16 to 19: 6.5% to 14.8% on these corpora. In the C library, this
    code is several thousand lines. It is the largest step, and the only one
    that gets near C level 19.
  }
}

The speeds in B.3 are for one core. eictar compresses chunks in parallel
({ref t=#concurrency-model:§8}), so a slow level costs less time on a machine with many CPUs. Each of
these steps belongs in the library, as a pull request, for the same reason
as the dictionary fix ({ref t=#outstanding-items:§15.2}).

The other method is to call the C library through cgo. It is not a good fit
for eictar. A release is one static binary for each platform, built with
{C:CGO_ENABLED=0} and cross-compiled for the BSDs and Windows. cgo removes
these properties.
